\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In a 24-Hour Period, a Snapshot of Domestic Violence's Spread\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Cochrane \par \uc2 1,912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some major Supreme Court decisions this term, including a ruling that allowed the government to prohibit people subject to restraining orders from having guns, have underscored the pervasiveness of domestic violence.\par \par \uc2 They call from work, to avoid being overheard, or from home before someone returns. They reach out because they have decided to leave or need to ask a stranger if they should.\par \par \uc2 I'm at my wit's end.\par \par \uc2 He stalks me outside of work.\par \par \uc2 I just wanted to know that I'm not crazy.\par \par \uc2 To listen to the National Domestic Violence Hotline is to witness how a confluence of stressors -- high prices, a lack of affordable housing, easy access to firearms and drugs, the ubiquity of technology -- can leave a person vulnerable to another's cruelty and manipulation.\par \par \uc2 Spikes in calls often align with highly publicized events: natural disasters, recession, quarantine during the height of the coronavirus pandemic, a celebrity's acknowledgment of being a survivor of domestic abuse.\par \par \uc2 But in recent years, staff at the hotline said more of the spikes could be traced in part to crucial court rulings, as people press for answers about the impact of the decisions or how they have factored into the violence they have experienced at home.\par \par \uc2 Already, the number of calls that mention forced unprotected sex or a partner sabotaging birth control -- as by puncturing condoms or hiding pills -- nearly doubled in the first year since the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to an analysis of calls and surveys done by the hotline. And calls mentioning firearms rose 40 percent after an appeals court in New Orleans last February struck down a federal law blocking people subject to a domestic violence protection order from owning a gun.\par \par \uc2 Staff members had been focused on the outcomes of two cases resting with the nation's highest court, involving gun access and the availability of a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. On Friday, the Supreme Court reversed the appeals court ruling, saying that the government may prohibit people subject to restraining orders from having guns.\par \par \uc2 But even before the courts took up the gun case, the hotline, understaffed and underfunded, struggled to keep pace with an escalating number of calls over the years. The legal battles have underscored the pervasiveness of domestic violence and the strains on existing support for survivors.\par \par \uc2 ''That makes me sad that we need lives to be in jeopardy for this to become a national conversation around domestic violence, because it shouldn't take a Supreme Court case,'' said Katie Ray-Jones, the chief executive of the hotline.\par \par \uc2 To capture a snapshot of the experiences of domestic violence survivors, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  observed some of the calls and messages the hotline received in one day. The Times agreed to only disclose certain nonidentifying details and limited excerpts from the conversations to protect the safety of those who consented to speaking with a reporter present.\par \par \uc2 Over 24 hours, the hotline received 2,002 incoming calls and messages and answered 1,348. Transcripts of calls to 1-800-799-7233 and website chats , observed with the consent of the caller, have been condensed to ensure anonymity.\par \par \uc2 Congress approved creation of a national hotline dedicated to domestic violence in 1994, including it in the landmark Violence Against Women Act. Founded two years later in Texas, the hotline now receives as many as 3,000 calls and messages a day. Everyone is kept anonymous, with the only formal record describing basic demographic and circumstantial categories, often leaving other details unclear.\par \par \uc2 They are typically women, but their ages, ethnicities and locations vary, as do the circumstances of their relationships. In a single day, those contacting the hotline included a 51-year-old Latina in California; an Asian mother, 38; and a white woman asking how to quietly document her partner's physical abuse.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes it is a question of finding housing during a nationwide shortage or seeking protection after leaving. Other times, it is about wrestling with the emotional contradictions of still having love for someone who makes you feel alone.\par \par \uc2 Working under pseudonyms remotely or at its headquarters in Austin, Texas, staff respondents spend as much as an hour at a time on calls and messages sent through a digital chat that arrive from across the country, at any time of day.\par \par \uc2 They pose sensitive but probing questions to uncover how a relationship has spiraled into deceit or danger, before connecting the survivor to support nearby. Many of the 158 staff respondents are women and survivors themselves.\par \par \uc2 They cannot directly offer legal or medical advice, but give encouragement or recommendations.\par \par \uc2 On a recent morning, a young Black woman from the South called in, describing the relationship she had recently escaped: ''I still sometimes have dreams of that night -- hearing the glass shatter and seeing stars.''\par \par \uc2 Around the same time, a white woman wrote from New England, expressing uncertainty. ''Is this gaslighting? Is this why I feel like I have no idea what is real and what isn't?''\par \par \uc2 The rise in calls reflects an increased willingness to confront domestic violence, as survivors have publicly shared their experiences and lawmakers have moved to improve support.\par \par \uc2 It also reflects a deeper understanding of what abuse can be: monitoring someone through their devices, keeping a person financially dependent, twisting emotions to isolate someone from their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 ''Domestic violence is very complex, and I felt like at different stages in my life, the people around me kept trying to simplify it,'' said Jose Tobias, 29, who has worked at the hotline for nearly two years. A soft-spoken Mexican immigrant who once enrolled in Catholic seminary, he turned to the hotline in part to more directly counter what he sees as the weaponization of faith and other, more nebulous, forms of abuse.\par \par \uc2 ''It's never the same, so the solution is never the same,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 Conversations at the hotline take on a new urgency once a caller confirms an instance of strangulation or the presence of a weapon. Research shows that millions of women have been threatened with a gun by an intimate partner.\par \par \uc2 ''If he does have access to the weapon, that can increase the lethality a lot,'' Mr. Tobias told one woman, who was unsure whether her former partner had access to a gun.\par \par \uc2 To a tearful father, who recounted how his daughter's abuser had access to a gun and already had physically assaulted her, he outlined the heightened risk: ''We've seen this person weaponize his body. We could see him take it'' further.\par \par \uc2 And while gun rights groups highlight the experiences of survivors who arm themselves as a means of self-defense and their constitutional right to own a firearm, many domestic violence groups say the presence of a gun exacerbates the psychological trauma of being threatened with one.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court had upheld the ruling by the appeals court, it would have rolled back a federal law that makes it a felony to possess a gun while under a domestic violence order.\par \par \uc2 In the three states covered by the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals, the increase in calls mentioning a firearm was even more significant after the lower court ruling, hotline employees said: 47 percent in Texas, 59 percent in Mississippi and 63 percent in Louisiana. And given how difficult it can be to ensure that any person subject to an order no longer has access to a gun, many domestic violence groups had feared losing a crucial means of ensuring the safety of survivors.\par \par \uc2 ''One way or another, the survivors are going to be affected -- it's just about to what extent,'' Mr. Tobias said.\par \par \uc2 Staff members have also struggled with a swiftly moving landscape on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, as reports of forced acts like rape and unprotected sex have flooded their lines and as the prospect of further limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and reproductive care looms.\par \par \uc2 In an analysis of nearly 3,500 responses to a survey conducted last fall, the hotline found that nearly a quarter of respondents were pressured into becoming pregnant, 20 percent were forcibly prevented from using birth control and nearly 10 percent faced threats of violence over seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And even as the Supreme Court last week maintained access to a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, it did not weigh in on the merits of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or rule out the possibility of other challenges. On the day of the ruling, hotline employees said calls mentioning interference in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased about 75 percent from what had been a daily average of about seven calls this month.\par \par \uc2 ''Thank goodness that I was able to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because my life -- I would not be sitting here,'' said Hannah Tucker, 33, an employee with a shock of green hair and an array of colorful tattoos, adding: ''I was going to be guilted into staying with somebody I knew was going to be really dangerous for me.''\par \par \uc2 The outcome of the two cases does not eliminate the financial and physical barriers that ensure a person stays in an abusive relationship. It will not change ingrained cultural and societal stigma about leaving, or the fact that certain communities are already more at risk.\par \par \uc2 And the hotline has only the resources to answer about 53 percent of its calls and messages. Ms. Jones and other staff estimated that it would take at least an additional $20 million a year to fully staff the hotline.\par \par \uc2 ''Domestic violence does not discriminate,'' said Josie Slawik, 74. ''Domestic violence affects everybody -- no matter what race you are, how much money you have, you don't have, it happens.''\par \par \uc2 She still remembers what it felt like in 1978, as she fled, her rib broken and two young daughters in tow, to a shelter in El Paso. With a warm personality, bright red curls and gold eye shadow shimmering around her eyes, she was there when the hotline took its first call in 1996.\par \par \uc2 Like others, she said she still had the capacity to be moved by hope and by horror.\par \par \uc2 ''He was punching me in the stomach when I was pregnant,'' a woman told her by phone. She soon hung up.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Slawik paused, removing her headset.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm going to take a reset -- that was hard for me,'' Ms. Slawik said, her voice soft. ''This movement has come a long way, but we have a ways to go.''\par \par \uc2 A services manager at the National Domestic Violence Hotline, which is struggling to keep pace with the volume of callers. (A1); Josie Slawik, top, a bilingual phone advocate at the National Domestic Violence Hotline in Austin, Texas. Hannah Tucker, who answers digital messages at the hotline, had an \uc2  after an abusive relationship. ''I was going to be guilted into staying with somebody I knew was going to be really dangerous for me,'' she said. (A14-A15); Callers to the hotline remain anonymous, with the only formal record describing basic demographic and circumstantial categories. Jose Tobias, who was once enrolled in Catholic seminary, said he became a hotline employee in part to counter what he sees as the weaponization of faith as a form of abuse. The hotline's offices. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ISADORA KOSOFSKY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240622ek6m0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
