\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 171,000 Traveled for Abortions Last Year. See Where the Patients Went.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Molly Cook Escobar, Amy Schoenfeld Walker, Allison McCann and Scott Reinhard \par \uc2 1,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 14,000 Texas patients crossed the border into New Mexico for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year. An additional 16,000 left Southern states bound for Illinois. And nearly 12,000 more traveled north from South Carolina and Georgia to North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 These were among the more than 171,000 patients who traveled for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023, new estimates show, demonstrating both the upheaval in access since the overturn of Roe v. Wade and the limits of state bans to stop the procedure. The data also highlights the unsettled nature of an issue that will test politicians up and down the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 Out-of-state travel for abortions -- either to have a procedure or obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- more than doubled in 2023 compared with 2019, and made up nearly a fifth of recorded abortions. On Thursday, the Supreme Court rejected a case that would have sharply curtailed access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing the pills to remain available to patients traveling from states with bans.\par \par \uc2 Most traveling patients went to the next closest state that allowed abortions. But those in the South, where 13 states banned or restricted the procedure, had to go farther.\par \par \uc2 One traveler was a 24-year-old woman from Columbus, Ga., who asked to be identified by only her first initial, A. She flew to New York City last summer after discovering she was past six weeks of pregnancy, when Georgia no longer allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She decided to travel over a weekend instead of self-managing with pills at home. ''I had to go back to work on Monday,'' she said. ''I just didn't have that kind of time.''\par \par \uc2 Texas, the largest state to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had the most residents travel across state lines for the procedure, the data shows.\par \par \uc2 On the receiving end, nowhere saw more out-of-state patients -- and from more states -- than Illinois.\par \par \uc2 People in states where the procedure remained legal also traveled for abortions, sometimes because the closest clinic was across state lines or the influx of out-of-state patients made appointments scarce. The data shows that abortions rose in nearly every state where they remained legal.\par \par \uc2 Many traveling patients faced multiday trips, lost income and child care costs. Some patients were unable to travel. Earlier research found that in the first half of 2023, almost a quarter of women living in states with near-total bans -- who may have otherwise sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- did not get one.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the most common procedures in medicine,'' said \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , the founder of Whole Woman's Health, which runs clinics in Maryland, Minnesota, New Mexico and Virginia.\par \par \uc2 ''We're having people travel hundreds or thousands of miles for a procedure that typically takes less than 10 minutes and can be done in a doctor's office setting,'' she said. ''Nobody does that for any other medical procedure.''\par \par \uc2 The new estimates of resident and out-of-state abortions come from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and they offer the first detailed picture of the interstate travelers who helped push the number of abortions nationwide to a high in 2023. The researchers surveyed a sample of clinics in each state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal to estimate the number of abortions.\par \par \uc2 For some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, the feeling of victory after the overturn of Roe has been dimmed by the number of people circumventing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans -- and the lack of political will to address the issue in an election year.\par \par \uc2 ''We're agitating some of the Republicans who would be very comfortable spiking the football, patting themselves on the back, running for re-election, and then focusing on other issues that they're more interested in,'' said John Seago, the president of Texas Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 ''We've never had a sense of finality. We've only seen the other side escalate their efforts to promote \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 The availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills has significantly blunted the impact of many state bans. But some patients still must travel to see a provider because of a medical condition or how far along they are in pregnancy. Others simply prefer it.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't want the pills to get delivered to my school,'' said Mia, 20, a college student in Houston who asked to be identified by only her first name. Instead, last August, she drove 12 hours to an Albuquerque clinic. ''In case anything went wrong, I didn't know if I could go to a hospital,'' she said. ''I figured it would be best to go in person and that way I'd know that it was taken care of.''\par \par \uc2 The clinic covered the procedure's cost, but Mia paid around $500 for gas, two nights at an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  and Uber rides to get to and from her appointment.\par \par \uc2 The explosion of out-of-state travel has been met with support from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, which expanded access to services and financial support for patients.\par \par \uc2 ''Now we have places where people who've been driving all night can nap in our clinics,'' said Ms. Hagstrom Miller. ''We have couches. We have waiting rooms specifically for children, with toys. We bring in sandwiches and food.''\par \par \uc2 States with liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws have also played a significant role.\par \par \uc2 ''It looks like the protective policies that the states are enacting do matter,'' said Kelly Baden, the vice president for public policy at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . ''But we should not be normalizing the reliance on networks of volunteers and donations.''\par \par \uc2 Illinois has invested upwards of $23 million into expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care since 2022. Providers in the state have extended clinic hours and increased staffing and the availability of hospital-based \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 ''Things are running along very smoothly,'' said Dr. Allison Cowett, the medical director at Family Planning Associates, a Chicago clinic whose patient volume has doubled since 2018. ''We've caught up to the speed of things. This is our new normal.''\par \par \uc2 The Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund provides, on average, about $880 to each patient seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Illinois, up from around $545 in 2022, thanks to donations and city and state grants.\par \par \uc2 ''It still feels precarious -- you don't know when the priority of a single institution or a single foundation will change,'' said Megan Jeyifo, the fund's executive director.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions is far from over, with consequences for women across the South. The state had an 18 percent rise in abortions last year, including nearly 10,000 out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 A six-week ban that took effect in May has already upended those patterns, and advocates are asking voters to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's Constitution in November.\par \par \uc2 For now, the closest state offering abortions later than six weeks in pregnancy is North Carolina, which requires counseling and a 72-hour waiting period.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a logistical nightmare,'' said Kelly Flynn, the chief executive of A Woman's Choice, which has clinics in Florida, North Carolina and Virginia. To save patients two trips out of Florida, physicians at her Florida clinic are licensed in North Carolina so that they can perform the mandatory counseling before the patient travels north.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A12, A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240619ek6j00055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
