\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Tough Week for the Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Movement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,121 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The movement looks for a path forward: ''Is the goal the absolute abolition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in our nation?''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  voted to condemn in vitro fertilization at its annual meeting in Indianapolis this week, over the objections of some members.\par \par \uc2 Conservative lawyers pushing to sharply restrict medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost a major case at the Supreme Court, after pursuing a strategy that many of their allies thought was an overreach.\par \par \uc2 Former president Donald J. Trump told Republicans in a closed-door meeting to stop talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans limiting the procedure at certain numbers of weeks.\par \par \uc2 In one chaotic week, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement showed how major players are pulling in various directions and struggling to find a clear path forward two years after their victory of overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The divisions start at the most fundamental level of whether to even keep pushing to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or to move on to other areas of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like fertility treatments. A movement that once marched nearly in lock step finds itself mired in infighting and unable to settle on a basic agenda.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, hard-liners are seizing the reins, rejecting the incremental strategy that made their movement successful in overturning Roe. Other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are backing away, sensing the political volatility of the moment.\par \par \uc2 At a lunch at the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  this week, Tom Ascol, a prominent conservative pastor from Florida, bemoaned what the fragmentation meant for their cause.\par \par \uc2 ''The most important thing is, can we agree on the goal?'' he said in a panel discussion. ''Is the goal the absolute abolition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in our nation?''\par \par \uc2 Some applauded loudly. Others stayed quiet.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the movement had honed a strategy to achieve a singular goal: ending a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But after that win, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has suffered a series of political losses. Democrats have won ballot initiatives in more than half a dozen states to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state law.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in half a century, Republicans are trying to win a presidential election without the rallying cry of ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a mobilizing tactic for their base. The stakes are higher because it is unclear whether Mr. Trump will simply defer to their wishes in a second administration as he did in his first.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents dominate many conservative statehouses and courts, their efforts to go further -- to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and transform \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- have occasionally backfired. Republicans in swing states, prominent Senate candidates and even their champion Mr. Trump have all backed away from the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda, for fear of more political fallout.\par \par \uc2 Even in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongholds, control is slipping. In Arizona, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  adherents make up the bulk of the Republican elected officials in the state Legislature, but this spring they narrowly lost their heated fight to reinstate a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from 1864.\par \par \uc2 ''We are at a reckoning point especially on the political side, where candidates, elected officeholders, have to make a choice on how much they want to protect life,'' said Greg Scott, vice president of policy at the Center for Arizona Policy, which supported the 1864 ban.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America who worked for years on an incremental strategy, has pushed for Republicans to embrace a 15-week national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When Roe fell, her organization was primarily worried about Democrats' use of referendums to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitutions. But now she sees a much bigger threat: Democrats winning office at the national level, and eventually codifying Roe with something like the Women's Health Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 You have another election like the 2022 midterms, she said, ''and all the ballot initiatives are a moot point.''\par \par \uc2 While disappointed in those results, she finds lessons in the long-term vision of her movement, which historically refuses to give up even amid major setbacks.\par \par \uc2 ''The hidden reality of the pro-life movement is the determination,'' she said. ''It would be unrealistic to think it would be a straight line to success.''\par \par \uc2 In interviews, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists sometimes sounded frustrated. Some blamed the media, while others acknowledged their positions were minority ones. A third of Americans agree with the statement that ''human life begins at conception, so an embryo is a person with rights,'' according to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Christina Francis, who leads the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists, lost at the Supreme Court this week in the case that could have restricted access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We have a lot of work to do in talking to people about this issue,'' she said in an interview last month. ''Public opinion isn't just going to change on a dime.''\par \par \uc2 A major challenge is getting lawmakers and politicians to push forward at all now that Roe is gone, said \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , president of Students for Life of America. ''They haven't really woken up to all that can and must be done,'' she said. ''For us, it is a lot of education.''\par \par \uc2 She sees a broader cultural challenge in the rising generation whose views about being human were formed by secularism, not by parents who took them to church, she said. Many do not want to have children at all, she noted.\par \par \uc2 An uncompromising segment of the movement is pushing ahead for ''fetal personhood,'' or giving embryos constitutional rights, a mission based in its Christian values. Jason Rapert, who founded the National Association of Christian Lawmakers, is organizing allied state lawmakers to push for such legislation and sees irreconcilable differences for America's future.\par \par \uc2 ''The bottom line for America, we are living in basically the same environment that the nation was in the 1850s, where the argument was that you could have slave states and free states,'' he said. ''The nation cannot go forward with kill states and life states.''\par \par \uc2 But other local actors in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement are urging a longer term strategy.\par \par \uc2 ''Gone are the days, in Ohio that is, of just introducing legislation to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Mike Gonidakis, president of Ohio Right to Life, citing his state's new constitutional amendment protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, which voters approved in November.\par \par \uc2 ''We might have to accept less than a whole loaf,'' he said. ''Our movement, our elected officials, need to appreciate sometimes you have to take what you can get now, and live to fight another day.''\par \par \uc2 The Southern Baptist Convention voted to condemn in vitro fertilization in Indianapolis last week. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240617ek6h00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
