\par\par \uc2 JESSE WEGMAN\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Stays Legal. But for How Long?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jesse Wegman \par \uc2 1,269 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rarely has a straightforward 9-to-0 decision at the Supreme Court felt as unsettling as it did on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The justices' unanimous ruling, in F.D.A. v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, rejected a challenge to the most commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, but it did so only on procedural grounds, without considering the merits of the lawsuit. That means another challenge to the drug, mifepristone, will probably reach the court before long. Based on the right-wing supermajority's open hostility to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there's good reason to worry.\par \par \uc2 For now, at least, and purely as a matter of law, the justices got it right, which these days is saying something.\par \par \uc2 The case decided on Thursday was brought by a group of associations and doctors who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and argued that mifepristone is unsafe for the women who use it. They wanted the courts to overturn recently instituted \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  rules that made mifepristone easier to get and use, so they maneuvered their case in front of a sympathetic federal judge in Texas, Matthew Kacsmaryk, who has publicly opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ruled in the doctors' favor. The Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, which has somehow figured out how to out-radical this Supreme Court, upheld much of Judge Kacsmaryk's decision.\par \par \uc2 The justices tossed the case for a simple reason: The plaintiffs could not show that they had personally suffered harm from the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone. This showing is a fundamental requirement of standing, the judicial doctrine that outlines who is qualified to bring a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The court quoted former Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 's description of this requirement as ''What's it to you?'' In this case, it's nothing, as Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  pointed out in the court's opinion. Because they oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the doctors neither use nor prescribe mifepristone, and the F.D.A. hasn't required them to do or not do anything. The doctors could not even show a single instance in which they were forced to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or even provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related services against their will.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs' moral opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be genuine, the court conceded, but they are already protected by federal and state laws that allow them to refuse to participate in any procedure that goes against their conscience. In that light, a ''desire to make a drug less available for others does not establish standing to sue,'' Justice Kavanaugh wrote.\par \par \uc2 This is obviously correct. And yet even a plaintiff who could meet the standing requirement to challenge mifepristone on the grounds of safety should lose, because the science is not in dispute.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone, in 2000, was based on reams of research that found it to be effective and safe. It has since become the go-to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, used for a majority of abortions in America and boasting a complication rate lower than that of other widely prescribed drugs.\par \par \uc2 The challengers sought to offset this mountain of evidence by presenting a few recent studies purporting to show that the use of mifepristone has led to more emergency-room visits and complications. In the trial court, Judge Kacsmaryk based his decision in part on these studies. But the journal that published those studies retracted them this year after discovering that their authors had hidden their affiliations with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups. The research itself was also poorly designed, the journal said, and the conclusions were based on ''unjustified or incorrect factual assumptions, material errors in the authors' analysis of the data, and misleading presentations of the data.''\par \par \uc2 Of course, this dispute isn't about science or law; it's about religion and politics. That's why the court's right-wing supermajority, which has proved to be as solicitous of religious claims as it is hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and executive agencies, was willing to hear it.\par \par \uc2 It's also why Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  felt free to inject his personal politics into an otherwise mundane concurrence to Thursday's ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''Just as abortionists lack standing to assert the rights of their clients, doctors who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot vicariously assert the rights of their patients,'' Justice Thomas wrote. Note the discrepancy between ''doctors who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' on the one hand and ''abortionists'' on the other -- perhaps the first time that epithet, so laced with sneering innuendo, has appeared in a Supreme Court opinion in a justice's own voice. Doctors can do many things; abortionists can do only one. Also note the differing use of the terms ''patients'' and ''clients'' -- Justice Thomas is saying, in essence, that the provision of basic health-care services that he objects to is not medicine but business.\par \par \uc2 These are the sorts of smoke signals that energize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, who were emboldened by the court's reversal of Roe v. Wade two years ago, and who are on the warpath to end all legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America. From the Comstock Act, a 19th-century anti-vice law that activists are trying to resurrect; to ''heartbeat bills'' that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks; to fetal personhood laws and efforts to restrict access to in vitro fertilization; to attacks on birth-control pills, Republican lawmakers in Congress and the states are working to impose certain people's religious values over most people's fundamental rights.\par \par \uc2 And that's why, if you care about a woman's right to control what happens in her own body, you should be very concerned about the next mifepristone case that reaches the Supreme Court. It may well come from any of three states, Idaho, Kansas and Missouri, that attempted but failed to join the current lawsuit. Immediately after Thursday's ruling was announced, the attorneys general in Kansas and Missouri promised to continue the fight.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans has for decades supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, sometimes by overwhelming margins. It's no surprise that one result of overturning Roe v. Wade has been a remarkable string of electoral victories for candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and for related ballot measures, in red and blue states alike. The people have been speaking clearly for two years, and yet anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers, who are increasingly also anti-democratic lawmakers, have been finding sneaky ways to subvert that voice.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, for example, legislators have sought to make it harder for voters to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights measures on the ballot. Other states have tried to prosecute women who travel to other states to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the time being, access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill is preserved. But make no mistake: This was a narrow, procedural win for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the bigger picture, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces feel the wind is at their backs. It is up to the majority of Americans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to turn out at the polls this fall, and every year, to demonstrate what protecting equality and bodily autonomy really means.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 A member of the editorial board, where he has written about the Supreme Court and national legal affairs since 2013.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY ASPEN MAYS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240614ek6e0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
