\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Senate G.O.P. Blocks I.V.F. Access Bill as Democrats Seek Political Edge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maya Miller \par \uc2 1,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The failed test vote was the latest move by Democrats to highlight reproductive issues in the run-up to the elections in November.\par \par \uc2 Senate Republicans on Thursday blocked legislation that would codify the right to access fertility treatments such as in vitro fertilization, in the latest election-year bid by Democrats to spotlight G.O.P. opposition to protecting reproductive freedoms.\par \par \uc2 On a vote of 48 to 47, all but two Republicans opposed advancing the bill, which would give Americans the statutory right to receive fertility treatments and decide how their reproductive material is used, stored and disposed of. That left the measure well short of the 60 votes it needed to move forward, an outcome Democrats anticipated and even welcomed as part of their strategy to remind voters where Republicans stand on issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Protecting I.V.F. should be the easiest 'yes' vote senators have taken all year,'' said Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Democrat of New York and the majority leader. ''It is a contradiction to claim that you are pro-family but then turn around and block protections for I.V.F.''\par \par \uc2 The action came a week after a test vote on a bill that would enshrine nationwide access to contraception in federal law, which Republicans also blocked.\par \par \uc2 It also came the day after Southern Baptists, the country's largest Protestant denomination and a bellwether for the larger American evangelical movement, voted to oppose the use of I.V.F. That decision could put many conservative lawmakers in an even tougher political position on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have struggled to find a winning message on I.V.F. that appeases their far-right evangelical base without alienating more mainstream conservatives. Many of them support legislation that declares that life begins at conception, which could severely restrict aspects of I.V.F. The treatment typically involves creating several embryos, freezing them and implanting only one or two. At the same time, many conservative lawmakers quickly voiced their support for fertility treatments after a decision by Alabama's Supreme Court in February that frozen embryos should be considered children.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether he agreed with the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 's stance that I.V.F. is unethical because a frozen embryo is a person, Senator Tommy Tuberville, Republican of Alabama, said he ''wouldn't go so far.'' But he then suggested he was ambivalent.\par \par \uc2 ''I'd have to look at the science on that,'' Mr. Tuberville, who cosponsored a fetal personhood bill during the last Congress, said on Thursday. He said he supported I.V.F. treatment but ''would have to think about'' whether frozen embryos should be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Since the Alabama decision, Democrats have stressed the importance of protecting access to fertility treatments and I.V.F.\par \par \uc2 The bill that Democrats sought to push forward this week, the Right to I.V.F. Act, was sponsored by Senator Tammy Duckworth of Illinois. It would codify a right to fertility treatments and require that government insurance providers that serve federal employees, military personnel and veterans provide coverage for it.\par \par \uc2 Americans overwhelmingly support access to I.V.F. treatment. A survey in April from the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found that seven in 10 adults said access to I.V.F. was a good thing, and only 8 percent said the opposite. Twenty-two percent said they were unsure.\par \par \uc2 As Senate Democrats sought to put Republicans on the record with a position that is at odds with a vast majority of voters, G.O.P. lawmakers in the House were seeking to attach anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendments to the annual defense policy bill. The House on Thursday was set to vote on a proposal from Representative Beth Van Duyne, Republican of Texas, that would prohibit payments and reimbursements from the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  for expenses for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, including travel expenses.\par \par \uc2 Across the Capitol, just two Republicans, Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  of Alaska, crossed party lines to support moving forward on Ms. Duckworth's bill. Other G.O.P. senators condemned the action as a ''show vote'' and listed several reasons they were opposed, while at the same time still claiming they unequivocally supported I.V.F.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , Republican of Texas, accused Democrats of playing political games with I.V.F. given that the treatment is legal in all 50 states and ''not in any jeopardy'' of being outlawed.\par \par \uc2 ''The only reason why they're doing this is to try to scare people,'' Mr. Cornyn said.\par \par \uc2 Several Republicans, including Senators \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida, Ted Cruz of Texas and \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  of Missouri, said Democrats would never give them a chance to put forward their own proposals to protect I.V.F. But the measures Republicans have offered have drawn little support in their own ranks and contain no affirmative right to fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, Republicans have sought to blunt their political liability on the I.V.F. issue with legislation of their own. Mr. Cruz and Senator Katie Britt, Republican of Alabama, have introduced a bill that would block Medicaid funding to any state that bans I.V.F. treatment. But that measure has only three Republican cosponsors and has drawn the ire of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters also condemn it as meaningless because it would do nothing to protect access to fertility treatments if states severely restricted them but did not impose an outright ban.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Duckworth called the Republican bill a ''sham'' because it would not explicitly protect I.V.F. providers from prosecution or civil liability under laws that hold that a fetus is a person.\par \par \uc2 ''If fetal personhood exists, and they don't address that issue, then you're going to see what happened in Alabama: All the I.V.F. clinics are going to shut down,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Britt said Ms. Duckworth's bill ''treads on religious freedoms,'' implying that it could force people who do not believe in I.V.F. treatments to provide them. But Democrats note that the legislation does not force anyone to furnish such treatments.\par \par \uc2 Annie Karni contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Annie Karni contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tammy Duckworth, who sponsored the bill, with Senator Chuck Schumer, left, and Representative Hakeem Jeffries. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIC LEE/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240614ek6e0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
