\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Groups Say Tech Giants Are Suppressing Posts and Accounts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Schmall and Sapna Maheshwari \par \uc2 1,507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TikTok has briefly suspended the account of Hey Jane, a prominent telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  service, four times without explanation. \uc2 Instagram\uc2  has suspended Mayday Health, a nonprofit that provides information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill access, without explanation as well. And the search engine Bing has erroneously flagged the website for Aid Access, a major seller of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online, as unsafe.\par \par \uc2 The groups and women's health advocates say these examples, all from recent months, show why they are increasingly confused and frustrated by how major technology platforms moderate posts about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 They say the companies' policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related content, including advertisements, have long been opaque. But they say the platforms seem to have been more aggressive about removing or suppressing posts that share information about how to obtain safe and legal procedures since the Supreme Court ended the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. And when the platforms do restrict the accounts, the companies can be difficult to contact to learn why.\par \par \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, an organization dedicated to abolishing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said big technology companies had routinely limited its and other groups' pro-life speech, suspending accounts and blocking ads with little explanation.\par \par \uc2 ''Transparency is the main point,'' said Jane Eklund, a fellow at the human rights group Amnesty International USA, which released a report on Tuesday calling on tech giants to clearly outline and explain their rules around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related content. ''Without clear guidelines, it's difficult to hold them accountable for their actions that could be impacting users or to identify and address any content moderation that affects what people can find online.''\par \par \uc2 Concerns that some of the tech platforms are suppressing posts about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have led to changes in how women and organizations talk about it online. They intentionally misspell the term as ''aborshun'' or ''ab0rti0n,'' or replace the ''bor'' with a boar emoji in hopes of reaching more people.\par \par \uc2 But that can also make it harder for people to find information, and coded language risks adding stigma to the procedure, experts and content creators say.\par \par \uc2 ''We shouldn't have to substitute words -- we shouldn't have to censor ourselves,'' said Ashley Garcia, a 24-year-old part-time creator, who made two videos promoting Hey Jane last year.\par \par \uc2 The tech companies did not detail how their moderation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related content may have changed since 2022, though TikTok said it had not made significant shifts. The companies said the issues with suspensions and flags of Hey Jane, Mayday Health and Aid Access were mistakes that they rectified.\par \par \uc2 TikTok said accounts can post about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it has a longstanding policy against advertising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, which it counts as ''unsuitable businesses, products or services,'' along with plastic surgery and organ transplants. \uc2 Instagram\uc2  allows ads for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The report released Tuesday from Amnesty International USA included details on how at least six organizations that promote or provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services have had their accounts and posts moderated by \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and TikTok in the past two years.\par \par \uc2 For example, TikTok removed videos from the account for Hey Jane, which has 105,000 followers, for promoting ''illegal activities and regulated goods'' -- including one that detailed the states where it operated and how it hoped to expand to other states. That video wasn't restored.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Hey Jane struggled for days to determine why TikTok had abruptly banned its account. The tech company eventually reinstated the account; Rebecca Davis, Hey Jane's head of brand marketing, said TikTok had told her that ''the suspension was due to 'over-moderation' of their policy surrounding prescription drugs and it should not have been removed.''\par \par \uc2 ''That's pretty much all they can say -- just that it was a mistake and they will try their best to not have it happen again,'' Ms. Davis said.\par \par \uc2 TikTok declined to comment on details about Hey Jane's experience.\par \par \uc2 Groups have complained about similar issues on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Last year, the social network removed a post from Ipas, a nonprofit that promotes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, that had shared the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 's recommended protocol for having a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Instagram\uc2  said at the time that the post had violated \uc2 Meta\uc2 's policy on the ''sale of regulated goods or services.''\par \par \uc2 Instagram\uc2  suspended Mayday Health's account in March for a second time since 2022 ''without any clear explanation or justification,'' said Olivia Raisner, the group's executive director. Mayday Health was told that it had violated \uc2 Instagram\uc2 's guidelines for posting about ''guns, drugs and other restricted goods.'' The group appealed and regained its account, with more than 20,000 followers, after five days. \uc2 Meta\uc2  said last week that the Mayday and Ipas issues were errors. (\uc2 Meta\uc2  said it had restored Ipas's post within days.)\par \par \uc2 ''Our fear would be that for every day our accounts are down, there are fewer people in states with bans who don't get information about how to get pills,'' Ms. Raisner said.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Daniels, a spokesman for \uc2 Meta\uc2 , said \uc2 Instagram\uc2  allowed ads and posts of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, as well as content by groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''We want our platforms to be a place where people can access reliable information about health services, advertisers can promote health services and everyone can discuss and debate public policies in this space,'' he said. ''That's why we allow posts and ads about, discussing and debating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Some women's health groups, as well as some doctors and creators, say they fear the platforms are also suppressing the distribution of posts about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 Mayday Health said the number of people who saw its \uc2 Instagram\uc2  posts had plummeted this year. An infographic it posted about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills reached 15,730 accounts in April 2023; a similar post from this March reached just 1,207 accounts, even though the account has more followers now.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davis said TikTok representatives had explicitly told her that if videos or captions used the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' content would be flagged and might not appear on users' main feeds.\par \par \uc2 TikTok said it did not prohibit posts about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appearing in personalized feeds, but did not address whether it limited such content. \uc2 Instagram\uc2  said this year that it would not recommend ''political content'' unless users opted into seeing it. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy groups haven't received clarity on whether the topic is deemed political, and \uc2 Meta\uc2  declined to specify.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups say the issues have also extended to search engines like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Bing.\par \par \uc2 Aid Access, based in Europe, is among the most prominent online suppliers of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the United States, where medication abortions have been rising sharply. In a search query for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills on Thursday, the Aid Access website was on the first page of \uc2 Google\uc2  results but not found within the first 10 pages of results on Bing.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  representative said sources that were similar in relevance and quality were showing up instead.\par \par \uc2 For months, Bing erroneously tagged Aid Access with a red warning pop-up that said the organization was on the \uc2 National Association of Boards of Pharmacy\uc2 's ''not recommended'' list. The pharmacy association removed Aid Access from the list in September after the organization switched the source of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from a pharmacy in India to providers in the United States approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bing kept posting the label even after Aid Access informed it about the change. The label was removed after an inquiry from a reporter at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  in May.\par \par \uc2 In several Republican-led states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been sharply restricted since the Supreme Court's 2022 decision, state officials have introduced measures to punish organizations that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or information on how to obtain abortions online.\par \par \uc2 Tim Griffin, the Republican attorney general of Arkansas, sent Aid Access a ''cease and desist'' letter in May, saying the organization was violating the state's law on deceptive trade practices because its ads could be seen by women in Arkansas, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prohibited unless necessary to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rebecca Gomperts, the founder and executive director of Aid Access, said the threat would not change the organization's approach. The organization does minimal online marketing because of the challenges posed by big tech companies, she said, depending instead on word-of-mouth referrals from patients and physicians.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been a game, up and down, with all the social media and search companies,'' Dr. Gomperts said.\par \par \uc2  groups and women's health advocates say they are increasingly confused and frustrated by how major technology platforms moderate posts about \uc2  services.; Ashley Garcia with her daughter, Bella, 3, in San Diego this month. They wear matching outfits or coordinate colors for TikTok videos. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SARAH BLESENER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; ARIANA DREHSLER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240612ek6c0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
