\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 G.O.P. on Spot To Guarantee Birth Control\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Karni \par \uc2 1,064 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 By starting a discharge petition on legislation to codify the right to contraceptive access, Democrats aim to spotlight Republican opposition to the bill, part of a broader push to highlight the issue for voters.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats on Tuesday went on the offensive on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , announcing they will seek to force a vote on legislation to codify the right to birth control access nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The maneuver, through a procedural move known as a discharge petition, is all but certain to fail for lack of Republican support, but that is by design. It is part of a broad election-year push by Democrats to highlight Republicans' record of opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and other \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  choices that voters fear will be stripped away following the fall of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''The choice to use birth control should be yours and yours alone,'' Representative Katherine M. Clark of Massachusetts, the Democratic whip, who has held \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  events with 11 members and candidates across the country, said at a news conference outside the Capitol. House Republicans, she said, ''can put aside their MAGA ideology, join us, join the American people and get this bill passed. Or they can triple down on their anti-freedom extremism in full view of the American people.''\par \par \uc2 The move comes as Senate Democrats plan to force a vote this week on an identical contraceptive access bill, which Republicans are expected to block. The coordinated legislative push shows that Democrats regard access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  options as their strongest issues on which to draw a contrast with Republicans before the November elections.\par \par \uc2 ''Voters know that Republicans oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that they are generally supportive of restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Molly Murphy, a pollster for President Biden's re-election campaign. ''What voters don't know is that Republicans are actively trying to find ways to ban and restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception. That's a significant gap of opportunity for Democrats. There's a difference between being against something and actively working to take it away.''\par \par \uc2 The complicated and drawn-out discharge petition process allows lawmakers to make an end run around their leaders and force consideration of a piece of legislation on the floor if they collect the signatures of a majority of the members of the House. With Republicans holding a slim majority -- 217 seats to Democrats' 213 -- only a handful of defectors would need to sign to meet the threshold of 218 votes.\par \par \uc2 The legislation, sponsored by Representative Kathy Manning, Democrat of North Carolina, already has 203 co-sponsors, all Democrats, putting it well within striking distance. Under House rules, Democrats must wait seven workdays before they can start collecting signatures for the petition. After that, Democrats say they plan to spotlight Republicans' failure to sign on early and often -- focusing especially on those from competitive districts.\par \par \uc2 ''All those freshman Republicans in New York and California that cost us the House majority in 2022 will have to answer a question of why are they not signing it,'' said Chris Fleming, a Democratic strategist.\par \par \uc2 The discharge petition mechanism was created as a last-ditch check on the power of the majority party. While it seldom succeeds, earlier this year Democrats and Republicans did use one to force a vote on legislation that would provide tax relief to victims of disasters around the country, and it passed overwhelmingly.\par \par \uc2 The House first passed the Right to Contraception Act in July of 2022, when Democrats controlled the House, immediately after the Supreme Court's decision, in Dobbs vs. Jackson Women's Health Organization, that overturned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the federal level. Democrats pushed through the bill over almost unanimous Republican opposition in part because Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in a concurring opinion in Dobbs, wrote that the court ''should reconsider'' other precedents beyond Roe, including those protecting same-sex marriage and the right to contraception. Senate Republicans blocked the legislation, which was opposed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that said the bill's definition of contraceptives could be interpreted to include pills that induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Extremist Republican politicians are waging war on women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Manning said. ''They've stripped women of their constitutional right to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , attacked fertility treatments and are now attempting to restrict access to birth control.''\par \par \uc2 Outside groups are boosting the legislative push by pouring tens of millions of dollars into competitive House districts to amplify the message. The main super PAC supporting House Democrats last month announced a new $100 million fund focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in swing districts. And the group Americans for Contraception plans to spend more than $7 million on television and digital ads, some targeting Republicans in the Senate who vote against the bill and House members who do not sign the petition, and others thanking vulnerable Senate Democrats who vote to pass the bill this week. They also plan to have a 20-foot-tall I.U.D. roaming around Washington to raise awareness on the issue of contraception.\par \par \uc2 Republicans remain in a bind on issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they struggle to reconcile their party's hard-line policies on women's health measures and the reality that they are out of step with the vast majority of the country. Despite that, they continue to tuck anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies into pending legislation, a sign of the power of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobby in national politics.\par \par \uc2 A new Republican-written bill to fund the Department of Veterans Affairs includes language that prohibits the department from offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  counseling and, in certain cases, abortions to veterans and beneficiaries. And the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 's 2025 appropriations bill once again includes language that says no funds can be used for ''any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including through a medical benefits package or health benefits program that includes coverage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 ''House Republicans' dangerous plans to embed extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in must-pass legislation make one thing super clear,'' said Viet Shelton, a spokesman for the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee. ''House Republicans are still obsessed with banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, and their most vulnerable members are all in on this radical pursuit.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats view \uc2  access, contraception and \uc2  options as winning issues. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RUTH FREMSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A10) This article appeared in print on page A1, A10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240605ek650003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
