\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Republicans in Crucial Races Are Softening Their Stances on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bidgood and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 2,030 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Several G.O.P. Senate candidates used to embrace anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views. Now they are shifting their positions on an issue that has become an electoral liability for their party.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates in all eight of the country's most competitive Senate races have changed their approach on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , softening their rhetoric, shifting their positions and, in at least one case, embracing policies championed by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 From Michigan to Maryland, Republicans are trying to repackage their views to defang an issue that has hurt their party at the ballot box since the Supreme Court overturned federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. While the pivot is endemic across races in swing states, the most striking shifts have come from candidates who unsuccessfully ran for the Senate just two years ago in their home states, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views that sounded very different.\par \par \uc2 When Bernie Moreno, a Republican businessman, ran for a Senate seat in Ohio in 2022, he described his views as ''absolute pro-life, no exceptions.''\par \par \uc2 ''Life begins at conception'' and ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the murder of an innocent baby,'' he said on social media.\par \par \uc2 He has since softened his position. In March, he said he supported a 15-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. But his spokeswoman also says \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''should be primarily decided at the state level,'' that he backs ''reasonable exceptions'' and that he has maintained those positions throughout the 2024 election cycle.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, David McCormick, a Republican businessman running for Senate in Pennsylvania, touted his staunch commitment to opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Asked at a Republican primary debate that April if he would support exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans if Roe v. Wade was overturned, he said he believed in exceptions in the ''very rare instances'' when a woman's life was at risk.\par \par \uc2 Now, as he makes his second bid for the Senate, he has urged Americans to ''find common ground.'' Language saying ''life begins at conception'' has disappeared from his website, which now notes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in the state until 24 weeks -- the federal standard under Roe. And a campaign spokesman told CNN in April that Mr. McCormick ''inadvertently left out'' exceptions for rape and incest from his debate answer two years earlier.\par \par \uc2 Those kinds of shifts mark the latest effort by Republican candidates to reconcile their party's decades-old opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with the changed political reality of an issue that has helped power Democrats to electoral victory since the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Before the 2022 midterms, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  tried to rally Republicans around a 15-week federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, arguing that voters would embrace what some in his party framed as a ''reasonable'' limit. But the position became a cudgel for Democrats in key races and cost Republicans seats, with losses in swing states including Arizona, Michigan and Pennsylvania. Now, many Republicans are adopting former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's position of leaving the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 ''They're iterating,'' said Angela Kuefler, a Democratic pollster who is involved with Senate races in Florida and Arizona. ''They're aware of what a massive liability their position has been in the last couple elections since the fall of Roe.''\par \par \uc2 Some of the candidates have staked out positions that seem at odds, describing themselves as ''pro-life'' but also supportive of their own state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, which legalize the procedure in almost all cases.\par \par \uc2 National Republicans say that their position is clear and that it is Democrats who are trying to overcome their weak poll numbers on the economy, inflation and controlling the border by focusing the Senate races on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''Republican Senate candidates have clearly stated their opposition to a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, as well as their support for exceptions in the case of rape, incest, and to protect the life of the mother,'' said Tate Mitchell, a spokesman for the National Republican Senatorial Committee, the Senate Republican campaign arm. ''We plan to aggressively combat Democrat attempts to demagogue this issue.''\par \par \uc2 A handful of Democrats, too, have shifted their position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bob Casey of Pennsylvania, the incumbent Democrat who is trying to fend off Mr. McCormick, voted in 2018 to advance a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks of pregnancy and frequently referred to himself as a ''pro-life Democrat.'' But after the 2022 leak of the draft Supreme Court decision that would end Roe, he joined the efforts to codify its protections into law.\par \par \uc2 ''No senator has made a more radical change to his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than \uc2 Bob Casey Jr.\uc2 ,'' said Elizabeth Gregory, a spokeswoman for Mr. McCormick. ''His extreme stance is out of step with Pennsylvania.''\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Casey is an outlier in his party, which placed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the center of its 2024 messaging. Nearly all Democrats support legalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the federal level and generally do not back restrictions on the procedure, such as the number of weeks in pregnancy, saying the decision should be left to women and their doctors.\par \par \uc2 It's a position that's been embraced by one Republican running in a deep blue state. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the former governor of Maryland and a moderate Republican who is running for Senate, recently said he, too, would vote to codify Roe -- an embrace of a new position that came just days after he won his primary. He also said he would vote to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's Constitution, a measure that will be on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 Asked by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  how he would describe his view on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Hogan said, ''Given the definition of what I'm supporting -- women's rights to make their own decision -- I would say that's pro-choice.''\par \par \uc2 The other Republican Senate campaigns declined to make their candidates available for interviews on the topic. Some did not reply to general requests about the issue.\par \par \uc2 None of the other candidates have gone quite as far on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Mr. Hogan. But several have backed away from more strident anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions they've previously supported in public office.\par \par \uc2 Former Representative Mike Rogers of Michigan, the front-runner in the Michigan Senate Republican primary, cosponsored multiple anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures in the House, including bills granting constitutional rights to zygotes at conception. But now he says that, as a senator, he wouldn't support federal proposals that would undo the protections Michigan voters have put into place to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal until 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''The people of Michigan spoke in a loud voice in 2022 and this is a settled issue in our state,'' Mr. Rogers said in a statement. ''I will take no position as their voice in Washington that is at odds with the rights guaranteed by voters in the Michigan Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 And in Wisconsin, \uc2 Eric Hovde\uc2 , a Republican Senate candidate who in 2012 told reporters he was ''totally opposed'' to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the Milwaukee Journal-Sentinel, now says women have a ''right to make a choice'' early in a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Eric Hovde\uc2  can't hide from the fact that he supports an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and thinks politicians like him should be in charge of women's health care,'' his Democratic opponent, Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, urged anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates to ''go on offense'' to expose their opponents' ''extremism.''\par \par \uc2 ''We know what doesn't win elections: the 'ostrich strategy' of burying one's head in the sand and hoping this issue goes away,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains one of only a few issues where Democrats, who are struggling to combat poor ratings on the economy, foreign policy and immigration, have a political advantage. Some Republican strategists doubt the issue will have the same resonance in 2024 as it did in the past two election cycles, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights energized a coalition of liberal, independent and even some moderate Republican women behind Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 A series of polls in battleground states by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Siena College and The Philadelphia Inquirer found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranked below the economy and immigration when voters were asked what issue was most important in determining their vote.\par \par \uc2 However, 11 percent of battleground state voters -- and 17 percent of women -- ranked it as their most important issue, which suggests there is still a core of voters deeply activated around the issue.\par \par \uc2 With a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums expected to appear on ballots in states across the country, Democrats believe the issue will remain central for voters in the fall. They've spent significant time and money reminding voters of Republicans' past support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and bans, blanketing airwaves with the records of their opponents. More than a quarter of their ads in the first four months of the year focused on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''On record and on video, Republican Senate candidates have made clear they back harsh restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and oppose women's right to make their own health care decisions,'' said David Bergstein, a spokesman for the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee. ''Republican candidates know their agenda is unpopular, and their pathetic attempt to hide their position just reinforces why voters don't trust them.''\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, much of the Democratic effort has been focused on Arizona, where party officials and their allies have spent millions boosting a ballot measure that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state Constitution. The issue emerged as a political flashpoint this month when lawmakers repealed a near-total ban on abortions but enforced a 15-week ban, with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two years, Kari Lake, the Republican now running for Senate, has appeared to be on all sides of Arizona's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation.\par \par \uc2 She praised the state's 1864 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that banned nearly all abortions in the state when she was running for governor in 2022, calling it a ''great law.'' She also said she would sign a ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But last month, when the state's Supreme Court reinstated the 1864 law, she denounced it as ''out of step'' with Arizonans and personally called Republicans in the statehouse to say she supported repealing it. In a video, she said she opposed federal funds on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as federal bans.\par \par \uc2 Not every Republican's shift has been drastic. Some candidates, Ms. Kuefler said, have simply quietly shifted their tone, eschewing graphic discussions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of warmer language.\par \par \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2 , a Republican running for Senate in Montana, accused Democrats of ''murdering our unborn children'' on a local radio show in 2023, before he announced his bid. Earlier this year, he described himself as backing ''common-sense protections'' on the procedure, said he believed further restrictions should be left to the states and said he supported exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Sam Brown, the Republican front-runner in the Senate primary in Nevada, was named chairman of his state's branch of the Faith and Freedom Coalition. The announcement of his appointment said one of the group's aims was ''protecting life.''\par \par \uc2 But as a candidate for Senate, he sat for an emotional interview in which his wife, Amy Brown, told the story of her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In that interview, he said he respected a Nevada state law allowing abortions up to 24 weeks of pregnancy and opposes a federal ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''Amy and I have spoken extensively about this topic and believe, first and foremost, that mothers who are facing an unplanned pregnancy deserve the utmost compassion and understanding,'' Mr. Brown said in a statement, in which he called himself ''pro-life'' but supportive of exceptions. ''Like President Trump, I believe the issue is now correctly left at the state level and applaud his leadership.''\par \par \uc2 Bernie Moreno in Ohio went from ''no exceptions'' to backing a 15-week \uc2  ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240531ek5v0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
