\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Liberal Groups to Focus on State Court Races\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Corasaniti \par \uc2 580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The National Democratic Redistricting Committee and Planned Parenthood Votes are joining together to help their preferred candidates for 2024 as state-level party battles gain steam.\par \par \uc2 Two groups on the left with differing missions are joining forces to bolster their preferred candidates in state supreme court races in November, as such elections grow increasingly expensive and politically polarizing.\par \par \uc2 The two organizations -- the National Democratic Redistricting Committee, the Democrats' arm in fighting for state and congressional maps; and Planned Parenthood Votes, the political arm of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  health care organization -- will initially target races in Arizona, Michigan, Montana, North Carolina, Ohio and Texas. The fund, with a budget of $5 million, will provide digital ads along with funding for canvassing and get-out-the-vote operations.\par \par \uc2 ''Our aim is to protect the independence of state supreme courts, to ensure that they are composed of justices who are dedicated to interpreting the law in a neutral way, who will adhere to precedent and who will protect the fundamental rights of all citizens,'' \uc2 Eric H. Holder Jr.\uc2 , the former attorney general who is chairman of the redistricting group, said in a statement announcing the joint venture.\par \par \uc2 Until recent years, state supreme court races were traditionally relatively nonpartisan affairs -- in most states candidates are not officially affiliated with a political party -- though partisan leanings could be gleaned from judicial rulings. But political interest in state supreme court races has exploded, culminating in a $50 million race for State Supreme Court in Wisconsin last year.\par \par \uc2 National interest in state government battles has rapidly accelerated since Donald J. Trump was elected president in 2016. State-level races, including ones for governor, secretary of state and legislatures, were estimated to surpass $7 billion in fund-raising in 2022. And with Congress largely deadlocked, heated political and policy debates have largely unfolded at the state level.\par \par \uc2 Many groups, both Republican and Democratic, will pour money into state supreme court races this year. The Republican State Leadership Committee spent more than $4.3 million on state supreme court races in 2018, and it has not announced plans for 2024.\par \par \uc2 The alliance between the redistricting organization and \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was forged over four years, when the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  issued landmark rulings upending both the governance of redistricting and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the Supreme Court ruled in Rucho v. Common Cause that federal courts are powerless to hear challenges to partisan gerrymandering, leaving state courts the lone venue to fight against a political tilt to a district.\par \par \uc2 And in 2022, the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade left \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access up to the states. Since then, state supreme courts have played an increasingly critical role in finalizing laws regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Just last month, it was the State Supreme Court in Arizona, and not its Legislature, that restored an 1864 law that effectively banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state. (The Republican-led Legislature would later repeal that ban.)\par \par \uc2 ''We are in the fight of our lives to protect and restore our fundamental freedoms,'' \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 , said in a statement. ''And our courts are the front lines.''\par \par \uc2 A race last year for the Supreme Court in Wisconsin hit $50 million in spending. Such campaigns are drawing interest from parties. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240521ek5l0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
