\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Harris Warns of Restrictive Future Rulings by Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,088 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In an interview with The Times, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  deepened her criticism of the conservative justices who overturned Roe and singled out \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's views on other settled cases.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  attacked the conservative-controlled Supreme Court on Wednesday, warning that its future decisions could limit a broad range of civil rights and personal freedoms for many Americans.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , she expanded on her criticism of the court's decision to overturn federally guaranteed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in 2022, going beyond President Biden's past comments to raise direct alarms about Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and the broader direction of the court.\par \par \uc2 ''This court has shown itself to be an activist court,'' said Ms. Harris, who previously served as California's attorney general and as the district attorney of San Francisco. ''I worry about fundamental freedoms across the board.''\par \par \uc2 Asked what specific legal precedents could be undone by the court, Ms. Harris demurred, saying she was ''hesitant'' to do so.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't want to, at this point, use my voice in a way that is alarmist,'' she said. ''But this court has made it very clear that they are willing to undo recognized rights.''\par \par \uc2 The interview, conducted after a campaign event focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Pennsylvania, covered a range of issues related to the court, the 2024 election and the state of American \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Ms. Harris's comments went beyond previous criticism of the country's highest court by the Biden administration, though her remarks were far less scathing than the direct attacks made during the previous administration, when then-President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  went after Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In March, Mr. Biden said the justices ''made a mistake'' and ''read the Constitution wrong'' in their decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, but he limited his assessment to that specific decision.\par \par \uc2 When pressed during the interview on Wednesday, Ms. Harris pointed to Justice Thomas's writings in the case that overturned Roe as an indication of where the court might be headed. In a frank concurring opinion, Justice Thomas wrote that the court should ''reconsider'' decisions that guaranteed rights to same-sex intercourse and marriage and to contraception.\par \par \uc2 ''You could even look at \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  saying a lot of the quiet part out loud,'' she said. ''Just look at what he said and then maybe that gives us some indication. Just look at one of the justices to see where they might go next.''\par \par \uc2 During her time as a senator from California, Ms. Harris opposed the confirmation of Justices \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 . Her combative and direct questioning of Mr. Kavanaugh during his contentious confirmation hearings in 2018 helped lift Ms. Harris into the national political spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris declined to say whether she believed any of the justices had lied during their hearings, when they were asked if Roe was an established legal precedent.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the presidential race, she said Mr. Trump doesn't trust women to make their own decisions, but she refused to say whether the former president respects women in any capacity.\par \par \uc2 She said she judged Mr. Trump based on his conduct, adding that she had never met him personally.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know what is in his mind,'' she said. ''I will say that when you look at Trump \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans around the country, those \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans suggest that there's a lack of trust of women to be able to know what's in their own best interest.''\par \par \uc2 The vice president predicted a dark future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should Mr. Trump be elected again to the presidency. Women's pregnancies could be monitored in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned to prevent them from obtaining the procedure, she said, and Mr. Trump would sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if such legislation reached his desk. ''I think they might do that,'' Mr. Trump said of states' monitoring of women's pregnancies in an interview with Time magazine last month. ''You'll have to speak to the individual states.''\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Mr. Trump has tried to soften his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, saying the issue should be left to states to decide and refusing to accede to the demands of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and endorse a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris said she was not worried that such efforts could help Mr. Trump win support from voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I think the American people recognize when they're being gaslit and have the ability to decipher beyond the charade,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a central cause of her vice presidency, traveling the country to visit clinics and hold events in states where bans have been enforced. In Washington, she has conducted a steady stream of meetings with activists, state legislators, doctors and patients to try to find ways to combat conservative efforts to enforce bans and limit access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 At the campaign event in Elkins Park, Pa., on Wednesday, she cast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as an issue of personal freedom and the bans as a type of government overreach into the private lives of American women -- messaging that has been widely adopted by Democrats since the Supreme Court overturned federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''One does not have to abandon their faith or their deeply held beliefs to agree that the government should not tell her what to do,'' she told the audience at an event with \uc2 Sheryl Lee Ralph\uc2 , an actress on the TV show ''Abbott Elementary.'' ''Do you not trust women to know what is in their own best interest?''\par \par \uc2 Later that evening, she returned to Washington to deliver remarks at the annual gala of Emily's List, the organization that supports Democrats who back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In her interview, Ms. Harris said ''more work has to be done'' to persuade voters to re-elect the Biden-Harris ticket, even on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''It's an election for president of the United States -- by its very nature, it requires a hard-fought campaign and a hard-won victory,'' she said. ''No one should be handed the presidency, right?''\par \par \uc2 At a campaign event in Elkins Park, Pa., Vice President Kamala Harris cast \uc2  rights, a central cause of her vice presidency, as an issue of personal freedom. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JUSTIN T. GELLERSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240510ek5a0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
