\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Latinas Shift on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Democrats See an Opportunity\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jazmine Ulloa \par \uc2 1,342 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hispanic views on the issue vary widely, and Democrats face hurdles, but opportunities, too. As one House candidate said: ''I go to Mass, but I also support a woman's right to choose.''\par \par \uc2 Hours before Arizona state legislators voted to repeal an 1864 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban last month, a group of mostly Latina Democrats huddled at a nearby Mexican restaurant for a strategy session on galvanizing Latina voters over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I am 23 -- why do I have less rights than my abuelita in Mexico?'' Melissa Herrera, a Democratic campaign staffer, asked the cluster of women at the restaurant, referring to her grandmother.\par \par \uc2 The question crystallized what Democrats hope will be a decisive electoral factor in their favor this year, one that upends conventional political wisdom: A majority of Latino voters now support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, according to polls, a reversal from two decades ago. Polling trends, interviews with strategists and election results in Ohio and Virginia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights played a central role, suggest Democrats' optimism regarding Latinas -- once considered too religious or too socially conservative to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- could bear out.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade in 2022, stringent curbs have been taking effect in Republican-dominated states. In Arizona, for one, the May 2 repeal of the blanket ban from 1864 still leaves abortions governed by a two-year-old law prohibiting the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy, with no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 As of April 2023, according to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 , 62 percent of Latinos believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. Twenty years earlier, most Hispanics told \uc2 Pew\uc2  that they opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights by a nearly two-to-one margin. (The most recent polling has been conducted online, instead of over the phone, but the surveys show an overall gradual shift in opinions.)\par \par \uc2 Latino majorities came out in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023 elections in Ohio and Virginia, according to other surveys, and women played a major role in stalling the shift of Hispanic voters toward the Republican Party in 2022, when many voted for Democrats, citing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the most important issue.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to be an essential issue this cycle,'' said Victoria McGroary, the executive director of BOLD PAC, the campaign arm of the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2 . ''We are going to see what I think is going to be crystal-clear evidence that reproductive freedom matters to Latino voters.''\par \par \uc2 Surveys show the diversity of the Latino voting population still poses some obstacles for Democrats, with support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights varying based on factors including age, geography and party affiliation. Latino voters in South Texas and South Florida remain more culturally conservative, and a majority of Latino evangelicals, a growing segment of the population, still says \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal.\par \par \uc2 Within that culturally conservative world, many remain unmoved.\par \par \uc2 Leaving a shopping plaza in Phoenix, Daisy Ochoa, 31, a paralegal, said she was planning to vote for Republicans in November because their stances on the issue are in line with her Christian faith.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe that if there is life, there is life,'' she said. ''I don't think anybody should take life, unless there's some threat to the mom.''\par \par \uc2 But outside a grocery store near downtown, Gina Fernandez, 52, a Democrat and an administrative assistant, offered signs that Democrats had struck a nerve. She said she had been raised in a Mexican American and Roman Catholic household but had considered her right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a foregone conclusion until the Supreme Court overturned Roe. That jolted her and her 19-year-old daughter. She used to vote for the best candidate regardless of party affiliation, Ms. Fernandez said.\par \par \uc2 ''This cycle, I'm voting for all Democrats,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Democratic officials and activists in Arizona point to lingering uncertainty over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state, since the repeal will not take effect until 90 days after the Legislature adjourns for the summer. That, they say, is fueling support for a November ballot initiative that would enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's Constitution -- and could lift Democrats up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 ''It is still not over,'' said Mary Rose Wilcox, a former city councilwoman and elected county official who owns El Portal, the restaurant that has served as a center of Latino political activity in Phoenix and hosted the April strategy session. ''We need a straight law that safeguards protections.''\par \par \uc2 The women also said they needed to counter what they called misconceptions about Latino voters' conservatism.\par \par \uc2 ''I always say I'm a pro-choice Catholic,'' Raquel Ter\u225\'00\'E1n, a Democratic House candidate who convened the round-table meeting, said in an interview. ''I go to Mass, but I also support a woman's right to choose.''\par \par \uc2 Rosie Villegas-Smith, a Mexican immigrant who founded Voces Unidas por la Vida, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization in Phoenix, said she believed Hispanic support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in recent polling was overblown. She accused Democrats of fear-mongering and misleading voters on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''They speak in euphemisms and say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is health care but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not health care,'' she said. ''Once Latinos learn what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  truly is, they are against it.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans at the national level argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not going to matter more to Latinos than crime, border security or the economy, particularly among working-class families worried about the cost of gas and groceries.\par \par \uc2 ''You have seen Republicans making up ground with Latino voters because of a message on those issues,'' said Jack Pandol, a spokesman for the National Republican Congressional Committee, the campaign arm of House Republicans. ''They have a better message on improving quality of life, on bringing costs down, on making communities safe.''\par \par \uc2 A crop of Latina Democratic candidates is nonetheless running on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in districts with large or fast-growing Hispanic populations. In interviews, some said the fall of Roe had made the issue more urgent for their constituencies -- and made voters more receptive to their message that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access was crucial to personal freedom and health care, even if the voters themselves were against the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In Oregon, Representative Andrea Salinas, who in 2022 became one of the first two Hispanic candidates elected to Congress from the state, said she cast the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a matter of ''empowering women to make their own personal choices with their doctor.''\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't have as much as my competitors to put out glossy mailers or fancy television ads, but what I did have I used to lean into \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Ms. Salinas, adding that the issue helped fuel her victory in a northeastern district home to the most Latinos in the state.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ter\u225\'00\'E1n, who is running to become the first Latina to represent Arizona in Congress, recalled that Democratic operatives cautioned her not to talk about her past work experience with \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group, when she first ran for a state legislative seat in 2018 because it was a Latino-heavy district. She disregarded that advice and won.\par \par \uc2 She went on to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights central to her platform in the Arizona House. In 2019, she and other state lawmakers visited El Salvador to study the impact of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and they met with women who had been imprisoned for having the procedure done. She later co-wrote the measure that repealed Arizona's 1864 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 Latina Democrats gathered last month at El Portal, a longtime center of Latino political activity in Phoenix, to discuss \uc2  rights.; ''I go to Mass, but I also support a woman's right to choose,'' said Raquel Ter\u225\'00\'E1n, a House candidate running to represent Arizona.; Rosie Villegas-Smith, who runs an anti-\uc2  organization in Phoenix, said that Democrats are misleading voters on the issue. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY PAUL RATJE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240509ek590004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
