\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 MAGA Tries to Dodge  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Foes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 1,172 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kari Lake's appearance at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  last week was billed as a town hall, but it wasn't really, because only representatives of young conservative groups were permitted to ask her anything.\par \par \uc2 Their questions were largely airy softballs about leadership, the border, and getting Republicans to turn out in November. Lake won applause for a casual threat of violence against trans women, saying that, if she had ever seen ''a man'' walk into a locker room while her daughter was changing, he wouldn't have ''walked out.'' Later she spoke about the gun she bought her daughter for her 21st birthday: ''I said, if anybody attacks you, unload that thing, baby. Unload it.'' The crowd laughed appreciatively.\par \par \uc2 Only one question, from Danise Rees, president of the campus chapter of Students for Life, was at all challenging. As Rees noted, Lake had taken conflicting positions on Arizona's 1864 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Two years ago, Lake called it a ''great law.'' But last month, after a court ruling upholding the statute generated widespread outrage, she came out against it, and even lobbied Republicans to repeal it. Then she waffled again, bemoaning the state attorney general's decision not to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 Rees wanted clarity about where Lake stood. ''We've seen both your opposition and your support for this law in the past,'' she said. ''Can you please define your values and tell us how you will remedy the doubts that pro-lifers have in you right now?''\par \par \uc2 Lake didn't have a concise answer. She echoed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the need for exceptions in cases of rape and incest, which the Arizona ban doesn't have. She suggested that if Republicans let the law stand, it would fuel liberal efforts to pass a ballot initiative enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state Constitution. She spoke about her own love of motherhood and the merits of family policy in Hungary, a nod to the MAGA movement's infatuation with the Hungarian strongman \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the core of her argument was electability. ''There's so much on the line this election,'' she said. ''We're either going to save our country, or the Democrats -- I call them Communists -- are going to drive us right to the ground.'' Given the existential stakes, Lake insisted, Republicans can't let themselves get ''caught up'' with wedge issues like this one.\par \par \uc2 It was a curious argument coming from Lake, who later offered a soliloquy against compromising the conservative movement's principles. But it was a sign of how difficult it has been for the Republican Party to balance its base's demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans with the public revulsion when those policies are enacted.\par \par \uc2 Eight years ago, many Christian conservatives put aside their qualms about Trump in the hope that he would lay the groundwork for outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was a bargain that paid off, at least in the medium term. But today, in purplish parts of the country like Arizona, that political equation is being turned on its head, as some on the right argue that they must put aside qualms about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so Republicans can win elections.\par \par \uc2 Just this week, Arizona repealed its sweeping 1864 ban, a law that Trump said went too far and would be ''straightened out.'' It was the first state with a Republican legislature to back off its most draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions since the fall of Roe. In Nevada, Sam Brown, a longtime \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponent running for Senate, has come out against a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and in February he gave a TV interview with his wife in which she spoke about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  she had before they met. \uc2 Eric Hovde\uc2 , a Republican Senate candidate in Wisconsin, once declared himself ''totally opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' but now says women should have a ''right to make a choice'' early in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party, of course, is still very much the party of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. This week, Florida, the third most populous state, began prohibiting the procedure at six weeks. In an interview with Time magazine, Trump said he wouldn't try to stop states from prosecuting women who have had abortions, and refused to say whether he would veto a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. If he wins in November, conservatives have plans to use the Comstock Act, a federal law from the same era as the Arizona ban, to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. Idaho is already in the Supreme Court fighting the federal government's attempts to make emergency rooms treat women with failing pregnancies before they're on the verge of death. And in Louisiana, where almost all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, lawmakers are moving to criminalize the mere possession of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Still, in swing states, Republican Party leaders are trying to distance themselves from the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, treating it much the way skittish Democrats once treated the movement for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Back in the 1990s, Democrats relied on pro-choice votes, but haunted by old taunts about representing ''acid, amnesty and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' they held activists at a remove, and their leaders often expressed either disapproval or ambivalence about terminating pregnancies. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  vetoed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation and put pro-choice judges on the Supreme Court, but he also said the procedure should be ''safe, legal and rare.'' As late as 2005, Hillary Clinton called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a ''sad, even tragic choice.''\par \par \uc2 Now, however, the Democratic Party is united in championing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with the vice president recently making history by visiting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, and it's Republicans who are flailing as they contend with a pro-choice backlash. It remains to be seen whether anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces can acclimate to their new status as the embarrassing stepchildren of a coalition that once coddled them.\par \par \uc2 At the Arizona Capitol last week, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents packed the House chamber to protest its vote to scrap the state ban, few blamed Trump or Lake, and some didn't even realize the ex-president had opposed the law. After Lake's event, however, Rees said she was disappointed in Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think that it's fair to expect pro-lifers to show up for you the second time around when you completely take a hands-off approach on pro-life issues,'' she said. Still, she recognized that people like her don't have another choice. ''I think there's going to be probably other reasons why pro-lifers go out to vote,'' she said, ''but I think it's going to be a very reluctant vote.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JESSE RIESER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page SR5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240505ek550003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
