\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Voters in Missouri and South Dakota May Have the Final Say on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 857 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Both states are reliably Republican and have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that are among the strictest in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Two more states with near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are poised to have citizen-sponsored measures on the ballot this year that would allow voters to reverse those bans by establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups in Missouri turned in 380,159 signatures to put the amendment on the ballot, more than double the 172,000 signatures required by law. The Missouri organizers' announcement followed a petition drive in South Dakota that announced on Wednesday that it, too, had turned in many more signatures than required for a ballot amendment there.\par \par \uc2 Both groups are hoping to build on the momentum of other states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have prevailed in seven out of seven ballot measures in the two years since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, which had established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for nearly five decades.\par \par \uc2 Groups in about 10 other states have secured spots on the ballot for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures or are collecting signatures to do so. Those include Arizona and Nevada, swing states where Democrats are hoping that voters who are newly energized around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will help President Biden win re-election.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota and Missouri are reliably Republican states. But their bans are among the strictest in the nation, outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except to save the life of a pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 Missouri, where post-Roe polls show that a majority of voters want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal in all or most cases, appears to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups the bigger chance of success. But both measures face significant hurdles.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who control the Missouri legislature are pushing another ballot question that would appear before voters in August and make it harder for future ballot amendments to succeed.\par \par \uc2 That measure would raise the threshold for victory, requiring not only a majority of voters statewide but a majority of voters in five out of the state's eight congressional districts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters fear the requirement would allow a minority in rural areas that tend to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to vote down the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Missouri legislators are expected to vote on that measure before their session ends this month. State officials will also have to decide whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure appears on the primary election ballot in August, when turnout tends to be light, or in the general election in November.\par \par \uc2 The state's Republican leaders have attempted to keep the measure from going before voters for more than a year. The secretary of state, \uc2 Jay Ashcroft\uc2 , attempted to change the language of the ballot measure in ways that could have inflamed fears that it would lead to dangerous and unregulated abortions; supporters of the measure successfully sued to block him.\par \par \uc2 The amendment in Missouri is similar to ones passed in Ohio and Michigan. It would establish ''the right to make and carry out decisions about all matters relating to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,'' including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Legislature could regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the point when, ''in the good faith judgment'' of the treating health care professional, the fetus could survive outside the uterus without ''extraordinary medical measures.''\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, getting the signatures certified could be a challenge because Republican legislators passed a law in March allowing signers to withdraw their support. Backers of the amendment collected about 55,000 signatures, 20,000 more than needed, but anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are working to collect enough reversals to keep the measure off the ballot.\par \par \uc2 The amendment effort in South Dakota has been largely driven by one group, Dakotans for Health. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and other usual allies of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have declined to support the effort. The groups say that the ballot amendment leaves open the possibility that the legislature could continue to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily that the amendment would allow, in the words of the state chapter of \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in name only.''\par \par \uc2 The South Dakota amendment would prohibit the state from regulating ''a pregnant woman's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision and its effectuation'' during the first trimester, but would allow regulations on the procedure during the second trimester only in ways that are ''reasonably related to the physical health of the pregnant woman.'' In the third trimester, the state could regulate and even ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as long as the ban included exceptions for the ''life or health of the pregnant woman.''\par \par \uc2 South Dakota is one of a handful of states where polls show that less than a majority of voters -- 47 percent in a recent survey -- believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. (The other states in that same survey were North Dakota, Arkansas, Idaho, and Utah.)\par \par \uc2 The Missouri House in session. An \uc2  rights measure could face a more difficult path if Republicans alter how ballot questions are approved. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DAVID A. LIEB/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240504ek5400039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
