\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats See  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights as a Galvanizing Issue Before the November Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As a six-week ban takes effect in Florida, and Arizona legislators repealed their 1864 law restricting the procedure, Democrats hope the issue will help carry them through a tough political environment.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, a ban on most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy took effect.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, state lawmakers repealed a stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that dates to the Civil War era.\par \par \uc2 And across the country, the presidential campaign trail on Wednesday was brimming with reminders of just how central Democrats hope the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights debate will be to voters' decisions this fall.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two years after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Democrats are betting that the tangible effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that many Americans are already experiencing -- and the threats of more to come -- will help their party power through an ominous and volatile political environment, as Republicans struggle to address an issue that has become a significant, sustained liability for them.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is to blame for the harm state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are doing to women every day in our country,'' Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wrote on social media on Wednesday morning, ahead of delivering remarks n Jacksonville, Fla., about the state's ''extreme'' new ban.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, she said there, would bring ''more bans, more suffering, less freedom,'' if he won re-election.\par \par \uc2 As they did in the midterm elections in 2022, Democrats are borrowing from language long favored by Republicans -- about freedom and limiting the reach of government -- to make their case.\par \par \uc2 They believe that Mr. Trump, whose Supreme Court nominees helped overturn Roe, recently bolstered their argument further.\par \par \uc2 In a Time magazine interview released on Tuesday, Mr. Trump refused to commit to vetoing a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and said he would allow states to monitor women's pregnancies and prosecute those who violated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''There seems to be no limit to how invasive Trump would let the state be,'' President Biden said in a video released on Wednesday morning. ''This should be a decision between a woman and her doctor, and the government should get out of people's lives.''\par \par \uc2 The focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights propelled Democrats in the midterm elections, when candidates harnessed voter anger over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions to overcome challenging national headwinds in key contests.\par \par \uc2 And it has remained a potent force in subsequent elections.\par \par \uc2 State Representative Mike Caruso of Florida, a Republican who opposed the six-week ban, noted that a number of states including Florida are expected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights-related measures on the ballot this fall.\par \par \uc2 ''It's going to hurt Republicans,'' he said. For Democrats who were unenthusiastic about Mr. Biden, he said, ''now they've got reason they can show up. I think it's going to have a major impact on the elections in November.''\par \par \uc2 But it is not yet clear how galvanizing the issue will be across the country in a presidential election shaped by economic concerns at home and crises abroad, with two well-known and unpopular men -- one of whom, Mr. Trump, faces multiple criminal cases -- at the top of their party's tickets.\par \par \uc2 ''President Trump has long been consistent in supporting the rights of states to make decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Karoline Leavitt, a spokeswoman for Mr. Trump, said in a statement. ''Women want a president who will secure our nation's borders, remove violent criminals from our neighborhoods and build an economy that helps hardworking families thrive.''\par \par \uc2 And even as Democrats sought to keep the issue at the forefront of voters' minds on Wednesday, they were competing with unrest at college campuses across the country, including in critical battleground states, as students protested the war in Gaza, with many objecting to Mr. Biden's support for Israel.\par \par \uc2 Such scenes of turmoil, some party strategists have warned, can be damaging for the party that controls the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Nehamas contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Nehamas contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240502ek520003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
