\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Will Florida's Strict  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Shuffle the State's Political Trends?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,215 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ban, which took effect on Wednesday, was part of Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's push into cultural conservatism. But Florida politics is rarely that simple.\par \par \uc2 As Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida prepared to run for president last spring, he gathered anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in his Capitol office for an unusual bill signing, held late at night and behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 Florida lawmakers had just approved a ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, a major policy shift that would sharply restrict access to the procedure for women in neighboring states as well as for Floridians. That law took effect on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 For Mr. DeSantis, the move seemed like something that would play well among some Republican presidential primary voters in states like Iowa. But this was Florida, and public opinion polls suggested broad opposition to such a strict law.\par \par \uc2 So Mr. DeSantis, who typically crisscrosses the state to sign bills, enacted the six-week ban in April 2023 with little fanfare, part of a headlong push into cultural conservatism meant to bolster his national campaign.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis dropped out of the presidential race in January. His culture wars appear to have peaked, at least for now. Voters in a string of states, including more traditionally Republican ones, have chosen to protect or expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A similar ballot measure will go before Florida voters in November, with the potential to significantly influence contests down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the biggest political question in Florida, though, is just how much \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might swing the election. Is it unique enough to turn around a state that has trended reliably Republican?\par \par \uc2 The proposed constitutional amendment, known as Amendment 4, would allow abortions ''before viability,'' or up to about 24 weeks, and would need more than 60 percent support to pass. That threshold is high, especially in the face of an organized opposition campaign characterizing the language as too far-reaching.\par \par \uc2 ''The average Floridian, when they hear the truth about this extreme amendment, they will vote it down,'' State Representative Jenna Persons-Mulicka, a Fort Myers Republican, said last month.\par \par \uc2 But some Floridians, including some Republicans, have wondered whether a relentless pursuit of divisive policies ahead of Mr. DeSantis's presidential run might now be forcing a bit of recalibration to be more in line with the state's diverse electorate.\par \par \uc2 The governor and Republican lawmakers pursued fewer culture war fights during this year's legislative session. They made it harder for residents to file book challenges in schools. The state also settled a lawsuit filed by opponents of a law prohibiting instruction on sexual orientation and gender identity through the eighth grade.\par \par \uc2 ''We're very much Middle America,'' said the Rev. Sarah Robinson, pastor of the Audubon Park Covenant Church in Orlando, who attended a ''Yes on 4'' rally last month. ''Middle-class people who are trying to raise families and care for their communities. And there are definitely things that they'd rather be doing than fighting these policies.''\par \par \uc2 National Democrats have expressed optimism that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure could put Florida in play, despite no clear commitment of how much money the party is willing to spend in the state and a substantial Democratic disadvantage in voter registrations. President Biden briefly spoke about the six-week ban in Tampa last week, and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will travel to Jacksonville to draw attention to the state ban on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 ''There is one person responsible for this nightmare: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,'' Mr. Biden said in a statement about the ban.\par \par \uc2 Asked on Tuesday about Democrats' hopeful claims, Mr. DeSantis offered a dismissive ''Pfft'' and laughed.\par \par \uc2 ''I welcome Biden-Harris to spend a lot of money in Florida. Light up the airwaves,'' he said, indicating that the funds would be poorly spent. ''We are fine with you doing that here, but I can confidently predict that you will see Republican victories, not just at the top of the ticket but up and down the ballot.''\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , the presumptive Republican nominee, who won Florida in 2016 and 2020 and resides in Palm Beach, has refused to say whether he will vote against the constitutional amendment. He has recently suggested that some states have gone too far in restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though in keeping with his shifting views on the issue, he has also said that the decision should lie with the states.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have tried to pin the strict state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on Mr. Trump for appointing to the Supreme Court justices who helped overturn Roe v. Wade in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Mr. DeSantis has rarely campaigned on the ban that he signed. On Tuesday, he mostly focused on waiving admission fees for Florida state parks during Memorial Day weekend and on promoting Gulf red snapper season.\par \par \uc2 ''This was done to help \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in his ambitious plan to run for president,'' State Senator Lauren Book, the Democratic minority leader, said of the ban. ''It didn't work, and it has really created dire, dangerous consequences for women.''\par \par \uc2 Florida is full of transplants from the Northeast and Midwest, and their cultural politics have skewed more liberal -- or at least more libertarian -- than those in other parts of the Deep South. Floridians have elected Republicans while also approving liberal ballot proposals, including ones that raised the minimum wage to $15 an hour, restored felons' voting rights and legalized medical marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Before Mr. DeSantis enacted a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in April 2022, Florida allowed abortions up to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 John Stemberger, the president of Liberty Counsel Action, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobbying group, said that Florida's 24-week law had less to do with public opinion and more to do with legal precedent set by the Florida Supreme Court in 1989. The court ruled then that a privacy clause in the State Constitution extended to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''It didn't really reflect the demographics of Florida,'' Mr. Stemberger said of the old ruling. ''It reflected the opinion of seven justices who made a policy-oriented decision.''\par \par \uc2 The court, now conservative and nearly entirely appointed by Mr. DeSantis, reversed that position on April 1. Mr. Stemberger credited Mr. DeSantis for stocking his administration with ''solid social conservatives'' willing to push \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions: ''Personnel is policy.''\par \par \uc2 Even with the 15-week ban in place, there was an uptick in abortions in Florida last year, in part because women from other Southern states with stricter laws had traveled to Florida for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Loraine Pi\u241\'00\'F1eiro, executive director of the Florida Access Network, a fund that helps women in Florida pay for abortions, said that requests for support doubled in April, as the countdown to the six-week ban was underway. The organization increased its budget by 25 percent for the month but still had to turn away some patients.\par \par \uc2 ''The reality is that people are going to continue to need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,'' she said, ''regardless of the election cycle.''\par \par \uc2  will be on the ballot in November in Florida, raising the question of whether it might affect the state's reliably Republican tilt. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARTINA TUATY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240502ek520003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
