\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Arizona Repeals 1864  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban as 2 Republicans Cross Aisle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Healy and Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,546 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two Republican state senators broke with their party to ensure final passage of the repeal. Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat, is expected to sign it on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Arizona lawmakers voted on Wednesday to repeal an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that first became law when Abraham Lincoln was president and a half-century before women won the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 A bill to repeal the law passed, 16-14, in the Republican-controlled State Senate with the support of every Democratic senator and two Republicans who broke with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  conservatives who dominate their party. It now goes to Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat, who is expected to sign it on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The vote was the culmination of a fevered effort to repeal the law that has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central focus of Arizona's politics.\par \par \uc2 ''We are standing in a moment of Arizona history,'' said State Senator Anna Hernandez, a Democrat who called the repeal measure up to a vote on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The issue has galvanized Democratic voters and energized a campaign to put an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot measure before Arizona voters in November. On the right, it created a rift between anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who want to keep the law in place and Republican politicians who worry about the political backlash that could be prompted by support of a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The 1864 law had gathered dust on the books for decades. But it exploded into an election-year flashpoint three weeks ago when a 4-2 decision by the State Supreme Court, whose justices are all Republican-appointed, said the ban could now be enforced because of the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 After the repeal is finalized, abortions in Arizona will be governed by a 2022 law that prohibits the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy, and makes no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 But the repeal will not take effect until 90 days after the Legislature adjourns for the summer, meaning that the 1864 ban could still end up temporarily derailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Arizona if a court allows it to go into effect. Arizona's Democratic attorney general and Planned Parenthood Arizona have gone to court to keep the law from being implemented.\par \par \uc2 Two Republican state senators, T.J. Shope and Shawnna Bolick, joined with Democrats on Wednesday to force that repeal bill to a vote over furious attempts by far-right Republicans to block it.\par \par \uc2 Before casting her pivotal vote, Ms. Bolick stood up and began a long, deeply personal speech describing her own three challenging pregnancies, including one that ended with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure in her first trimester because the fetus was not viable.\par \par \uc2 ''Would Arizona's pre-Roe law have allowed me to have this medical procedure even though my life wasn't in danger?'' she asked.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Bolick, whose husband is one of the Arizona Supreme Court justices who upheld the 1864 law, also railed against \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and Democratic support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. She suggested that her vote to repeal the 1864 ban could be the best shot for conservatives to thwart the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 ''We should be pushing for the maximum protection for unborn children that can be sustained,'' she said. ''I side with saving more babies' lives.''\par \par \uc2 As she spoke, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents watching from the public gallery erupted with angry shouts: ''Come on!'' ''This is a disgrace!'' ''One day you will face a just and holy God!''\par \par \uc2 The repeal was a significant loss for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, which had relied on its outsized strength in the Arizona Legislature, The vote came hours after conservatives celebrated a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that went into effect in Florida on Wednesday morning. The repeal leaves Republican lawmakers divided about whether to place their own more restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure on the ballot in November to compete with the constitutional amendment supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups.\par \par \uc2 Conservative activists had worked for years to elect and support anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proponents to the Legislature, enabling them to stand firm against outside pressure. But the narrow loss showed that they are vulnerable even in the places where they have built some of their greatest power, as their national political influence weakens.\par \par \uc2 Several anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republican lawmakers made fiery speeches that framed the vote in spiritual terms. They equated abortions to Naziism and compared the repeal with the Sept. 11 attacks. They read graphic descriptions of later-term abortions. They quoted the Bible and made direct appeals to God from the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 Some saw the repeal not simply as a rejection of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  principles, but an explicit rejection of Christianity.\par \par \uc2 Two choked up. Senator J.D. Mesnard, who represents a suburban swing district, held up his phone and played a sonogram recording of his daughter's heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 ''If I vote yes, these will be fewer, these heart beatings,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 State Senator Anthony Kern, a Republican who was also among Arizona's fake electors indicted last week in an election-conspiracy case, said the Senate was betraying its opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and predicted that the vote would pave the way for acceptance of pedophilia.\par \par \uc2 ''This is innocent blood,'' he said. ''Why can't we show the nation we are pro-life? We will have the blessing of God over this state if we do that. Our only hope is Jesus Christ.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats, for their part, mostly stayed silent or made brief statements supporting repeal.\par \par \uc2 After the repeal passed, State Senator Eva Burch, a Democrat, called it ''one step in the right direction'' and quickly pivoted to focus on the coming fight over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Burch became a particularly visible face of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights movement in Arizona this year after she made an emotional floor speech to announce she needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end a nonviable pregnancy. She criticized the two Republican defectors for their past votes on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues but said, ''They stood with us,'' to overturn the 1864 ban.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm grateful for that,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Legislators had tried twice to force a repeal bill to a vote in the Republican-controlled state Legislature, only to be blocked by conservative lawmakers. In tense scenes inside the State Capitol, Democratic lawmakers shouted ''Shame!'' at Republicans, and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists filled the chambers with prayers to uphold the law.\par \par \uc2 Then last week, three Republican members of the House joined with every Democrat in the chamber and voted to repeal the 1864 ban, sending it to the Senate for final approval.\par \par \uc2 Before the vote on Wednesday, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists gathered outside the Capitol in a last-ditch effort to urge lawmakers to reconsider. They prayed under a tree, read scripture over a loudspeaker and argued with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 Amirrah Coronado, 17, took the morning off from her high school classes, put on a light pink T-shirt and drove to the Capitol with her mother and siblings to support the repeal effort. As she walked toward the sun-splashed plaza, a woman yelled at her, ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is murder!''\par \par \uc2 ''I know how to speak,'' Ms. Coronado said as an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist made a case that Arizona needed stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. ''This law -- it's from when slavery was here.''\par \par \uc2 In another corner of the plaza, Marisol Olivia Valenzuela faced off with a cluster of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrators from Apologia Church, a Phoenix congregation that supports so-called ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolition'' that would criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception as homicide.\par \par \uc2 ''It's murder,'' Charlie Casteel, 16, told Ms. Olivia Valenzuela. She was not having it.\par \par \uc2 ''You're standing here as a male, but you will never have to make that decision,'' she said. ''I am totally pro-life, but why can't we meet in the middle? Government shouldn't govern our bodies. Government has nothing to do with this.''\par \par \uc2 After the Senate vote, Representative Nancy Gutierrez, a Democrat and the House minority whip, said she was excited that they ''finally'' got the bill to the governor's desk. ''It will absolutely save lives,'' she said. ''However, we are not finished. We still have an initiative to get on the November ballot that will codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in our Arizona Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 Ada Martin, 55, who owns her own educational consulting company and is on the board of Planned Parenthood Advocates of Arizona, heard the news of the repeal as she was celebrating her birthday. It was a positive development, she said, but noted the three-month lag time in which the ban could be in effect.\par \par \uc2 Passing the ballot measure in November is still her primary goal, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''People tend to have a perception about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but as far as I am concerned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is health care, and the communities that are most adversely affected, regardless of religious influence, are communities of color,'' she said. ''I am just worried about the ability for those individuals to make choices about their bodies.''\par \par \uc2 Shawnna Bolick, one of the two Arizona G.O.P. state senators who joined with Democrats to force the repeal bill to a vote. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MATT YORK/ASSOCIATED PRESS) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A1, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240502ek520003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
