\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Florida's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rule Will Cut Off a Major Access Point\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state has dozens of clinics that serve tens of thousands of women a year, including from across the Southeast. The six-week ban will require most to travel much farther.\par \par \uc2 Florida has long played a significant role in the American \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape, with dozens of clinics providing the procedure to tens of thousands of residents a year while also taking in patients from across the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 That era will end, at least for now, on Wednesday, when a ban on most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy will take effect. The strict new law will replace a 15-week ban and require most Floridians and other Southerners seeking the procedure to travel to Virginia or farther.\par \par \uc2 Almost every other state in the region banned or sharply restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022; many had few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers even before the ruling. North Carolina still allows abortions up to 12 weeks, but with a 72-hour waiting period that makes it a less practical option for out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 ''The surrounding states have been desperate to find a place to go within a reasonable distance,'' said Kelly Flynn, the president and chief executive of A Woman's Choice, a network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, including one in Jacksonville, Fla., ''and we have been that place.''\par \par \uc2 Instead of the number of abortions in Florida decreasing after Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed the 15-week ban into law in April 2022, as proponents had hoped, it went up because more patients were coming from Southern states with more restrictions or near-total bans.\par \par \uc2 Florida, the third-largest state by population, has about 50 clinics and last year provided some 84,000 abortions; nearly 8,000 of them were for women from outside the state. Until July 2022, Florida allowed abortions until about 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't want to be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tourism destination,'' Mr. DeSantis, a Republican, said last year.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers and Mr. DeSantis approved the six-week ban in April 2023, when the governor was preparing to run for president. His message to Republican primary voters focused on how he had reshaped Florida's political identity, turning it from a swing state to a beacon of right-wing policy. His campaign failed, but the policies remained.\par \par \uc2 The six-week ban was conditioned on the Florida Supreme Court first upholding the 15-week ban, which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups had challenged. The conservative court did so on April 1, starting a 30-day countdown for the six-week ban to become law.\par \par \uc2 The new law provides exceptions for abortions to take place up to 15 weeks for pregnancies that result from rape, incest or human trafficking, though women would be required to show documentation such as a restraining order, medical record or police report. There are also exceptions to save the life of the woman and, before the third trimester, for fatal fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 The law also prohibits doctors from prescribing medication abortions through telehealth and dispensing the pills by mail. Medical professionals who violate any part of the ban could face criminal penalties.\par \par \uc2 Many women do not realize that they are pregnant by the sixth week, so backers of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say the new law will represent a near-total prohibition -- exactly the shutdown of access that they worried would happen when Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Florida, many Southern states had few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics left even before that ruling. When Texas, the nation's second most populous state, banned the procedure at six weeks in 2021, it had about 20 clinics that provided about 50,000 abortions a year, significantly fewer than Florida.\par \par \uc2 The six-week ban is ''going to protect thousands of unborn children,'' said John Stemberger, the president of Liberty Counsel Action, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobbying group.\par \par \uc2 He said Florida has almost 200 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancy centers, which sometimes provide pregnant women with ultrasounds and baby supplies. That is about four times the number of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, which are mostly clustered around big cities. Mr. Stemberger said he has worked with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to expand the centers' capacities.\par \par \uc2 ''There's going to be an increased demand for their services,'' he said. ''We want to communicate to mothers who are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -minded that there are options.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and nonprofit funds that help women pay for abortions in Florida have spent the last year preparing for this moment.\par \par \uc2 Between 2018 and 2023, about 60 percent of abortions in Florida happened after six weeks of pregnancy, according to state data.\par \par \uc2 Clinics are scheduling ultrasounds earlier and ramping up other health care services to try to stay open. Funds are training volunteers to plan travel for patients to Illinois, Virginia, Maryland and Washington, D.C. (North Carolina is closer but its waiting period to get the procedure makes it a less feasible destination.)\par \par \uc2 All of the changes could prove temporary: A ballot measure in November will ask Florida voters whether to amend the constitution to allow abortions until about 24 weeks. It will require more than 60 percent support to pass, a high threshold, and it would not take effect until January, assuming that lawmakers or anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups did not challenge it in court.\par \par \uc2 For now, Florida will have to adjust to being another Southern state where abortions are extremely rare.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  desert,'' said Michelle Quesada, a spokeswoman for Planned Parenthood of South, East and North Florida, which operates eight clinics in the state. (Planned Parenthood of Southwest and Central Florida operates another nine clinics.) ''It's going to be really challenging for patients.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Quesada said that after the Florida Supreme Court ruling clinics called patients scheduled to be seen in May and moved up their appointments. Out-of-state patients have been sent as far south as Miami -- more than 600 miles from the Georgia and Alabama borders -- because wait times in Tallahassee and Jacksonville, in the northern part of the state, have been two or three weeks, she added.\par \par \uc2 In the last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics have added other services, including prenatal care and adoption referrals. They have also seen an increase in patients requesting contraception, Ms. Quesada said, including vasectomies for men in their 30s and 40s.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Marian Sampson, an obstetrician and gynecologist who provides abortions for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's clinics in Fort Myers and Naples, on Florida's Gulf Coast, said that over the past two weeks, ''pretty much every appointment slot is double booked every day.''\par \par \uc2 Inevitably, clinics will be far less busy under the six-week ban, said Nikki Madsen, the co-executive director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Network, a national association of independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that had 36 members in Florida as of last year.\par \par \uc2 ''The number of patients those clinics will see will drastically plummet,'' she said. ''When a clinic closes, it's incredibly difficult to reopen.''\par \par \uc2 Clinics and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds said that, unlike in the months after Roe was overturned, they have not seen an uptick in contributions since the ruling allowing the six-week ban. That is most likely because donors are focused on funding the November ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 ''While that is a positive thing if it passes, there's a lot of time between May 1 and January when that would go into effect,'' said McKenna Kelley, who is on the board of the Tampa Bay \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund. ''We are doing the work day in and day out.''\par \par \uc2 Not only will it be more expensive for funds to help patients now that they have to also pay for their travel, but some people may find the plane travel that can be required overwhelming, said Jessica Hatem, the executive director of the Emergency Medical Assistance \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund in West Palm Beach. The fund was founded in 1972, pre-Roe, to help women in Florida go to New York for legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''When we are sending people on a plane, generally it's their first time flying,'' she said. ''When we're sending Southerners to the North in the middle of winter, people don't have coats.''\par \par \uc2 It is hard to predict just how much the number of abortions performed in Florida will drop after Wednesday, said \uc2 Isaac Maddow-Zimet\uc2 , a data scientist at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Researchers found that abortions declined by about 50 percent in Texas and Georgia after those states' six-week bans took effect. South Carolina had a much steeper drop-off -- about 71 percent, he said -- in part because so many women had been traveling there for abortions from other states. The South Carolina Supreme Court upheld a six-week ban law in August.\par \par \uc2 ''Every state,'' Mr. Maddow-Zimet said, ''has ripple effects through every other state.''\par \par \uc2 Kelly Flynn, president and chief executive of A Woman's Choice, a network of \uc2  clinics, including one in Jacksonville. A six-week ban starts Wednesday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AGNES LOPEZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Jessica Hatem, the director of the Emergency Medical Assistance \uc2  Fund in West Palm Beach, which helps people travel for abortions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GESI SCHILLING FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Gov. Ron DeSantis has said he doesn't want Florida to be an ''\uc2  tourism'' destination, as many neighboring states have few, if any, clinics. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOHN RAOUX/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240430ek4u0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
