\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Taking Heat For Voting To Repeal  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Healy \par \uc2 1,256 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On social media, Arizona lawmakers are accused of being baby killers, cowards and traitors.\par \par \uc2 State Representative Matt Gress, a Republican in a moderate slice of Phoenix, was in line at his neighborhood coffee shop on Thursday when a customer stopped and thanked him for voting to repeal an 1864 law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 ''I know you're taking some heat,'' he told Mr. Gress.\par \par \uc2 More than some.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the repeal bill squeaked through the Arizona House on Wednesday with support from every Democrat, as well as Mr. Gress and two other Republicans, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists denounced Mr. Gress on social media as a baby killer, coward and traitor. The Republican House speaker booted Mr. Gress off a spending committee. And some Democrats dismissed his stance as a bid to appease swing voters furious over the ban during an election year.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on Thursday, Mr. Gress said that he was trying to chart a middle path through a wrenching debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has consumed Arizona politics in the two weeks since the State Supreme Court revived the Civil War-era ban.\par \par \uc2 ''There are extremes on both ends here,'' he said. ''To go from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being legal and constitutionally protected to nearly a complete ban overnight is not something that the electorate is going to be OK with.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gress, 35, a former teacher and school-board member, worked as a budget director under Arizona's previous governor, the Republican \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 . He was first elected in 2022 to represent a swath of Phoenix and Scottsdale that spreads from middle-class neighborhoods through strip malls, desert parks and wealthy gated communities.\par \par \uc2 He speaks with the measured cadences of someone who has appeared on plenty of news programs, and had focused his attention on homelessness and teacher pay before \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  erupted into an all-consuming legislative battle.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, some voters in Mr. Gress's district praised him for helping to get the repeal bill through the House. Josh Offenhartz, a 36-year-old lawyer, said he believed that life began at conception, but agreed with former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's position that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be safe, legal and rare -- not banned altogether.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't believe a draconian law from the 19th century should tell us what to do today,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 But Diana and Marco Collins, a retired couple who recently moved to the Phoenix area, said they were disgusted by the repeal vote and upset with the Republicans who sided with Democrats. As devoted Christians, they said they viewed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as genocide.\par \par \uc2 ''It's about my faith,'' Ms. Collins, 56, said. ''We've totally put God out.''\par \par \uc2 The State Senate is expected to vote on repealing the 1864 ban on Wednesday, and lawmakers say the bill appears to have enough Republican support to pass. Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat, has been urging lawmakers to pass the repeal and said that she would sign it.\par \par \uc2 For years, Democrats said, Republican lawmakers in Arizona steadfastly opposed their efforts to undo the 1864 ban. Most Republicans voted against the repeal on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But the political ground shifted this month after the Arizona Supreme Court upheld the 1864 ban.\par \par \uc2 A handful of Arizona Republicans quickly put out statements criticizing the ruling, worried about the blowback from voters who called it archaic and extreme. Former President Donald J. Trump, who has taken credit for the overturning of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , urged Arizona's legislators to repeal the ban. So did Kari Lake, a Republican Senate candidate and Trump ally who once called it a ''great law.''\par \par \uc2 The law outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception except to save the mother's life, and makes no exceptions in cases of rape or incest. It has been on hold since the court's decision and is not expected to take effect until at least June.\par \par \uc2 The fractures within the Republican Party over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were on display on Wednesday as Mr. Gress stood up to call for a vote on a Democratic legislator's bill to repeal the 1864 law. As he started to speak, Representative Jacqueline Parker, a member of the ultraconservative Arizona Freedom Caucus, yelled out ''Point of order!'' and tried to scuttle the vote on procedural grounds.\par \par \uc2 Watching it all were hundreds of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists from churches and advocacy groups who thronged to the Capitol to pressure legislators to reject the repeal effort. Outside, they waved posters with images of fetuses on them and handed out models of fetuses. Inside the House chamber, they quietly prayed and lifted their hands. Many filed out before the final vote was tallied.\par \par \uc2 Some Arizona Republicans who broke with their party said they had worried that leaving the 1864 ban in place would supercharge voter support for a ballot measure to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections to the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Organizers say they have already collected more than 500,000 signatures -- more than the 384,000 they need to get on the ballot. Cheryl Bruce, manager of the Arizona for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access campaign, said that donations and enthusiasm from volunteers and voters spiked after the court upheld the 1864 ban.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists' measure would prevent Arizona from restricting abortions before fetal viability, and allow for abortions after viability if they were necessary to protect a patient's ''life or physical or mental health.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Tim Dunn, a Republican from the farming county of Yuma, said he voted for a repeal in the hopes that doing so would complicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  options for voters in November.\par \par \uc2 If the 1864 law is repealed, lawmakers say that abortions in the state will be allowed through the 15th week of pregnancy under a partial ban passed by Republicans in 2022.\par \par \uc2 That 15-week ban has largely been in place since Roe v. Wade was overturned. Several Republicans have called the 15-week ban a reasonable middle ground, but critics say it sets an arbitrary cutoff and contains no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Republicans are also considering whether to offer their own competing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures to muddy the choice for voters, according to documents prepared by lawyers for House members.\par \par \uc2 ''Arizonans do want a choice,'' Mr. Dunn said. ''I'd like it to be lower than the 15 weeks, but we have to go back to something reasonable. Because we can't go to something extreme.''\par \par \uc2 Stacy Pearson, a Democratic political consultant in Phoenix, said Arizona voters would remain galvanized by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She said that the 1864 ban could still lead \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to shut down because the repeal would not take effect for 90 days.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think you can heal this wound, with female voters in particular,'' she said. ''Even if the repeal passes, Arizona's going to stay in a state of limbo. This isn't about politics. We're talking about women who are in need of health care. This was going to kill people.''\par \par \uc2 ''To go from \uc2  being legal and constitutionally protected to nearly a complete ban overnight is not something that the electorate is going to be OK with,'' Matt Gress, left, a Republican state representative, said after voting to repeal Arizona's 1864 \uc2  ban. Right, anti-\uc2  protesters outside the State Capitol. Below, the State Legislature on Wednesday, when its vote to repeal sent the bill to the State Senate. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ASH PONDERS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240427ek4r00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
