\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices Divided Over Abortions In an Emergency\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle \par \uc2 1,460 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The justices weighed whether a federal law aimed at protecting access to emergency medical care superseded Idaho's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court appeared sharply divided on Wednesday over whether federal law should allow doctors to perform emergency abortions in states with near-total bans on the procedure, in a case that could determine access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in emergency rooms across the country.\par \par \uc2 The lively, two-hour argument focused on a clash between Idaho, whose law limits access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the life of the pregnant woman is in danger, and federal law. Questioning by the justices suggested a divide along ideological -- and possibly gender -- lines.\par \par \uc2 ''What Idaho is doing is waiting for women to wait and deteriorate and suffer the lifelong health consequences with no possible upside for the fetus,'' said Solicitor General Elizabeth B. Prelogar, arguing on behalf of the federal government. ''It just stacks tragedy upon tragedy.''\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  interjected that the current situation seemed untenable: ''It can't be the right standard of care to force somebody onto a helicopter.''\par \par \uc2 Although the collision between the two laws affects only those women who face dire medical complications during pregnancy, a broad decision could have implications for more than a dozen states that have enacted near-total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the court overturned a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 The dispute was the second time in less than a month that the Supreme Court has grappled with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is a potent reminder that even after Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  vowed in 2022 that the issue would return to elected representatives, it continues to make its way back to the court. In late March, the justices considered the availability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The federal law at issue, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, or EMTALA, enacted by Congress in 1986, mandates that hospitals receiving federal funds provide patients with stabilizing care.\par \par \uc2 Under Idaho's near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the procedure is illegal except in cases of incest, rape, some instances of nonviable pregnancies or when it is ''necessary to prevent the death of the pregnant woman.'' Doctors who perform abortions could face criminal penalties, prison time and loss of their licenses to practice medicine.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration maintains that the federal law conflicts with -- and should override -- Idaho's. Lawyers for the state contend that the administration has improperly interpreted the federal law in a way that would bypass state bans.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents assert the Biden administration is trying to use the federal law to turn hospitals into legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sites in states where the procedure is mostly banned.\par \par \uc2 Several of the conservative justices appeared skeptical of the federal government's argument that the decades-old law aimed at preventing ''patient dumping'' -- hospitals refusing to treat the poor and uninsured -- should override Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''How can you impose restrictions on what Idaho can criminalize simply because hospitals in Idaho have chosen to participate in Medicare?'' Justice Alito asked.\par \par \uc2 The three liberal justices pushed back strongly on the lawyer arguing for Idaho, Joshua N. Turner, broaching several examples of pregnant women facing severe complications that could leave them unable to have children or with debilitating injuries. They also cited recent reports that, since Idaho's ban went into effect, hospitals have flown several women to other states to receive emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , a conservative, joined the tough questioning.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  appeared skeptical of Mr. Turner's argument that the state's law allows for doctors to make a good-faith determination about whether a patient's life is in danger, describing a patient whose water broke early and required a hysterectomy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''She can no longer have children,'' Justice Sotomayor said. ''All right? You're telling me the doctor there couldn't have done the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier?''\par \par \uc2 ''Again, it goes back to whether a doctor can in good-faith medical judgment --'' Mr. Turner began.\par \par \uc2 ''That's a lot for the doctor to risk,'' Justice Sotomayor replied.\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett asked what would happen if a local prosecutor did not agree with a doctor's judgment that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Turner answered that ''it is very case by case,'' Justice Barrett joined in: ''I'm kind of shocked actually because I thought your own expert had said below that these kinds of cases were covered,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Even as Mr. Turner responded that such cases would be covered if a doctor acted in good faith, Justice Barrett continued to probe.\par \par \uc2 ''What if the prosecutor thought differently?'' she asked. ''What if the prosecutor thought, well, I don't think any good-faith doctor could draw that conclusion.''\par \par \uc2 ''That, your honor, is the nature of prosecutorial discretion, and it may result in a case,'' Mr. Turner said.\par \par \uc2 An extended exchange between Justice Alito and Ms. Prelogar raised a broader question about whether some of the conservative justices may be prepared to embrace the language of fetal personhood, that is, the notion that a fetus would have the same rights as a pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito, who had relied on the language of fetal personhood in writing the court's majority opinion in Dobbs, noted that ''one potentially very important phrase'' had not been mentioned -- the federal law's ''reference to the woman's quote, unquote, unborn child.''\par \par \uc2 ''Isn't that an odd phrase to put in a statute that imposes a mandate to perform abortions?'' Justice Alito asked. ''Have you ever seen an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statute that uses the phrase 'unborn child?'''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Prelogar responded that it made sense in terms of that federal law because it was an attempt to ensure that hospitals treated both a pregnant woman and the fetus in an emergency medical situation.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has relied on EMTALA as a narrow way to challenge state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 After the court overturned a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , near-total bans on the procedure swiftly took effect in some states, including in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 Once the Republican-controlled Legislature in the state passed the Defense of Life Act, which makes it a crime to perform or assist in performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Biden administration sued the state in August 2022, arguing that federal law should trump the state law when the two directly conflict.\par \par \uc2 The federal law specifies that a hospital must provide care to a person with an ''emergency medical condition.'' For pregnant women, the law states, that means when ''the absence of immediate medical attention could reasonably be expected'' to jeopardize ''the health of the woman or her unborn child.''\par \par \uc2 If a hospital breaks the federal law, it can be sued and potentially lose Medicare funding. The federal law also includes a provision that it will not pre-empt a state or local law unless ''the requirement directly conflicts with'' it.\par \par \uc2 But the state law imposes a prison sentence of up to five years if it is violated and can lead to the loss of a doctor's medical license.\par \par \uc2 A federal trial judge temporarily blocked the state's ban. Last fall, a three-judge panel from the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit put the ruling on hold and reinstated the ban. But that decision was ultimately overridden by an 11-member panel of the appeals court, which temporarily blocked Idaho's law as the appeal continued.\par \par \uc2 Outside the Supreme Court on Wednesday, demonstrators gathered at competing rallies.\par \par \uc2 Mylissa Farmer, 43, who said she was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Missouri and Kansas after her water broke at 17 weeks, spoke in support of the federal law's protections.\par \par \uc2 ''I just don't want anyone else to go through what I did,'' Ms. Farmer said. ''That's why I'm speaking out, because it's so wrong, and I don't see any kind of light.''\par \par \uc2 Just steps away, about a dozen anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrators raised signs that read ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  betrays women'' and ''Emergency rooms are not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.''\par \par \uc2 ''What this law would do is it essentially make our hospitals and emergency rooms \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics,'' Bethany Janzen, 30, the founder of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said of the federal law. ''And that's a problem.''\par \par \uc2 Aishvarya Kavi contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Aishvarya Kavi contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2  rights advocates at the Supreme Court on Wednesday. The Biden administration says a federal law should override Idaho's.; The crowd included health care workers. The case could determine access to \uc2  in emergency rooms across the country. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY HAIYUN JIANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) This article appeared in print on page A1, A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240425ek4p00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
