\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Planned Parenthood To Spend $10 Million\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maya King \par \uc2 880 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The party is banking on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as an issue to animate the state's competitive race for governor and, they hope, galvanize voters for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Aiming to bring the national fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to a key battleground state, a political arm of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  will spend $10 million on organizing efforts in North Carolina this year, its largest-ever investment in a single state.\par \par \uc2 The money will pay for digital advertisements, new field offices and a canvassing operation concentrated in a handful of swing counties. The leaders of the group, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  Votes South Atlantic, say it plans to knock on more than one million doors through the end of 2024 to talk to voters about preserving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Even though \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not explicitly on the ballot in North Carolina, Democrats are banking on the issue to animate the state's competitive race for governor and, they hope, to galvanize voters to boost President Biden in the process. A Democratic candidate hasn't won the state since 2008, and Mr. Biden lost it to former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in 2020 by just over a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina is also the last state in the Deep South where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal after six weeks of pregnancy, a fact Democrats have sought to highlight in underscoring the stakes for voters. The Republican nominee for governor, Lt. Gov. Mark Robinson, has endorsed a ban on all abortions once a fetal heartbeat is detected -- one he said he would push to pass if elected.\par \par \uc2 ''As we head into November, all eyes are on North Carolina because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the entire region will be determined by the results of this election,'' said Emily Thompson, the deputy director of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  Votes South Atlantic and spokeswoman for Planned Parenthood Votes, the group's national super PAC. ''Our success is absolutely critical this year to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and defend the bodily autonomy of every North Carolinian.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats and their allies are hopeful that Mr. Robinson's incendiary past comments about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , combined with the grass-roots energy around the issue that has benefited the party in recent elections, can help propel both Mr. Biden and \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2 , North Carolina's attorney general and the Democratic nominee for governor. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's political investment will also focus on 16 state House and Senate races, where Democrats need to win just one additional seat to break the G.O.P.'s supermajority.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Robinson has called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''murder'' and said that when a woman becomes pregnant, her body no longer belongs to her. In 2012, he said on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that he paid for his wife to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when she became pregnant while they were dating. He later called their decision to end the pregnancy a mistake.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Mike Lonergan, a spokesman for Mr. Robinson, called \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's organizing efforts part of the ''same old playbook'' that President Biden and Democrats have used to campaign on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , calling their stances ''extreme and out of step with our state's values.''\par \par \uc2 ''Lt. Gov. Mark Robinson is pro-life because of the painful and difficult experience he and his wife had, and his faith,'' Mr. Lonergan said. ''He's said that as governor he would sign a heartbeat bill with exceptions for rape, incest and when the life of the mother is in danger.'' He added that Mr. Robinson ''also wants to turn North Carolina into a destination state for life by doing more to support women that choose life, like improving our foster-care and adoption systems, and preserving access to I.V.F.''\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's community organizers have said that they will focus specifically on reaching suburban white women in the early months of their canvassing, arguing that they are among the most persuadable voters on the issue. The group has also hired organizers who will focus specifically on mobilizing young, Black and Latino voters. The first phase of its organizing began in earnest on Saturday, with a daylong training near downtown Raleigh that taught canvassers how to communicate to voters, in less-than-20-minute conversations, the ways that North Carolina's politics are part of the regional battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other groups, pointing to the governor's race and thin State House margins, have shifted their focus entirely to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messaging ahead of November's elections. Janice Robinson, the North Carolina director for Red, Wine and Blue, a nonpartisan group that mobilizes women in the suburbs, said her group had identified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a top issue. She said her organization hoped to recruit at least 5,000 more new members in the state this year, powered in large part by a message focused on protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel North Carolina is at the forefront of that fight'' she said. ''So we are prepared to fight with everything in us to stop what's happening in North Carolina and we will not give up.''\par \par \uc2 Ashley Farmer training volunteers at the Planned Parenthood South Atlantic field office in Raleigh, N.C., on Sunday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY VEASEY CONWAY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240424ek4o0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
