\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden Discusses  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights in Florida, Tying State's Ban to Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Nehamas and Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  one of its top issues, as polling shows it is one of the few subjects in which voters place more trust in President Biden than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Biden on Tuesday strongly condemned a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that will soon take effect in Florida, saying it would force women to endure ''unbearable pain and cruelty'' and blaming former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  for their suffering.\par \par \uc2 ''Let's be clear. There's one person responsible for this nightmare,'' Mr. Biden said in a speech to roughly 200 supporters at a community college gymnasium in Tampa. ''And he's acknowledged it and he brags about it: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a top issue, as polling shows it is one of the few subjects in which voters place more trust in Mr. Biden than Mr. Trump. Democrats have sought to tie Mr. Trump to laws like the one in Florida, labeling them ''Trump \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans'' and arguing that the former president would seek to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  if he regains the White House.\par \par \uc2 Throughout his brief remarks, Mr. Biden signaled his intention to make the election a referendum on Mr. Trump's first term as much as his own.\par \par \uc2 ''Now in America today in 2024, women have fewer rights than their mothers and grandmothers had -- because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 While the president campaigned on Mr. Trump's home turf, Mr. Trump had spent the day in a Manhattan courtroom, where prosecutors laid out what they called the ''Trump Tower conspiracy'' -- an effort by Mr. Trump and his allies to conceal negative or unflattering stories, including a porn star's claim of a sexual encounter with Mr. Trump, during the 2016 campaign. Mr. Trump faces 34 felony counts of falsifying business records to hide a payment to the porn star, Stormy Daniels. Mr. Trump has denied the claim of a sexual encounter, and has pleaded not guilty to the charges in the case.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has said little about the criminal charges Mr. Trump faces. But his campaign aides believe the contrast between Mr. Trump sitting at the defendant's table and Mr. Biden campaigning will resonate with voters.\par \par \uc2 His speech in Florida was an attempt to sharpen his national message on a crucial issue for his re-election campaign even in a state that he may not win. Although Florida has voted reliably for Republicans in recent elections, Mr. Biden's team has expressed optimism that the state could be in play this year. They point to the fact that in November, Floridians will vote on a ballot initiative that would guarantee access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''before viability,'' or at about 24 weeks, and overturn the six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 While the vote could motivate liberal and independent voters to come to the polls, Mr. Biden would have to invest heavily in Florida to defeat Mr. Trump, which his campaign has not yet done.\par \par \uc2 ''The idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has the state in the bag could not be further from the truth,'' said Michael Tyler, the Biden campaign's communications director. ''He owns not only the state of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights across the country, but he owns the restriction that we're seeing play out in Florida. And so yes, that means there's an opportunity for us.''\par \par \uc2 Nationally, Democrats have been energized after an Arizona court upheld a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in that state, arguing that it illustrates the stakes of electing Republicans. They have said that Mr. Trump is responsible for the restrictions in Florida, Arizona and other states that have imposed bans since Supreme Court justices appointed by the former president helped overturn Roe v. Wade. ''\uc2 Trump\uc2  did this'' has become a frequent messaging slogan from the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''It's going to absolutely turn people out,'' said Whitney Fox, a Democrat seeking to flip a Republican-held congressional seat in the Tampa area.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has criticized the bans in Florida and Arizona but also said that decisions about the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left up to the states. He has said that he no longer supports a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, reversing his previous position, and he has blamed Republican losses in recent elections on stringent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban last year as he courted right-wing voters in his failed bid for the Republican presidential nomination. The Florida Supreme Court upheld the ban this month. It goes into effect on May 1, one week after Mr. Biden's speech in Tampa. Speaking in Naples, Fla., just before the president took the stage, Mr. DeSantis said that ''Floridians are not buying what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is selling and in November, we're going to play an instrumental role in sending him back to Delaware where he belongs.''\par \par \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2 , a Democratic former congresswoman from Miami seeking to challenge Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , a Republican, said the Biden campaign should not give up on the nation's third-largest state.\par \par \uc2 ''Florida is a purple state,'' Ms. Mucarsel-Powell said. ''It's an independent state.''\par \par \uc2 Competing in Florida will be difficult for Democrats, even with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a motivating factor. Mr. Biden seemed to acknowledge that when he addressed Ms. Mucarsel-Powell in the audience, joking that he would ''campaign for you or against you, whatever would help the most.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump handily carried the state in 2020. Mr. DeSantis won overwhelmingly in his re-election bid two years later. And the number of Republican voters in the state has skyrocketed since Mr. Trump entered politics, overtaking the number of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The state is also extremely expensive to advertise in. Although the Biden campaign has a significant financial advantage over Mr. Trump's operation, it has not spent heavily in Florida compared with the major battlegrounds. Campaign aides have been vague about how much they plan to spend in the state.\par \par \uc2 ''The president is traveling there,'' Mr. Tyler said when asked how much the campaign would invest in Florida. ''We've got staff on the ground. You've seen our paid investments begin to pop up in the state of Florida. It is one of the pathways that we have to 270 electoral votes, and we're going to take it very, very seriously.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats nationwide have had unexpected success protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the ballot box since the demise of Roe, including in red states such as Kentucky, Kansas and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But unlike many other states, Florida requires more than 60 percent support for amendments to pass. To reach that high threshold, supporters of the constitutional amendment need to appeal to a broad coalition of voters, including independents and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''The focus right now cannot be on how this impacts electoral turnout,'' said Lauren Brenzel, director of the Yes on 4, the main group supporting the amendment. ''The focus is going to be on how this policy significantly harms and puts these women at risk.''\par \par \uc2 Alex Andrade, a Republican state representative who voted for the six-week ban, said Democrats were fooling themselves if they thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was going to ''fix things for them'' after losing Florida in 2016 and 2020 and seeing Mr. DeSantis flip traditional Democratic strongholds like Miami-Dade County.\par \par \uc2 ''Their political apparatus is clueless,'' Mr. Andrade said.\par \par \uc2 Florida voters have approved liberal-leaning ballot questions in recent elections while also electing Republican politicians. But those successful initiatives -- for medical marijuana, restoring felons' voting rights and raising the minimum wage -- have not faced the sort of organized opposition campaign that detractors of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure are expected to mount.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, a Florida resident, has not said how he will vote on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum.\par \par \uc2 Although Mr. Biden spoke on Tuesday under a banner that read ''Restore Roe,'' he offered few specifics about how he would accomplish that, beyond asking voters to deliver him a Democratic Congress.\par \par \uc2 An 81-year-old practicing Catholic, Mr. Biden can sometimes seem uncomfortable talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in detail, and often relies on surrogates to do so. His speech on Tuesday lasted roughly 13 minutes, a surprisingly rapid address given the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to his re-election.\par \par \uc2 In Tampa, he was introduced by Kaitlyn Joshua, a Louisiana woman who said she was denied medical care in that state when she experienced a miscarriage. Mr. Biden's campaign has featured the stories of several such women in its ads and events, and he brought up the criminal penalties that medical professionals could face in Florida.\par \par \uc2 ''It's criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care before women even know whether they're pregnant,'' Mr. Biden said. ''I mean, this is bizarre.''\par \par \uc2 President Biden in Tampa, Fla., on Tuesday. He condemned the state's six-week \uc2  ban signed by Gov. Ron DeSantis last year. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM BRENNER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240424ek4o0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
