\par\par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Republicans Are Telling on Themselves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamelle Bouie \par \uc2 1,045 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last Monday, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were best left to the states. ''The states,'' he said, ''will determine by vote or legislation or perhaps both, and whatever they decide must be the law of the land. In this case, the law of the state.''\par \par \uc2 The next day, as if answering a captain's call to fire from the line, the Republican-led Arizona Supreme Court, in an uncanny coincidence, revived a 160-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with no exceptions for either rape or incest. In a 4-to-2 decision, the court held that the 1864 ban was ''enforceable'' and not superseded by more recent legislation. Tasked with reconciling the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, some more permissive than others, the Arizona court chose the most restrictive option available -- one that ties the hands of Arizona residents with the restraints of yesteryear, forged by the settlers of a not-yet-state in the middle of the 19th century.\par \par \uc2 Beginning next week, a law once thought unenforceable will govern the lives of millions of people who had a say in neither its creation nor, for that matter, its resurrection.\par \par \uc2 A few thoughts come to mind here.\par \par \uc2 It does not escape my attention that this law owes its rebirth to an effort by \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , then the governor, to expand the Arizona Supreme Court's membership from five to seven justices. Ducey then stacked this enlarged court with reliable conservatives.\par \par \uc2 All four of the justices who were part of the majority in last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling were appointed by Ducey. One of them, Clint Bolick, is a longtime conservative legal activist and the author of ''David's Hammer: The Case for an Activist Judiciary.'' He is a type of judge the legal scholars Robert L. Tsai and Mary Ziegler call a ''movement jurist,'' defined as ''someone who is socially embedded in movement-aligned networks outside of the formal legal system and is willing to use a judge's tools of the trade in the service of a movement's goals.'' (Another Ducey-appointed justice, William G. Montgomery, once said that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was ''responsible for the greatest generational genocide known to man.'' He recused himself from this case.)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade was not inevitable, but once it was handed down, the Arizona Supreme Court was practically fated to move the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in a reactionary direction. (Which makes it striking that Ducey would express dismay: The ruling, he wrote on X, was ''not the outcome I would have preferred.'')\par \par \uc2 You can say the same for other political institutions in other states. Nearly everywhere Republicans hold power, they fight to rewire the institutions of government in the hope that they will then generate the desired result: more and greater Republican power.\par \par \uc2 And so we have the North Carolina Legislature gerrymandered to produce Republican majorities, the Ohio Legislature gerrymandered to produce Republican supermajorities, the Florida Legislature gerrymandered to produce Republican supermajorities and the Florida Supreme Court overhauled to secure and uphold Republican priorities.\par \par \uc2 The states' rights case for determining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access -- let the people decide -- falters on the fact that in many states, the people cannot shape their legislature to their liking. Packed and split into districts designed to preserve Republican control, voters cannot actually dislodge anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republican lawmakers. A pro-choice majority may exist, but only as a shadow: present but without substance in government.\par \par \uc2 When the demands of the living do begin to press against the will of Republican lawmakers or Republican jurists, they can respond, with the dead hand of the past. Not the past broadly constructed -- one attentive to the silences of those who were missing, excluded or never recorded in the first place -- but a narrow past, the main purpose of which is to extinguish new freedoms and forms of living.\par \par \uc2 The federal courts and the Arizona Supreme Court have conjured a past that smothers the right to bodily autonomy. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are also trying to conjure a past, in the form of the long-dormant Comstock Act, that gives government the power to regulate the sexual lives of its citizens. As Moira Donegan notes in a column for \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , ''Comstock has come to stand in, in the right-wing imagination, for a virtuous, hierarchically ordered past that can be restored in a sexually repressive and tyrannically misogynistic future.''\par \par \uc2 This effort may well fail, but the drive to leash the country to an imagined vision of a reactionary past should be seen as a silent confession of weakness. The same is true, for that matter, of the authoritarian dreams of the former president and his allies and acolytes.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives can win, of course. They have real institutional power. But it is important to understand that they are fighting from a position of social, cultural and even political weakness. Even that great champion of conservative electoral strength, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , has never won a popular majority.\par \par \uc2 To put it a bit differently: A confident political movement does not fight to dominate; it works to persuade. It does not curate a favorable electorate or frantically burrow itself into our countermajoritarian institutions; it competes for power on an even playing field, assured of its appeal and certain of its ability to win. It does not hide its agenda or shield its plans from public view; it believes in itself and its ideas.\par \par \uc2 In this context, Arizona is instructive. Conservatives may have gotten their desired result from the legislature and the courts. But there is still an election in November. And proponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say they have already collected enough signatures to put the issue on the ballot. Unlike their opponents, they are confident that the public is on their side.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY REBECCA NOBLE/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page SR3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240421ek4l0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
