\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Women Share  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Input Across TikTok\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Schmall \par \uc2 1,470 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ''Have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with me,'' a single mother from Brooklyn named Sunni says as she twirls around her kitchen to light jazzy piano, before walking TikTok viewers through the steps she took to end her pregnancy at home.\par \par \uc2 With states expanding restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the issue likely to be at the forefront of the presidential election, women are creating videos on social media describing their own abortions and sharing practical information on how to obtain one.\par \par \uc2 Sunni explained to viewers that she was craving information when she was planning her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''This is the video I was looking for,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The reaction to her video, which has been viewed more than 400,000 times and has drawn comments of both commiseration and condemnation, shows how deeply personal and divisive the issue remains in the run up to the November elections.\par \par \uc2 One viewer, a campaigner with the group Protect Life Michigan, remixed the video on the group's own TikTok account, criticizing Sunni for her lighthearted tone and for making the video at all.\par \par \uc2 ''I just don't understand how we are making a video, and we are laughing and joking about going through the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  process,'' the campaigner said.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade in 2022 led to a cascade of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions across large parts of the United States. Twenty-one states now ban or restrict the procedure earlier than the standard set by Roe.\par \par \uc2 In response, there has been an explosion of social media content related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- some of it overtly political, some informational and some testimonial as women seek answers, seek support, or simply seek to share.\par \par \uc2 The landscape for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is changing rapidly. Last month, the justices heard arguments over whether to curtail access to a widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, with a decision expected this June or July. This month, Arizona's Supreme Court upheld an 1864 law that bans nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has taken credit for a Supreme Court that overturned Roe v. Wade, but has since distanced himself from the idea of a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. President Biden, meanwhile, sees advantage from pinning the narrowing landscape for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Republicans.\par \par \uc2 With the laws in flux state by state, Sunni and others have made TikToks to explain how to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and have the procedure at home. In other videos on the site, women have grappled with their own experiences, expressing everything from relief to regret. These personal videos have become fodder for political campaigns, which have used them to argue either for an expansion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or for further restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Confused over where and what forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are allowed state to state, young people seeking to end their pregnancies are increasingly turning to social media for guidance, researchers have found.\par \par \uc2 ''The chaos and the confusion and the stigma is the point with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and targeted regulations,'' said Rebecca Nall, the founder of an online database that directs users to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  resources.\par \par \uc2 ''More and more people are going online with their most personal questions,'' she added, ''and more and more people are offering information.''\par \par \uc2 Before Roe v. Wade, desperate women called Jane, an underground \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  network, for advice on what to do about unwanted pregnancies. Later, campaigns encouraged women to talk about their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  openly.\par \par \uc2 With women now turning to TikTok for information and as a vehicle for self-expression, the app has also become a forum for discussion. On some videos, viewers posed practical questions about procuring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs or finding a provider. They shared fears of physical pain and anxieties over the logistical complexities of arranging one. Other viewers expressed regret for having had abortions.\par \par \uc2 Some voices were critical, faulting women for having abortions and for speaking openly about it, without remorse.\par \par \uc2 The women sharing their stories -- and the viewers who write to them asking for advice -- are engaging in conversations that could be at risk. Some states' attorneys general have expressed an appetite to prosecute those who ''aid and abet'' abortions, including those who provide information, and to subpoena online messages.\par \par \uc2 Sunni, 30, who asked that her full name not be used out of fear that she could be further targeted by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, said in an interview that she became interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  justice when she was pregnant with her daughter in 2021.\par \par \uc2 She had become active on TikTok and was alarmed to find videos of people recommending herbal remedies like parsley to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When she was pregnant last year, after experiencing a difficult childbirth the first time, she decided to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to share the experience with her followers.\par \par \uc2 With TikTok awash in activism from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners and proponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Sunni said she wanted to focus on the practicalities of a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most common form in the United States. That included the order that the mifepristone and misoprostol pills must be taken, and the creature comforts -- like Totino's frozen pizza -- she relied on to help with pain management and recovery.\par \par \uc2 ''It's something that so many people go through,'' she said in an interview. ''There are people walking around you going through this thing and until they feel normal and accepted, they're not going to be able to heal.''\par \par \uc2 The video she made received more than 1,000 comments. Sunni said she received hundreds of messages from girls and young women seeking direction on how to obtain the pills and manage pain.\par \par \uc2 ''You do have to navigate it,'' she said, ''and nobody shows you how.''\par \par \uc2 Another testimonial came from Mikaela Attu, a Canadian who said in an interview that she was shocked by the overturning of Roe v. Wade, particularly because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care was not difficult to access in Canada.\par \par \uc2 In a TikTok video, she took viewers along to multiple hospital visits near her home in Vancouver, from an ultrasound to confirm her pregnancy to a shot of her feet in stirrups at the beginning of a procedure to terminate it.\par \par \uc2 In another video, viewed 7.5 million times, Ms. Attu talked about the heartbreak of getting pregnant with a man she loved, but not being able to go through with it.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Attu and her husband plan to have children, she said, but she was dealing with mental health issues when she got pregnant last year and did not feel prepared to start a family.\par \par \uc2 ''I wanted to show that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is complicated,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Other women have made TikToks to express their grief over having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One viewer of another woman's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  video commented that it reminded her of the pain she endured as a 16-year-old, going through her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Desire\u233\'00\'E9 Dallagiacomo, 33, a writer and poet in California, recorded a video as she got ready for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointment.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm fine and stable,'' she told viewers, ''and I just don't want a child.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dallagiacomo, 33, said in an interview that she wanted to share her story, in part, to challenge the prevailing narratives about why people have abortions.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights increasingly targeted, what women share about their abortions on social media has come into focus.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys general in Texas, Alabama and Louisiana have indicated an interest in prosecuting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and other groups that coordinate them, creating uncertainty over whether those who share information online could be held liable.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a movement afoot to criminalize information,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who has written extensively about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In July, a teenager in Nebraska was charged with concealing a death, her aborted fetus, and sentenced to 90 days in jail. In the case, prosecutors subpoenaed \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages she had exchanged with her mother, in which the two discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 The case in Nebraska suggests the conversations that people have about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be used against them, Professor Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 ''In the post-Dobbs era, there's an interesting and tricky trade-off,'' she said, between sharing stories to destigmatize the experience ''and the fact that speaking out could create unintended legal risks.''\par \par \uc2 The specter of punishment for sharing information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was just one of the ways Ms. Dallagiacomo said she found her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  experience ''isolating.''\par \par \uc2 ''There is just so much keeping us from honestly telling our story,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 SUNNI, a single mother from Brooklyn who uses TikTok to explain how to get \uc2  pills. (PHOTOGRAPH BY PAOLA CHAPDELAINE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); DESIRE\u201\'00\'C9 DALLAGIACOMO, a writer and poet in California, in a video before an \uc2  appointment. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BRYAN MELTZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) This article appeared in print on page A1, A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240418ek4i0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
