\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Clinic's Patients Feel Fear and Anger After Arizona Court Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Healy and Caitlin O'Hara \par \uc2 1,594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leah found out she was five weeks pregnant on the same day that the Arizona Supreme Court upheld an 1864 law banning nearly all abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 The law is not expected to take effect until June, but Leah, 29, worried that the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics might be overwhelmed by an influx of patients or shut down abruptly. And she could not afford to take time off from her job installing bathroom showers to travel to another state for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 So on Saturday morning, she threaded past a handful of protesters waving signs that read ''You Shall Not Murder'' and checked in at the Acacia Women's Center in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 ''I might have taken a couple more weeks'' to consider her options, she said. ''But I kind of felt like my hands were tied.''\par \par \uc2 The court's ruling last week reinstated a Civil War-era law that outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of conception, which could have far-reaching consequences for women and has the potential to reshape the 2024 election. Inside the lobby of Acacia, the ruling felt deeply personal to Leah and other women, a decision that made them reluctant players in a series of national battles over contraception, in vitro fertilization and women's health.\par \par \uc2 The ruling set off outrage and political maneuvering. The state's Democratic lawmakers scrambled, but failed, to repeal the law, and lawyers on both sides are preparing for more battles over whether to implement it.\par \par \uc2 As the patients at Acacia scrolled through their phones and texted friends while waiting for their names to be called this weekend, they said the judges and politicians who supported banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not understand their lives, or why they had decided to get abortions.\par \par \uc2 In the decision, the justices said that because the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Roe v. Wade had now been overturned, nothing prevented Arizona from enforcing the 1864 law. They also said their job was to interpret two potentially conflicting state laws, not to make a policy judgment about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups argued that the 1864 ban -- which prohibits all abortions, including in cases of rape or incest, but makes an exception for ones that would save the mother's life -- had essentially been superseded by a 2022 law prohibiting abortions after 15 weeks. But Arizona never took the earlier law off the books, and the 15-week ban stressed that it was not repealing the 1864 law or creating any state right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The women at the clinic said it was already challenging to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Arizona. Patients have to consult with their doctors and have a sonogram at least 24 hours in advance, as well as listen to a state-mandated script describing the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and development of their fetus. Some of Arizona's seven \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics were already booked through the end of April, they said, and they worried that their window was closing fast.\par \par \uc2 Patients at Acacia raced to find out: Would their appointments be canceled? Where would they go if Arizona's clinics shut down? Would they have to continue their pregnancies?\par \par \uc2 ''It freaked me out,'' said Joanne, 29, who called the clinic the instant she heard about the ruling. ''It's terrifying. It's baffling that it would even be a reality.''\par \par \uc2 Most of the 10 women interviewed for this article asked to be identified only by their first names to protect their privacy or to avoid losing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Some came alone, and said they could not tell their parents they were pregnant. Others sat with their mothers or boyfriends. They counted out cash for the procedures -- $1,000 for a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and $750 for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- and made plans with family about how to get home and what they would order for dinner afterward.\par \par \uc2 Many said they could not understand why the Arizona Supreme Court, whose justices are all Republican appointees, would force them to live under a law written before women could vote or open bank accounts.\par \par \uc2 Some said they supported Arizona's existing 15-week restriction on abortions, but said the 1864 ban was too much.\par \par \uc2 Like 60 percent of women nationwide who receive abortions, Jordan, 29, already had a child. But she said she suffered such severe postpartum depression after having her son that she almost committed suicide. She worried she might not survive a second pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Esmerelda, 25, also already had a child, a 7-month-old daughter, but said it would be impossible for her to afford another one. She spends $1,000 a month for a babysitter, and said another baby would force her to quit her job and hurt her family's finances.\par \par \uc2 As she sat in the lobby, Esmerelda said she wanted the doctor to give her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills while they were still legal. She said she was worried about what would happen to women who need abortions once the 1864 law is implemented.\par \par \uc2 Like many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, Acacia has long been a battleground. Protesters with bullhorns yell at women to turn around and wave signs condemning its owner, Dr. Ronald Yunis, an obstetrician-gynecologist, as a baby killer.\par \par \uc2 ''We know he doesn't like us here,'' said Chad McDonald, 43, a construction worker who was part of the protest at the clinic on Saturday. ''These babies are human beings, just like a 4-year-old is a human being.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. McDonald said he had been disappointed that some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republicans in Arizona had recently changed course and called to scrap the 1864 law, including Kari Lake, who is running for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other conservatives, including the state's Freedom Caucus, have defended the ruling. Jake Warner, a lawyer with the conservative Christian group \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , said it would allow Arizona to ''fully protect life.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Yunis turns on the sprinklers and blares Nine Inch Nails outside the clinic to drown out the demonstrators' shouts. Protesters said he had poured ammonia at their feet. In 2019, he was arrested on a charge of pointing a gun at protesters as he drove away from the clinic. Dr. Yunis pleaded guilty to a charge of disorderly conduct and was dropped by Medicare, but he said he had been defending himself against increasingly aggressive protesters.\par \par \uc2 ''The guy was coming at my car,'' he said. ''How many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have been murdered in the last 20 years? There's a legitimate fear that someone running at your car after blocking your driveway might be meaning you harm.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Yunis and his small staff said the two years since Roe was overturned have been full of chaos and uncertainty. Now, some are bracing to be laid off if they can no longer perform abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 Irma Jo Fernandez-Leos signs in women at the front desk. These days, she also asks them whether they want to sign a petition to support a ballot measure that would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right in Arizona. She said a vast majority of patients tell her they are not registered to vote.\par \par \uc2 ''It frustrates me,'' she said. ''It's like, do you know how many of us it takes to make a difference?''\par \par \uc2 Some patients fly in from Texas, Florida and other states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and cannot vote in Arizona. Others are undocumented. And some, like Iris, 17, are too young to vote.\par \par \uc2 Iris said she learned she was pregnant three weeks ago. She had a son when she was 16, and she said she was already stretched to her limit as she tried to raise a 13-month-old, finish high school and work afternoon shifts at \uc2 Burger King\uc2 . She worried that having another child would make it impossible for her go to community college next year to study medical assisting.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not the right time,'' she said. ''I don't want to work at \uc2 Burger King\uc2  the rest of my life. I want to go to school.''\par \par \uc2 She did not know how she could squeeze another crib into the bedroom where she and her son sleep in her family's mobile home. So, on Friday morning, she sat in the clinic's lobby, bouncing her 13-month-old on her lap and waiting for her name to be called.\par \par \uc2 Her mother, Ruby, had taken the morning off from her job as a cleaner to accompany Iris to the clinic. She said she had religious misgivings about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but was forced to set them aside.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not allowed under God,'' Ruby said, ''but sometimes, it's necessary.''\par \par \uc2 Iris said she believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal, and that she would vote to support pro-choice candidates or Arizona's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot measure. But she turns 18 on Nov. 6, one day after the election.\par \par \uc2 Iris, 17, learned she was pregnant three weeks ago. She has a 13-month-old son and said she was already stretched to her limit.; Iris with her son in the waiting area of Acacia Women's Center in Phoenix last week, and getting medication before the \uc2 . She supports Arizona's \uc2 -rights candidates and the ballot measure, she said, but she turns 18 the day after the election.; Dr. Ronald Yunis talking to patients, including Irene, left, who traveled from Texas for care. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CAITLIN O'HARA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240416ek4g0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
