\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The Point\b0\par\par\uc2 764 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Will This Save Children's Lives?\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Kristof, Opinion columnist\par \par \uc2 Soon after the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist attacks on Israel, the Israeli defense minister vowed to impose a ''complete siege'' on Gaza. Since then, Israel has allowed some food and other supplies in, with tight restrictions. As a result, aid organizations have reported starvation, particularly in northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 This appears to have resulted in famine, according to \uc2 Samantha Power\uc2 , the administrator of the \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\uc2 . In testimony before Congress on Wednesday, Ms. Power suggested first that famine was imminent, then, when pressed, that it is already occurring in northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The official arbiter of famines, an international group of specialists, has not yet designated a famine underway in Gaza, but official declarations are based on lagging indicators. Ms. Power's statement should be taken as a sign of the general seriousness of the food crisis and the risks of widespread death.\par \par \uc2 Malnutrition is already killing children in Gaza and when an area is teetering on the edge of famine, deaths can escalate very rapidly.\par \par \uc2 Famines primarily kill children under the age of 5. I've covered hunger crises around the world, and the scenes are horrible to witness. Dying children are passive, expressionless, silent, not crying; the body uses every calorie to keep the major organs functioning.\par \par \uc2 What is unusual about Gaza is this hunger crisis is unfolding in a small, accessible area where 3,000 to 7,000 trucks are reportedly waiting at the border with food. \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  has accused Israel of using starvation as a weapon of war.\par \par \uc2 Israel has repeatedly denied that it obstructs aid. But in the past few days, after a threat by President Biden to put conditions on arms transfers, Israel has allowed far more trucks to enter Gaza. That aid also needs to be distributed, which requires a well-functioning infrastructure that is not now in place.\par \par \uc2 The importance of the word ''famine'' is that it can light a fire under international officials and groups to act urgently to save children's lives. The test of Ms. Power's warning is whether, for America and Israel alike, this actually leads to steps on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Arizona May Be Just Purple Enough for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Matter\par \par \uc2 Michelle Cottle, Opinion writer\par \par \uc2 Hear that? It's the sound of Republican politicians smacking their foreheads as yet another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related development complicates their 2024 election landscape.\par \par \uc2 This time, it's Arizona. Tuesday's decision by the State Supreme Court to uphold a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from 1864 is not going to do the party any favors. No exceptions for rape and incest? Tossing doctors in prison? That ought to win over some hearts and minds.\par \par \uc2 It also throws a wrench in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's weak efforts to defuse the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue by insisting he wants it left up to the states. I mean, with state laws like these ...\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  hope to put a measure on the ballot in November that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the Arizona Constitution. Organizers say they already have more than enough signatures to qualify. This promises to put Arizona in a position very similar to the one in which Florida finds itself.\par \par \uc2 But Arizona ain't Florida. It is swingier. More than a third of its voters are not registered with any political party, and the electorate prides itself on being mavericky. This \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  dust-up could cause Republicans up and down the state ballot some serious heartburn.\par \par \uc2 Already, Kari Lake, the MAGA front-runner in the Republican Senate primary, has been compelled to execute an awkward flip-flop. Having proclaimed the 1864 ban a ''great law'' during her 2022 run for governor, she is now running away from it as fast as she can.\par \par \uc2 And the presidential race? For months, Mr. Trump has been running some four to five points ahead of President Biden in Arizona. Yes, November is several political eternities away. But those aren't comforting numbers for Democrats, especially with the border crisis, an urgent concern for Arizonans, shaping up to be a prime campaign cudgel for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments will not fix the border mess -- or erase the general disappointment many voters feel toward Mr. Biden. But they could stick in the craws of plenty of Arizona mavericks, making them think twice about giving their votes to the guy who boasts of killing Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MOHAMMED SALEM/REUTERS; ALLISON DINNER/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) This article appeared in print on page SR5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240414ek4e0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
