\par\par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Trump Is Not Done With  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamelle Bouie \par \uc2 1,178 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Donald Trump does not speak from conviction. He does not speak from belief or at least any belief other than self-obsession. He certainly does not speak from anything we might recognize as reason; when he's holding forth at a microphone, even the most careful students of Trump the rhetorician will struggle to find the light of complex thought.\par \par \uc2 You should think of Trump instead as a purely instrumental speaker. It does not matter to him whether a statement is true or false. It does not matter if one statement contradicts another in the same speech or in the same paragraph or in the same sentence. What matters to Trump is whether the words serve the purpose at hand. He will say anything if it's what he feels an audience wants to hear or if it moves him one step closer to a personal or political goal.\par \par \uc2 Trump's fundamental lack of interest in the truth value of his words is the only context that matters for his comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday morning. In a direct-to-camera statement on Truth Social, the former president told his audience that he does not support a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''My view is now that we have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  where everybody wanted it from a legal standpoint,'' Trump said. ''The states will determine by vote or legislation or perhaps both, and whatever they decide must be the law of the land. In this case, the law of the state.''\par \par \uc2 It should be said that this position is itself a strikingly conservative departure from mainstream thinking. Nearly two-thirds of Americans support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most or all cases -- the constitutional status quo under Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey. To leave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights up to the states is, as we've seen since the Supreme Court's decision two years ago in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, to give state legislatures broad discretion to restrict and limit the bodily autonomy of anyone within their borders.\par \par \uc2 Compared with the mounting push from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to ban the procedure nationwide, however, Trump's stance is designed to look almost moderate. And if you were born yesterday, you could even say that Trump was beginning his pivot to the center, to blur the difference on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  between himself and other Republicans. If he can persuade skeptical voters that he's not a \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  or a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , then he's one step closer to a second term.\par \par \uc2 But there's no reason to take Trump's words at face value. Trump is aware, like virtually everyone who follows American politics, that Republicans are dangerously vulnerable on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The Biden campaign has already begun airing ads that blame Trump directly for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. He knows that he needs to neutralize this issue as much as possible without alienating his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  followers. When it looked, for example, as though support for a 15-week ban would do the trick, Trump floated support for a 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 It does not require any particular powers of political analysis to see that Monday's statement is a ploy -- and an obvious one at that. What does Trump say immediately before giving his states' rights position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? He praises himself for ending Roe: ''Many people have asked me what my position is on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, especially since I was proudly the person responsible for the ending of something that all legal scholars, both sides, wanted and, in fact, demanded be ended: Roe v. Wade.'' This claim, that all sides wanted an end to Roe, is a total fabrication, but it serves to give Trump cover as he tries to be all things to all people.\par \par \uc2 Later in the video, as if to emphasize his responsibility for the end of Roe, Trump applauds the Dobbs majority, by name no less: ''I want to thank the six justices -- Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , incredible people -- for having the courage to allow this long-term, hard-fought battle to finally end.''\par \par \uc2 Trump is saying what he thinks his audience -- in the public and in the press -- wants to hear. He's trying to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the rearview mirror, to treat it as a settled fact that he wants a less strident approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The truth of the matter is that given a second term in office, Trump and his allies will do everything in their power to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, with or without a Republican majority in Congress. Recall that in his 2016 campaign, Trump said that there had to be ''some form'' of punishment for women who had abortions. Later, as president, he backed a House bill that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists have not been shy about their plan to use the 1873 Comstock Act, an anti-obscenity law, as legal authority for executive actions to limit abortions throughout the country, in blue states as well as red ones.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  believe that under the act -- whose enforcement produced a political and legal backlash so fierce that it made the law a dead letter for most of the 20th century -- the president has the power to prohibit the mailing, sale, advertisement or distribution of any drug or instrument that could be used to cause or perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If you're not inclined to put Trump's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments in the context of his habitual disregard for the truth, then you should at least put them in the context of his political coalition, which is dominated by forces and constituencies that want nothing less than the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the constitutional protection of fetal life and state regulation of bodily autonomy for the sake of patriarchal gender norms.\par \par \uc2 Trump embraced this movement and its demands as president, and there's every reason to believe he'll do the same with another four years in office. Put another way: When has Trump ever bucked anyone who defends him, supports him and showers him with praise?\par \par \uc2 We do not have to speculate about Trump's relationship to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  as leader of the Republican Party. He is, after all, a former president of the United States. We already know what he wants, what he'll do and what he'll sign. Trump landed a major blow against legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his first term. If given a second, he will land another.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY JENNY HUESTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page SR3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240414ek4e00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
