\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans Spread, Fractures Begin Emerging Among Republican Women\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,564 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Across the country, fractures are emerging among conservative and centrist women, as they confront a steady drumbeat of new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and court rulings.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Gau, a self-described ''reasonable Republican'' in Mesa, Ariz., is conflicted about many things that her party promotes. But she knows exactly what she thinks about Arizona's new -- or rather, very old -- Civil War-era \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. And about the idea that Republicans like her might be happy with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 ''Are you nuts?'' she said, adding that she was frustrated with the ban and Republican politicians inserting themselves into women's health choices.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Gau, 52, said she probably would not have chosen an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for herself. But she said she would never judge a woman for making her own decision. ''It is not a cut and dry line,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, fractures are emerging among conservative and centrist Republican women, as they confront an unrelenting drumbeat of new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and court rulings. For years, the party's message was simple and broad: Republicans oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Its politicians rarely dove into the specifics of what the position meant for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues like miscarriage, medical emergencies and fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 Now, those complicated realities are everywhere. In Alabama, the State Supreme Court ruled that frozen embryos could be considered children, raising concerns over future access to in vitro fertilization procedures. In Florida, women are preparing for a new six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to soon go into effect.\par \par \uc2 Nowhere is the conversation more intense this week than in Arizona, a key battleground state in the 2024 election. On Tuesday, the State Supreme Court ruled 4 to 2 in favor of reinstating an 1864 law banning all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of conception, except to save the life of the mother. It made no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The ruling came just one day after former President Donald J. Trump, who rose to power in large part through an alliance with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, said he believed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 Conversations with Republican women revealed a spectrum of views about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its effect on their political identities as they looked ahead to November.\par \par \uc2 Some disagree with the bans but say the new laws are not shaking their support for Mr. Trump and Republican candidates. Another group, the most committed opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , see the bans as victories, thanks in part to Mr. Trump, and as a moral call to action to further advance their cause. And some self-described Republicans who backed Mr. Biden in 2020 say the bans have solidified their support for his re-election.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Gau, who works in education policy, the new law challenged the long-held conservative tenet that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should be returned to the states. Leaving some decisions up to the states is not necessarily bad, she said, but some issues need consistency over time, and even across states. ''This is one of those issues.''\par \par \uc2 She was frustrated with politicians, especially Republicans, who treated \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  like just another ''red meat'' political issue. The ruling seemed to her like another reminder of how her party had betrayed her values. When she casts her vote, she plans to vote for President Biden, as she did in 2020.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Republicans continue to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . About 60 percent of Republicans oppose a law that would guarantee a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and half support banning the use of mifepristone, a common medication used in terminating pregnancies, according to a recent poll by KFF, a nonprofit health policy organization. And about two in three Republican women say they trust Mr. Trump to move \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the right direction, according to KFF.\par \par \uc2 But many women's views are more nuanced than a broad survey can capture. And there is a portion of conservative women whose views are evolving in real time, in response to changes that have swept the nation since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 According to Tresa Undem, a public opinion researcher who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the share of Republican women who believe their party's views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are ''too extreme'' increased in February to 39 percent, from 22 percent in June 2022 before the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an issue has quickly become tied with health, medicine, safety and security, on top of bodily autonomy, Ms. Undem said. A majority of Republican women also now cite ''women's rights'' as an ''extremely or very important issue'' in their vote, up from 31 percent, a phrase Ms. Undem says is closely associated with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''Becoming pregnant has become an even scarier prospect than it already was,'' she said. ''Everyone knows someone who has had a complication in pregnancy, so this issue is far-reaching.''\par \par \uc2 Still, a vocal contingent of the Republican Party remains committed to opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For these women, the fall of Roe was the beginning, not the end, of their efforts to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Trussell, the chair of Arizona Right to Life, was elated by the State Supreme Court's decision and furious that Arizona's attorney general, Kris Mayes, a Democrat, had called the ruling ''an existential crisis'' for residents.\par \par \uc2 ''We have an attorney general who is saying she will not enforce the law, which is terrifying,'' Ms. Trussell said. ''If you don't have an attorney general enforcing the law, that is anarchy.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Trussell said her group had gained fresh local momentum in the past couple of years and was working with Students for Life, a national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, to push residents to oppose a ballot initiative that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the State Constitution, a measure that has received a groundswell of support from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Yet surveys are also clear that Republicans generally feel less politically motivated by the issue than Democrats, a reversal from the mobilizing power that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had before Roe was overturned. Even some women who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are more politically motivated by a broader set of social issues this year.\par \par \uc2 In Scottsdale, Ariz., Kimberly Miller, 61, who founded Arizona Women of Action, a group of Christian women that aims to ''protect kids and restore schools,'' said she supported the State Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''To the people who want to 'keep religion out of it,' just realize that most every law is based on a moral premise,'' she said in a statement. ''We believe that every single life is precious, and we commit ourselves to saving lives rather than ending them.''\par \par \uc2 But while Ms. Miller is working to defeat the Arizona ballot initiative, the women in her network are particularly mobilized against the ''politics of using race and gender as means of creating division and activism,'' she said. ''Parents want nonpolitical academics.''\par \par \uc2 The issue of fertility treatments presented new challenges for Republicans this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 In Lehigh County, Pa., Lorraine Mory, 70, had always voted Republican as a single-issue voter, with that issue being \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said. But now she says she can't imagine voting for a Republican ever again.\par \par \uc2 Her evolution on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  took time, and happened through conversations she had with her daughter, an obstetrician-gynecologist, throughout the Trump presidency. Ms. Mory had supported Mr. Trump's Supreme Court nominees, until they overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''I wouldn't have my grandchildren if it weren't for I.V.F.,'' she said. ''I am a very strong Christian, I think that is why the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue was such a black and white thing for me before. Now I consider myself pro-choice.''\par \par \uc2 For other Republican women who back some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the rise of new restrictions hasn't been significant enough to shake their support of Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 On a sunny day last August at the Iowa State Fairgrounds in Des Moines, just weeks after the governor signed a law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state after six weeks, Shirley Grandstaff, 60, explained why she believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be all-but-unregulated by the government.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not your judge and jury,'' said Ms. Grandstaff, a physician assistant. ''I don't think we should govern what we do with your body. Right or wrong biblically, whatever it is, I don't believe it.''\par \par \uc2 Still, she planned to cast her third vote for her party's nominee in November. ''I'm all Trump,'' Ms. Grandstaff said.\par \par \uc2 In north Phoenix, Lisa Hoberg, a Republican committeewoman, said she was ''barely hanging on'' to her Republican registration.\par \par \uc2 The Jan. 6, 2021, attack on the U.S. Capitol was the turning point for her with Mr. Trump. At this moment, she said, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is important but only one of many concerning issues. She plans to vote for a mix of Republicans and Democrats in November and struggles to categorize her views as ''pro-life'' or ''pro-choice.''\par \par \uc2 ''My ideals are small government, freedoms,'' Ms. Hoberg said. ''Stay out of our classrooms, stay out of our bedrooms, stay out of my exam rooms.''\par \par \uc2 The Arizona State Courts building in Phoenix, where the state Supreme Court reinstated a near-total \uc2  ban from 1864. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CAITLIN O'HARA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240413ek4d0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
