\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Use Simple Attack on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 : Trump Did This\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer, Reid J. Epstein and Nicholas Nehamas \par \uc2 1,704 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The party is unifying around a blunt message that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  pushed for privately ahead of her Friday trip to Arizona, where Democrats hope to keep Republicans reeling.\par \par \uc2 In a meeting with her staff last week, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  offered a prediction: Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  would not support a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Instead, she said, he would take a position that would muddy the waters on an issue that she believed could be deeply damaging for his campaign.\par \par \uc2 We need to make him own this, she told her aides.\par \par \uc2 Days later, as rumors circulated that a court ruling was coming on Arizona's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Ms. Harris instructed that an event in Tucson about student loans should instead focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, according to three Democratic officials familiar with the matter, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss the internal deliberations.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Arizona's top court upheld an 1864 law that bans nearly all abortions. And on Friday, before more than 100 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists and supporters, Ms. Harris plans to deliver a simple message: Blame \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 From campaigns for state legislatures to the race for the White House, Democrats have unified around a central message of protecting what remains of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the United States, along with the availability of long-established \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures like contraception and fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic effort underscores how the 2022 Supreme Court decision ending federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights remade American politics. Four years ago, \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  rarely mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in his general-election campaign, fearing the issue could alienate moderate voters and would not sufficiently energize his base. Now, after the fall of Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are a centerpiece of his re-election bid, the first time that an American presidential campaign has focused so intensely on women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After largely abandoning an effort to brand economic progress under the banner of Bidenomics, the president's team has found a simpler, easier-to-understand slogan to use wherever states are restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this,'' reads the closing shot of one of the campaign's new advertisements about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That message, Democrats say, has been supercharged by the Arizona \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision. The state is only the most prominent of dozens where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain front and center in November, between continued bans on the procedure, a steady drumbeat of legal rulings and painful personal stories of women affected by the restrictions, and ballot measures asking voters whether to expand rights and access.\par \par \uc2 ''The more and more we see these examples, the more and more people are like: 'Oh God, that could happen to my family. That could happen to someone I love,''' said \uc2 Jennifer O'Malley Dillon\uc2 , the chairwoman of Mr. Biden's campaign. ''We believe now when fundamental rights are taken away, it is not an issue people are going to forget.''\par \par \uc2 This week, Democrats have rallied around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in races from coast to coast. In Florida, former Representative \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2 , who is running for the Senate, began a ''Florida Freedom Tour'' after a decision by the Florida Supreme Court last week allowing a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. In North Carolina, two women who experienced life-threatening complications after being denied abortions in their home states appeared at Biden campaign events.\par \par \uc2 Spanish-language signs erected in Phoenix and Tempe blamed Mr. Trump for the Arizona ban, and billboards in swing House districts from California to Texas accused Republicans of voting against treatments like in vitro fertilization. And in Arizona, the Biden campaign announced a ''seven-figure investment'' in ads focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Much more is scheduled for the coming weeks. The Biden campaign is sketching out plans to campaign around notable -- and some far less known -- anniversaries over the next two months.\par \par \uc2 In May, it plans to mark the leak of a draft of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe and the anniversary of a Nebraska ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 12 weeks. June will bring the anniversaries of the 1965 Supreme Court decision in Griswold v. Connecticut, which legalized contraception for married couples, and of the ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization that ended a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Biden campaign aides believe the issue could sway the presidential race in their favor not only in Arizona but also in North Carolina, which a Democrat last won in 2008. Even Florida could possibly be in play, they suggest, despite its recent shift to the right.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in places like Florida could have national ramifications, Biden campaign aides say. Florida and Arizona are not perceived to be as socially conservative as some of the Southern states that enacted bans on the procedure before the Supreme Court ruling, these aides argue, and their tightening restrictions will help illustrate the widespread impact of the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''Folks are keenly aware that it may be Arizona today, but in an election or two, it could come to your neighborhood,'' said Lt. Gov. Austin Davis of Pennsylvania, a Democrat. ''It is very much something that I hear on the road and that people come up and talk about.''\par \par \uc2 Not everyone agrees: Some within the Biden campaign worry that focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lead it to neglect other issues on which the president is struggling more for approval, including inflation, immigration and -- this week -- student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Molly Murphy, a Biden pollster, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were particularly motivating for younger voters, whom Mr. Biden has struggled to energize. But she said the issue resonated far beyond them, reaching much of the party's voting base, including white women without college degrees, Latino women and suburban swing voters.\par \par \uc2 ''I think we actually can't talk about it enough,'' she said. ''I've actually not seen any group where it just falls flat.''\par \par \uc2 Polls have shown that a broad majority of voters believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be entirely or mostly legal and that more voters trust Mr. Biden than Mr. Trump on the issue. Twelve percent of voters in a recent survey by KFF, a nonprofit group focused on health policy, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the ''most important issue'' in their 2024 vote. That is a significant shift from 2020, when a larger share of self-described ''pro-life'' voters were more likely to say the issue was important to their vote than self-described ''pro-choice'' voters.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Mr. Trump distanced himself from the Arizona law and speculated that Florida's six-week ban was ''probably, maybe going to change.'' He continued to defend the position he had taken in a video statement on Monday, when he said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws should be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 Yet Democrats believe Mr. Trump will not be able to outrun blame for appointing the three Supreme Court justices who helped overturn Roe -- a ruling he often highlights as a major accomplishment of his presidency. Democrats are also leveraging this strategy against other Republican candidates by pointing to their past support for legislation that would curtail or even eradicate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''It's gone from an abstract concern that women might lose their right to make their own health care decisions to reality'' that they have already lost it, said \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2 , the Democratic nominee for governor in North Carolina, which enacted a 12-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban last year. ''When you compound it with the fact that we are now the southernmost state in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains lawful, it becomes all the more concerning.''\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee has identified 18 competitive races in states where measures expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are likely to appear on the ballot in November. There also are at least five competitive Senate races in states where advocates are working to place \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  questions on the November ballot -- including Florida, where Mr. Trump lives. On Wednesday, he dodged a question about how he would vote on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''Democrats can run on a unifying message from state legislative races to the Senate to the presidential campaign,'' said \uc2 Ben Wikler\uc2 , the chairman of the Democratic Party of Wisconsin, who helped liberals win a critical State Supreme Court election last year. ''It creates the possibility of a surround-sound campaign.''\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats believe their most powerful message comes from the women directly affected by the state bans. Democratic strategists from the Biden campaign and for congressional candidates anticipate featuring more accounts of women delivering heart-rending accounts of losing their pregnancies and facing dire medical consequences when they were denied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 Even some Democratic candidates have begun sharing stories about their own abortions that would have been considered too politically charged even just two years ago. On Thursday, Lucia B\u225\'00\'E1ez-Geller, a Democrat running for Congress in a South Florida district held by a Republican, wrote about her decision to end a nonviable pregnancy at 12 weeks in an opinion essay published in The Miami Herald. Under the six-week ban, which is expected to go into effect by May 1, such a procedure would become harder, if not impossible, to receive.\par \par \uc2 ''There is definitely heightened attention around this, and I just feel that my story is one of many,'' Ms. B\u225\'00\'E1ez-Geller said in an interview. ''And I think my story is going to pale in comparison to the stories that we're going to hear when this ban goes into effect.''\par \par \uc2 Michael Gold contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gold contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 With Donald J. Trump and the Republican Party struggling to talk about \uc2 , Democrats are hoping to use the issue as a central message. Left, Vice President Kamala Harris will visit Tucson on Friday, for an event focusing on \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY NIC ANTAYA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JENN ACKERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Jesse Goodsell, with Healthcare Rising, spoke with voters in Phoenix last month. Efforts to place \uc2  questions on the November ballot are underway in Arizona and several other states. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ASH PONDERS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240412ek4c0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
