\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Lake Denounces Arizona Ban She Had Once Called a 'Great Law'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chris Cameron \par \uc2 735 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In her 2022 race for governor, Ms. Lake delivered a strict anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message. Now running for Senate, she is retreating from that position.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, the leading Republican candidate for Senate in Arizona, was quick to denounce the state Supreme Court's ruling upholding an 1864 law banning nearly all abortions in the state. The law is ''out of step with Arizonans,'' she said in a statement. She called on state lawmakers to ''come up'' with a ''solution that Arizonans can support.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Lake, an ally of former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and a 2020 election denier, had voiced enthusiastic support for the law less than two years ago, when she was in the midst of a scorched-earth campaign for the Republican nomination for governor. Asked then what she thought of the ban, she said she was thrilled it existed and called it a ''great law.''\par \par \uc2 Asked for comment, the Lake campaign pointed to a post from Caroline Wren, a senior adviser to Ms. Lake, who insisted on Tuesday that Ms. Lake was not referring to the territorial-era law in the interview. But in that 2022 appearance, Ms. Lake cited the 1864 law's number in the Arizona state code.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm incredibly thrilled that we are going to have a great law that's already on the books. I believe it's ARS 13-3603,'' she said in a 2022 interview on ''The Conservative Circus With James T. Harris.'' She made other remarks in support of the 1864 law during that campaign as well.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lake's retreat from the fervent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric of her early 2022 campaign reflects the sharp changes in the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the nearly two years since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, ending the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her shift also signals grave concern from Republicans, both in Arizona and across the country, that the issue will leave them electorally vulnerable in the fall -- particularly in crucial battleground states like Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been searching for a position that will shield them from the electoral blowback they have seen since Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 But the revival of the 1864 law in Arizona amounts to something of a nightmare scenario for Republicans in the state. The Civil War-era law, which had lain dormant for decades, was enacted shortly after Arizona was organized as a remote frontier territory of less than 10,000 residents -- and almost half a century before Arizona became a state and, months later, adopted women's suffrage.\par \par \uc2 Starting in the 2022 midterms and in governors' races, special elections and ballot measures, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue has helped Democrats notch victories across the country. And the Democratic Party is eager to push the issue to the front of this year's races.\par \par \uc2 The White House said on Tuesday that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- who has focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on the campaign trail -- would travel to Tucson, Ariz., on Friday to campaign on the issue. Last month, Ms. Harris met with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and staff members at a clinic in St. Paul, Minn., a striking political move that underscored Democrats' new assertiveness on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, who had already seized on Mr. Trump's new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance on Monday, unleashed a salvo of fresh attacks after the Arizona ruling. They pointed to his latest statement that whatever states decide ''must be the law of the land, and in this case, the law of the state,'' as well as to his repeated boasting that he was responsible for ending Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats also trained their focus on Ms. Lake, posting other remarks from 2022, during which she expressed strict anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lake, who is expected to win her primary, is likely to face Representative \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , a Democrat, in the fall, in a contest to determine the successor of Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , who is not seeking re-election. Mr. Gallego's campaign recently said it had raised $7.5 million in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake said an 1864 state law banning \uc2  was ''out of step with Arizonans.'' During her run for Arizona governor in 2022, she praised the law. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAIYUN JIANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240411ek4b0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
