\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Waffle Leaves Backers on Right Questioning True Views\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With his video statement on Monday, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  laid bare how faulty a messenger he had always been for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  ran for president in 2016, the leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement extracted a series of promises from him in exchange for backing his nomination.\par \par \uc2 They demanded Supreme Court justices who would overturn Roe v. Wade. They insisted that he defund \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . They pushed for a vice president who was a champion of their cause. And each time, he said yes.\par \par \uc2 But that was then.\par \par \uc2 With Roe v. Wade left on the ''ash heap of history,'' as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders are fond of saying, they find themselves no longer calling the shots. Their movement remains mighty in Republican-controlled statehouses and with conservative courts, but it is weaker nationally than it has been in years. Many Republican strategists and candidates see their cause, even the decades-old term ''pro-life,'' as politically toxic. And on Monday, their biggest champion, the man whom they call the ''most pro-life president in history,'' chose politics over their principles -- and launched a series of vitriolic attacks on some of their top leaders.\par \par \uc2 With his clearest statement yet on the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the fall of Roe in 2022, Mr. Trump laid bare how faulty a messenger he had always been for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause. When he first flirted with a presidential run in 1999, Mr. Trump was clear about his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : ''I'm very pro-choice,'' he said. He reversed that stance a dozen years later: ''Just very briefly, I'm pro-life,'' he told attendees at the Conservative Political Action Conference in 2011.\par \par \uc2 His support shifted again after the Supreme Court's decision. While he bragged about appointing three of the justices who overturned Roe, he blamed the movement for Republican losses in the midterm elections. He mused aloud about the idea of a federal ban, but refused to give it the kind of ringing endorsement anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders wanted.\par \par \uc2 In his four-minute video statement on Monday, Mr. Trump said that states and their voters should decide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies for themselves, in language that sounded like a free-for-all to the staunchest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. He backed access to fertility treatments such as I.V.F., and supported exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in cases of rape, incest and the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 His remarks were low on specifics. Mr. Trump skirted the question of whether he would back a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if legislation came to him as president. He did not say whether he supported state bans that did not provide those exceptions, or whether he would vote for a measure enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in his home state of Florida. And he did not address the experiences of women who have faced impossible choices and medical crises in states where the procedure is now banned.\par \par \uc2 ''You must follow your heart, or in many cases, your religion or your faith,'' he said. ''Do what's right for your family and do what's right for yourself.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said later he believed his statement defanged what he saw as a toxic issue for his party by liberating Republicans to run on more politically favorable issues, including what he described on social media as ''the Horrible Border, Inflation, Bad Economy, and the Death & Destruction of our Country!''\par \par \uc2 Some of the most stalwart anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  champions said that as much as Mr. Trump wanted to neutralize the politics of the issue, he could not outrun what his presidency had unleashed. States across the country are enmeshed in battles over the details of their restrictions on the procedure, as Democrats push ballot measures across the country to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitutions. Stories about women being denied the procedure continue to dominate the news. And the Supreme Court is expected to rule in June on curtailing access to a prominent medication used in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures.\par \par \uc2 Even as Mr. Trump's views on whether he would sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban remain opaque, his allies and supporters are moving ahead with plans to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with proposals and executive actions that could go beyond a national ban in a potential second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 ''Saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue belongs in the states will not make it disappear from national elections,'' \uc2 Leonard A. Leo\uc2 , a longtime leader of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  who played an influential role in Mr. Trump's selections for the Supreme Court, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Yet Mr. Trump's comments underscored how the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has struggled to find its footing in the post-Roe era. For decades, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had one central goal of overturning Roe. Now, they face a political landscape radically reshaped by that decision, and a presumptive Republican presidential nominee who no longer sees them as an undeniable asset but as a potential political liability.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, leaders of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups started to get word that a statement from Mr. Trump was coming. They called around to try to learn what it would say.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, the leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said she spoke with Mr. Trump on Monday morning. Her group had extracted many promises from Mr. Trump in 2016 and frequently visited the White House. But it had failed in its push for Mr. Trump to endorse a federal 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 ''His concern is political only,'' Ms. Dannenfelser said in an interview. ''It is a huge disappointment. It is a total eclipse of reason, and that only happens in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics for Republicans.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , called the announcement from his former boss a ''slap in the face'' to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters who supported Mr. Trump in two previous elections. ''Too many Republican politicians are all too ready to wash their hands of the battle for life,'' he wrote on social media.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump reacted to the criticism with a series of scathing attacks on Ms. Dannenfelser and Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, who also questioned the former president's commitment to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement on Monday. Mr. Trump took full credit for the decision to overturn Roe, disregarding the decades of work by activists and lawyers to build a conservative movement to undercut Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''Lindsey, Marjorie, and others fought for years, unsuccessfully, until I came along and got the job done,'' he posted on his social media site, Truth Social. ''We cannot let our Country suffer any further damage by losing Elections on an issue that should always have been decided by the States, and now will be!''\par \par \uc2 Those who were less publicly critical did not face the same ire. \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , the president of Students for Life, said Mr. Trump was ''very clear affirming the family'' in his statement. She was confident that if elected Mr. Trump would staff his administration with aides who would pursue efforts to further limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and access across the country.\par \par \uc2 ''I hope he sticks with this statement,'' she said, ''and then moves forward and starts naming pro-life appointments, naming his pro-life vice president pick, pledging that he is going to only appoint pro-life leaders to the Department of Justice, Health and Human Services, Department of Education, F.D.A., E.P.A., down the entire cabinet line.''\par \par \uc2 None of the critics said they planned on withholding their support for Mr. Trump in November, an indication that the former president may not pay a very high price for failing to adopt a more aggressive federal position.\par \par \uc2 The idea of a 15-week ban was always more about politics than policy. Such a proposal would not end many abortions. Nearly 94 percent of abortions happen before 13 weeks in pregnancy, according to data collected by the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . Nor would such a ban be likely to capture enough support in the Senate for passage. And it would not affect the 18 states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently banned before that point in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But it was unpopular among independent and moderate voters. Polling from KFF, a nonprofit organization focused on health policy, found that six in 10 voters opposed a federal ban after 16 weeks -- a finding disputed by many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates but not other Republican strategists.\par \par \uc2 ''He hit all the right notes in this statement actually,'' said Nicole McCleskey, a Republican pollster who has conducted focus groups about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''He landed where the majority of most Americans are.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats disagree. They point to polling that shows most Americans support some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and want to see those rights restored into federal law. From the White House to candidates in down-ballot races, Democrats were ready to pounce with a flurry of attacks that blamed the former president for what some called the ''cruelty and the chaos'' caused by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made it clear once again today that he is -- more than anyone in America -- the person responsible for ending Roe v. Wade,'' President Biden said in a statement. ''Having created the chaos of overturning Roe, he's trying to say, 'Oh, never mind. Don't punish me for that. I just want to win.'''\par \par \uc2 Democrats argued that Mr. Trump's silence on the issue was effectively an endorsement of total bans in states such as Texas, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in nearly all circumstances. Their effort underscores the difficulty Mr. Trump may face as he tries to distance himself from an issue that will most likely remain in the headlines through Election Day and beyond.\par \par \uc2 ''He understands acutely how unpopular his party's position is on this and how unpopular his actions were,'' said \uc2 Mini Timmaraju\uc2 , the president of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 , an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group formerly known as \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . ''He's trying really hard to have it both ways, and we can't let him get away with it.''\par \par \uc2 Within hours of Mr. Trump's statement, Mr. Biden's campaign released a digital ad spotlighting the story of a Texas woman who was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , developed sepsis and may be unable to ever have another child.\par \par \uc2 In the ad, text flashes onto the screen. ''\uc2 Trump\uc2  did this,'' it reads, over the sounds of her sobs.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gold contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gold contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Former President Donald J. Trump has blamed the anti-\uc2  movement for Republican losses in the midterm elections. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240410ek4a00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
