\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling Could Become a Major Factor in Critical Arizona Races\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer, Nicholas Nehamas and Reid J. Epstein \par \uc2 1,338 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats quickly aimed to capitalize on a ruling by the state's highest court upholding an 1864 law that bans nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 Democrats seized on a ruling on Tuesday by Arizona's highest court upholding an 1864 law that bans nearly all abortions, setting up a fierce political fight over the issue that is likely to dominate the presidential election and a pivotal Senate race in a crucial battleground state.\par \par \uc2 Even though the court put its ruling on hold for now, President Biden and his campaign moved quickly to blame former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  for the loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, noting that he has taken credit for appointing the Supreme Court justices who overturned a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Just a day earlier, Mr. Trump had sought to defang what has become a toxic issue for Republicans by saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions should be decided by the states and their voters.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump offered no immediate response to the decision, but Karoline Leavitt, a spokeswoman for his campaign, said: ''President Trump could not have been more clear. These are decisions for people of each state to make.''\par \par \uc2 Nowhere are the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more distilled than in Arizona, where liberal advocates have been pushing for a ballot measure in November that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the State Constitution. Supporters of the measure say they have already gathered enough signatures to put the question on the ballot ahead of a deadline in early July.\par \par \uc2 That means the state is likely to be front and center in a national push by Democrats to transform the 2024 race into another referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The issue has emerged as one of the party's strongest political weapons since the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade in 2022, powering them to a series of electoral victories. The Arizona ruling on Tuesday will pose yet another test for Republicans, who after decades of efforts to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and access have struggled to find a winning message on the issue amid the transformed politics of the post-Roe era.\par \par \uc2 The 1864 law will not be enforced immediately: In putting its ruling on hold, the court sent the matter back to a lower court to hear additional arguments about the legislation's constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats quickly aimed to capitalize on the news.\par \par \uc2 ''This ruling is a result of the extreme agenda of Republican elected officials who are committed to ripping away women's freedom,'' President Biden said in a statement minutes after the decision.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was also set to travel on Friday to Tucson, Ariz., to talk about the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- a trip that was fortuitously preplanned, a spokesman said. She has become a leading messenger for the Biden campaign on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , becoming the highest-ranking U.S. official to visit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. She has also held events on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in key battleground states including Wisconsin, Georgia and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, she squarely blamed Mr. Trump for the court's decision -- a tactic Democrats have taken on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 ''Arizona just rolled back the clock to a time before women could vote -- and, by his own admission, there's one person responsible: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The condemnation of the ruling was bipartisan, reflecting the continued power of the issue since the overturning of Roe in 2022. Both candidates in Arizona's Senate race, which is seen as one of the most important contests for control of the chamber, quickly disavowed the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , the Democratic candidate, called it ''devastating for Arizona women and their families'' and warned that ''women could die'' as a result of the new ban.\par \par \uc2 More strikingly, Kari Lake, his Republican opponent, echoed his comments, despite having praised the law in the past.\par \par \uc2 ''It is abundantly clear that the pre-statehood law is out of step with Arizonans,'' she said in a statement, adding, ''This is a very personal issue that should be determined by each individual state and her people.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats quickly blasted out examples of Ms. Lake's past support for the 19th-century legislation, including comments in which she called it a ''great law.''\par \par \uc2 Polling has shown that Mr. Biden has a clear edge over Mr. Trump on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite voters' preference for the former president on almost every other major issue. The political calculus for Mr. Biden and other Democrats is that every day in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the primary political topic is a better day than one spent discussing the economy, immigration or thorny foreign policy issues.\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe, Democrats have repeatedly won elections by making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the main issue in their campaigns. Last year, a liberal Wisconsin judge won a commanding victory in the state's crucial Supreme Court race, and Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky won re-election in a deep-red state -- both by focusing heavily on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are one of the strongest issues working in its favor in Arizona, where independents make up about a third of the electorate. In March, 50 percent of registered voters in the state said they trusted Mr. Biden to do a ''better job'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with 44 percent for Mr. Trump, a Fox News poll found.\par \par \uc2 ''We have folks across the spectrum who are going to be outraged by this decision and who are going to hold the person who brags about being responsible for it to account,'' said Jen Cox, a senior adviser for the Biden campaign in Arizona. ''And that's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said they expected to continue performing abortions through May as their lawyers and Democratic lawmakers search for new legal arguments and additional tactics to delay the ruling.\par \par \uc2 If reinstated, the law would pre-empt the state's current restriction on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks with a total ban outlawing the procedure from the moment of conception, except when necessary to save the life of the mother. The 1864 law contains no exceptions for rape or incest. Doctors prosecuted under the law could face fines and prison terms of two to five years.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement celebrated the ruling as a major step forward.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, a prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, praised the ruling as an ''enormous victory for unborn children and their mothers,'' saying her movement ''must continue to fight.''\par \par \uc2 But they largely stood alone in their support for the ruling. Some Arizona Republicans who are facing competitive re-election fights criticized the decision, despite their past opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Representative Juan Ciscomani, who said in 2022 that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law should be left to the states, called the ruling a ''disaster for women and providers.'' And Representative David Schweikert, who previously expressed support for the overturning of Roe and for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, wrote on social media, ''This issue should be decided by Arizonans, not legislated from the bench.''\par \par \uc2 Still, the Arizona ruling underscored the political limitations of efforts by Republicans to skirt specific questions about the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in more conservative states.\par \par \uc2 In Mr. Trump's remarks on Monday, he supported exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother, but he did not offer an opinion on whether state bans that do not include those caveats -- like the one in Arizona -- should stand.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Ruth Igielnik, Jack Healy, Annie Karni, Kellen Browning and Michael Gold.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Ruth Igielnik, Jack Healy, Annie Karni, Kellen Browning and Michael Gold.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Supreme Court justices at a hearing in 2021. Both rivals in the state's Senate race, seen as one of the most important contests for control of the chamber, quickly disavowed Tuesday's ruling. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MATT YORK/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240410ek4a0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
