\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 President Condemns Decision by State's Supreme Court as 'Cruel' and 'Extreme'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aishvarya Kavi \par \uc2 585 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden, who promised to continue to fight for the restoration of Roe v. Wade, said the ban was first enacted ''well before women had secured the right to vote.''\par \par \uc2 President Biden condemned a decision by Arizona's Supreme Court on Tuesday to uphold an 1864 ban on nearly all abortions as ''cruel'' and ''extreme,'' saying the law was first enacted well before women even had the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 In a statement released within an hour of the decision, Mr. Biden called the ruling an ''extreme agenda of Republican elected officials'' and promised to continue the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a restoration of Roe v. Wade, which had protected the right of women to have abortions for nearly a half century.\par \par \uc2 ''Millions of Arizonans will soon live under an even more extreme and dangerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which fails to protect women even when their health is at risk or in tragic cases of rape or incest,'' Mr. Biden said. ''This cruel ban was first enacted in 1864 -- more than 150 years ago, before Arizona was even a state and well before women had secured the right to vote. This ruling is a result of the extreme agenda of Republican elected officials who are committed to ripping away women's freedom.''\par \par \uc2 The decision in Arizona, a critical battleground state, comes as Mr. Biden's campaign and Democratic officials blame the dwindling access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in America squarely on former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The issue has been central in several recent Democratic election victories.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump on Monday released a video saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should be left to the states. The former president, whose conservative appointees to the Supreme Court helped overturn Roe in 2022, had for months refused to say whether he supported restrictive measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  like those outlined in the 160-year-old law that Arizona's highest court said on Tuesday ''is now enforceable.''\par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe ''paved the way for the chaos and confusion we're seeing play out across the country today,'' \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the White House press secretary, told reporters on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 ''There are now 21 extreme state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect across the country,'' Ms. Jean-Pierre said. ''One-third of all women of reproductive age now live in a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.''\par \par \uc2 The White House also said Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would visit Arizona on Friday to highlight ''extremists'' in the state who were pushing for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 During a trip there last month, Ms. Harris criticized Mr. Trump's role in the spread of state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''The former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , handpicked three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  because he intended for them to overturn Roe,'' Ms. Harris said last month. ''He intended for them to take your freedoms, and he brags about it.''\par \par \uc2 The Arizona law bans nearly all abortions, a decision that could have far-reaching consequences for women's health care and election-year politics. Doctors prosecuted under the law could face fines and prison terms of two to five years.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal in Arizona through 15 weeks of pregnancy until Tuesday's ruling.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden on Tuesday reaffirmed his support for a federal law that would restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights once protected by Roe v. Wade. But congressional action on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections is unlikely.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240410ek4a0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
