\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Says  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Will Be Left to the States. Don't Believe Him.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Donald Trump was asked about the recent Florida Supreme Court decision upholding his adopted state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, he promised that he would announce where he stands this week -- a sign of how tricky the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  have become for the man who did more than any other to roll them back. \uc2 Sure\uc2  enough, on Monday he unveiled his latest position in a video statement that attempted to thread the needle between his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  base and the majority of Americans who want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal.\par \par \uc2 Trump's address was, naturally, full of lies, including the absurd claim that ''all legal scholars, both sides,'' wanted Roe v. Wade overturned and the obscene calumny that Democrats support ''execution after birth.'' But the most misleading part of his spiel was the way he implied that in a second Trump administration, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law would be left entirely up to the states. ''The states will determine by vote or legislation or perhaps both, and whatever they decide must be the law of the land, in this case the law of the state,'' said Trump.\par \par \uc2 Trump probably won't be able to dodge the substance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy for the entirety of a presidential campaign; eventually, he's going to have to say whether he'd sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if it crossed his desk and what he thinks of the sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibitions in many Republican states. But let's leave that aside for the moment, because when it comes to a second Trump administration, the most salient questions are about personnel, not legislation.\par \par \uc2 Before Monday, Trump reportedly considered endorsing a 16-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but the fact that he didn't should be of little comfort to voters who want to protect what's left of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in America. Should Trump return to power, he plans to surround himself with die-hard MAGA activists, not the establishment types he blames for undermining him during his first term. And many of these activists have plans to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally without passing any new laws at all.\par \par \uc2 A key to these plans is the Comstock Act, the 19th-century anti-vice law named for the crusading bluenose Anthony Comstock, who persecuted Margaret Sanger, arrested thousands and boasted of driving 15 of his targets to suicide. Passed in 1873, the Comstock Act prohibited the mailing of every ''obscene, lewd, lascivious, indecent, filthy or vile article,'' including ''every article, instrument, substance, drug, medicine or thing'' intended for ''producing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' Until quite recently, the Comstock Act was thought to be moot, made irrelevant by a series of Supreme Court decisions on the First Amendment, contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it was never repealed, and now that Trump's justices have scrapped Roe, his allies believe they can use Comstock to go after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't need a federal ban when we have Comstock on the books,'' Jonathan F. Mitchell, a former solicitor general of Texas and the legal mind behind the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty law, told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  in February. He is very much a MAGA insider; he represented Trump in the Supreme Court case arising from Colorado's attempt to boot the ex-president off the ballot as an insurrectionist. As The Times has reported, Mitchell is on a list of lawyers vetted by America First Legal, a nonprofit led by the Trump consigliere \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , as having the ''spine'' to serve in a second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Mitchell is far from the only Trumpist dreaming of bringing Comstock back from the dead. The 2025 Presidential Transition Project, a coalition of major right-wing think tanks, has published a 920-page plan for a new Trump administration, ''Mandate for Leadership.'' In it, Gene Hamilton, America First Legal's vice president and a former Trump Department of Justice official, lays out an agenda for the department to target \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 ''Following the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs, there is now no federal prohibition on the enforcement of this statute,'' he wrote of Comstock. ''The Department of Justice in the next conservative administration should therefore announce its intent to enforce federal law against providers and distributors of such pills.'' (''Mandate for Leadership'' also says that a Trump Food and Drug Administration should repeal approval for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 A resurrected Comstock Act wouldn't just stop women from ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail. It could also prevent doctors and pharmacies from dispensing them, since neither the Postal Service nor express carriers like UPS and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  would be allowed to ship them in the first place. And it would give the Justice Department a rationale for cracking down on the networks that help provide pills to women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Some interpretations of the Comstock Act might curtail surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, since supplies used to perform them travel through the mail. Abortions could remain legal in some states but become nearly impossible to obtain.\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders, knowing that their schemes are unpopular, don't want Trump to talk about them before he's in office. Speaking of Comstock, a movement attorney told The Atlantic's Elaine Godfrey: ''It's obviously a political loser, so just keep your mouth shut. Say you oppose a federal ban, and see if that works.''\par \par \uc2 That is clearly what Trump is trying to do. Whether it works is up to all of us.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY MULTIEXPOSURE IMAGE BY DAMON WINTER/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A22. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240410ek4a00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
