\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Case Opens Path to Block Wider  \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,052 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Though the Florida Supreme Court allowed a ballot question on expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, it also laid out a way for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to challenge such an expansion.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters celebrated last week when the Florida Supreme Court said voters could decide this fall whether to approve a state constitutional amendment protecting and expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Florida. But the court also laid out a road map for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to challenge any expansion, by raising the prospect of ''fetal personhood.''\par \par \uc2 In concurring and dissenting opinions that accompanied the ruling, four of the seven justices on the conservative court indicated that they may interpret the State Constitution to grant fetuses the same legal rights as people, foretelling the next likely court fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The proposed state constitutional amendment would guarantee access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''before viability,'' or before about 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''If this does pass -- but even if it doesn't pass -- I think that there is an open door to go to the Florida Supreme Court,'' said Mathew D. Staver, a lawyer for two anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that oppose the ballot measure, the Liberty Counsel and Florida Voters Against Extremism.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have promoted the fetal personhood legal strategy since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  eliminated the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. What has been perhaps their biggest success came in February, when the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  held that a frozen embryo should be considered a person, opening a new front in the legal debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What was striking in Florida was that the argument was not raised by one of the lawyers opposing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure, but rather by the court.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Carlos G. Mu\u241\'00\'F1iz asked during oral arguments in February -- before the Alabama ruling -- whether the proposed Florida amendment might interfere with the rights of the fetus.\par \par \uc2 Put on the spot by Chief Justice Mu\u241\'00\'F1iz, neither side took a firm position. But after the hearing, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers filed an additional brief that cited the Alabama case and urged the court to consider fetal rights in its deliberations.\par \par \uc2 When the court's ruling came down on April 1, Chief Justice Mu\u241\'00\'F1iz sided with the 4-3 majority in allowing the proposed amendment to go to voters. But a footnote in the majority opinion called the question of fetal rights ''unsettled.''\par \par \uc2 ''Someone is certainly going to take him up on that,'' said Mary Ziegler, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law expert at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who previously taught at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2  in Tallahassee. ''Someone is going to test the water.''\par \par \uc2 The three justices in the minority -- Justices Renatha Francis, Jamie R. Grosshans and Meredith L. Sasso -- also directly addressed fetal rights in separate dissents.\par \par \uc2 ''The voter may think this amendment results in settling this issue once and for all,'' Justice Grosshans wrote. ''It does not.''\par \par \uc2 The court, she wrote, has yet to address whether the rights guaranteed by the Florida Constitution ''apply to the unborn and, if so, what the scope of those rights could be.'' She went on to call the amendment defective because it did not make clear to voters that the question was not yet settled.\par \par \uc2 Aadika Singh, a lawyer for the Public Rights Project, which filed a court brief in support of the ballot measure, said it was ''troubling'' that the justices had signaled they would be willing to take up a challenge to the amendment, even as they ruled that voters could decide whether to approve it.\par \par \uc2 ''They went out of their way,'' she said of the court, to say, '''We are willing, in a future case, to undermine that democratic decision.'''\par \par \uc2 Still, she added: ''The ballot measure decision is an important victory. It allows Floridians to weigh in directly on the issue, and that is a win for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and democracy.''\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are trying to put measures on the ballot in about 10 states to secure access to the procedure. Former President Donald J. Trump, who had given mixed signals on his position, said in a video statement on Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions should be left up to the states: ''It's all about the will of the people,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In a separate ruling last week, the Florida justices affirmed a state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 15 weeks of pregnancy -- a decision that effectively allowed a more recently enacted 6-week ban to take effect by May 1.\par \par \uc2 The fine print of the court's opinions underscored that getting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure on the ballot was not likely to end the battle, even if the amendment gets more than 60 percent of the vote in November, as is needed for it to pass.\par \par \uc2 There is precedent for a Florida constitutional amendment to be challenged before Election Day and overturned long afterward. The state Supreme Court ruled in 2000 that an amendment adopted nearly two years earlier, regarding the wording of the death penalty in the State Constitution, was unconstitutional because it had misled voters.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Staver said he was aware of that precedent and was considering options for challenging the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure. ''That's an avenue that we are looking at,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Florida Legislature could also try to thwart the amendment if it passes. After voters in Ohio protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with a constitutional amendment last year, some Republican lawmakers tried to strip state courts of the power to enforce the amendment.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and fellow Republicans in control of the State Legislature could seek to define key terms in the constitutional amendment, including ''viability,'' said Barbara J. Pariente, a former chief justice on the Florida Supreme Court who retired in 2019.\par \par \uc2 ''My feeling is that, in Florida, because of the governor we have and the Republican majority, that there will be every attempt to interfere with the intent of the amendment,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Florida voters approved a constitutional amendment restoring voting rights for most felons. By 2020, much of the measure had been gutted by lawmakers and the courts.\par \par \uc2 Outside the Florida Supreme Court as justices heard arguments on an \uc2  amendment. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALICIA DEVINE/TALLAHASSEE DEMOCRAT, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240409ek4900045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict