\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats See a Potential Opening in Florida\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,066 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rulings allowing a strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, along with a ballot question on expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, may inject new life into Democratic campaigns in the state.\par \par \uc2 Suddenly, November got a lot more interesting in Florida.\par \par \uc2 The nation's third-largest state, once the biggest battleground in presidential politics, has become less important as its election results have trended repeatedly toward the political right. Few consider it a true swing state anymore.\par \par \uc2 But three rulings from the Florida Supreme Court on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and marijuana, released on Monday, may inject new life into Democratic campaigns before the general election on Nov. 6.\par \par \uc2 The court, which leans conservative, upheld a ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, allowing an even more restrictive six-week ban to soon take effect. However, the court also allowed a proposed constitutional amendment on the ballot that would guarantee access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''before viability,'' or at about 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In a third decision, the court gave the go-ahead to a separate ballot measure that would legalize recreational marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, Democrats see the rulings as an opening to drive their voters -- and perhaps new voters likely to support their candidates -- to the polls.\par \par \uc2 ''It has the potential to pull out more voters, and those voters are more likely to be with us than with the other guys,'' said Christina Reynolds, senior vice president of communications for Emily's List, which supports and funds Democratic women running for office. ''It draws some focus to Florida that might otherwise not be there, because we've had our hearts broken before.''\par \par \uc2 No one is suggesting that two constitutional amendments are enough to swing the presidential race in Florida against former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , a Palm Beach resident who won the state in 2016 and 2020. Though President Biden has traveled to Florida for fund-raisers, he is not expected to spend much time campaigning -- or paying for expensive television advertising -- in the state.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's campaign manager, Julie Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez, said in a memo after the rulings were released that the president had an ''opening'' in Florida, though it ''is not an easy state to win.''\par \par \uc2 In the past, Floridians have elected Republicans while also approving ballot proposals promoted by liberal-leaning groups, including ones that set a $15 hourly minimum wage, restored felons' voting rights and legalized medical marijuana.\par \par \uc2 And Florida elections tend to be closer in presidential years than in midterm years. Moving a few thousand votes here and there could affect races down the ballot. On Monday, Democrats rushed to point out that Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , a Republican running for re-election, said that he would have signed the six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican, enacted the 15-week ban in 2022. Last year, as he prepared to run for president in more religious states like Iowa, he signed the six-week ban, even though polls have repeatedly shown that a majority of Floridians want most abortions to be legal. Mr. Trump criticized Mr. DeSantis for supporting the six-week ban, calling it ''a terrible thing.''\par \par \uc2 Several political observers noted that Mr. DeSantis's party might have had an easier time rallying voters against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure if Republicans had stuck with the 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to Republicans what immigration is to Democrats: If you're talking about it, it's a complication, it's a problem, it's an obstacle,'' said \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , a former Republican congressman from Miami. ''It just gives Democrats a path to relevance in the state again, where they've been just kind of completely bankrupt for some years now.''\par \par \uc2 Florida Democrats have lost significant ground in voter registration to Republicans, struggled to raise money and failed to organize a political apparatus that can compete with the perennially well-funded Republican machine. In 2022, Mr. DeSantis flipped Miami-Dade County, the state's most populous county, which had previously been part of Democrats' liberal stronghold in southeast Florida.\par \par \uc2 Anna Hochkammer, the executive director of the Florida Women's Freedom Coalition, said that she expected the effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure to be ''significant'' for other races. Polling conducted last month for her group and Floridians Protecting Freedom, which includes \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , showed 73 percent support for the constitutional amendment, she said. It needs more than 60 percent to pass.\par \par \uc2 ''Supporters tend to be quite firm in their support, while opponents tend to be quite squishy,'' Ms. Hochkammer said. ''This polls well across all demographics. It's motivating to young people and women, too. No one can deny that it will shape the voter universe.''\par \par \uc2 State Representative Paul Renner, a Republican who is the speaker of the Florida House, told reporters on Monday that legislative leaders would help try to defeat the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have vowed to wage a vigorous campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''There will be an organized effort, I can say that definitively,'' Mr. Renner said. ''The effort really will be focused on those in the middle in Florida.''\par \par \uc2 He and other Republican lawmakers portrayed the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban as a common-sense compromise that allows for exceptions when the mother's health is at stake. The 15-week ban does not have exceptions for rape or incest, though the six-week ban does.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Renner dismissed a suggestion that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and marijuana ballot questions would make the general election more competitive. ''No,'' he said. ''I think every election is important and consequential.''\par \par \uc2 ''With full education and with full understanding of what these amendments do,'' he added, ''they'll both be voted down.''\par \par \uc2 But members of the board of the RBG Fund, a Tallahassee organization that provides financial assistance to people who travel to Florida to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the rules in their home states are more restrictive, said that they felt the political ground shift.\par \par \uc2 ''Everybody who was biting their nails about this decision is jubilant right now,'' said Karen Woodall, 66. ''This is going to inspire people.''\par \par \uc2 Valerie Crowder contributed reporting from Tallahassee, Fla.\par \par \uc2 Valerie Crowder contributed reporting from Tallahassee, Fla.\par \par \uc2 Activists protested \uc2  bans in January outside the Miami office of State Representative David Borrero, a Republican. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CRISTOBAL HERRERA-ULASHKEVICH/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240403ek430003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
