\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Florida Court Allows Six-Week Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,084 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Florida Supreme Court found that the State Constitution's privacy protections do not extend to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it also allowed a ballot question on whether to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The Florida Supreme Court overturned decades of legal precedent on Monday in ruling that the State Constitution's privacy protections do not extend to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , effectively allowing Florida to ban the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But in a separate decision released at the same time, the justices allowed Florida voters to decide this fall whether to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. The court ruled 4 to 3 that a proposed constitutional amendment that would guarantee the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''before viability,'' usually around 24 weeks, could go on the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 The rulings encapsulated, in a single state on a single day, how the country has grappled with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned the Roe v. Wade decision that recognized federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in 1973.\par \par \uc2 The conservative-leaning court found 6-1 that a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban enacted in 2022 was constitutional. That ruling -- in response to a lawsuit brought by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  and several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers -- will allow a six-week ban enacted last year to take effect by May 1.\par \par \uc2 ''Based on our analysis finding no clear right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  embodied within the Privacy Clause, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  cannot overcome the presumption of constitutionality and is unable to demonstrate beyond a reasonable doubt that the 15-week ban is unconstitutional,'' Justice Jamie R. Grosshans wrote in the majority opinion.\par \par \uc2 In paving the way for the six-week ban, the court cemented the rapid transformation of Florida, once a destination for women seeking abortions in the American South, into a place with restrictive policies akin to those in surrounding states.\par \par \uc2 But allowing the ballot measure gave supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a chance to continue their national campaign to preserve access to the procedure by giving voters the opportunity to directly weigh in on the issue. Ballot measures in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have already succeeded in several states, including Ohio and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a historic day in the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Florida,'' said Lauren Brenzel, director for the Yes on 4 campaign that put forth the ballot measure. ''No longer will decisions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be left between politicians disconnected from the realities of everyday Floridians' lives.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups in about 10 states are trying to put measures on the ballot to secure access; Florida is the largest of those states.\par \par \uc2 Historically, many women from Southern states with tighter restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have traveled to Florida for the procedure. They will now have to seek abortions much further away, perhaps in Virginia or Washington, D.C. Few women realize that they are pregnant at six weeks, and backers of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say the stricter ban, once effective, will amount to a near-total prohibition.\par \par \uc2 In its ruling allowing the six-week ban to take effect, a majority of the justices argued that past \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases had been wrongly decided based on an overly broad interpretation of the State Constitution's privacy clause -- an argument similar to the one the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  made in overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 The privacy clause in the Florida Constitution states: ''Every natural person has the right to be let alone and free from governmental intrusion into the person's private life.'' The Florida Supreme Court first ruled that it applied to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1989; in 2012, voters rejected an amendment that would have exempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from constitutional privacy protections.\par \par \uc2 But on Monday, a majority of the justices said that voters did not understand the privacy clause to extend to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they added it to the State Constitution in 1980, citing among other things the public and legislative debate at the time.\par \par \uc2 Justice Jorge Labarga, the lone dissenter, noted that the ruling would have far-reaching consequences.\par \par \uc2 ''The impact of today's decision extends far beyond the 15-week ban at issue in this case,'' he wrote. ''By operation of state statute, the majority's decision will result in even more stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in this state.''\par \par \uc2 The justices narrowly approved the ballot measure, saying it complies with Florida requirements that it be clear and limited to a single subject. Those who dissented argued in part that the language in the ballot question is too vague and could lead to years of further litigation.\par \par \uc2 Neither of Monday's rulings was a surprise: The seven-member court has moved to the right politically, with Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican, appointing four of the justices. But while the court had a Monday deadline to rule on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, it faced no similar timeline to rule on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 By issuing both rulings on the same day, the court gave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents a sought-after decision narrowing privacy protections. But by also allowing a ballot measure to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, it gave those groups little time to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 Immediately after the court ruled, opponents and proponents of the ballot measure accused the other side of being extreme, previewing their likely campaign messages.\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers who supported the 15-week and six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans argued that the ballot measure would allow abortions late in pregnancy. Abortions after 21 weeks are extremely rare and usually follow severe medical diagnoses.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not about government's interference with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but about allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the moment of birth,'' said State Senator Erin Grall of Vero Beach, who sponsored the six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Brenzel countered that it was the lawmakers who were out of step with a majority of Floridians. Public opinion polls have suggested that most Floridians believe abortions should be legal in most cases.\par \par \uc2 And Danielle Tallafuss, a Central Florida resident who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 22 weeks after learning that her fetus had a life-limiting heart condition before the state restricted the procedure, said women should be able to make the decision to terminate a pregnancy with their doctors.\par \par \uc2 ''My heart has been in turmoil since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and Florida's passage of a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,'' she said. ''Today I am filled with hope that residents will be able to vote in November to give \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access back to the women of this state.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A1, A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240402ek420003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
