\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Devastating Diagnosis, and Agonizing Choices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gina Kolata and Kim Raff \par \uc2 2,787 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ashlee Wiseman, a waitress at a Sizzler in Idaho Falls, Idaho, was 10 weeks pregnant when a nurse phoned with crushing news: a test of fetal DNA in her blood had found that her baby girl had trisomy 18, a catastrophic genetic abnormality, and was unlikely to survive.\par \par \uc2 Devastated, she called her partner, Clint Risenmay, who was at work. He broke down in tears.\par \par \uc2 Ashlee's response was different.\par \par \uc2 ''A still small voice took over me,'' she said. ''I'm like, 'I'm not going to listen to them. There has to be something that can help her. And there has to be someone who can help.'''\par \par \uc2 A social media search led her to Dr. John Carey, a professor emeritus of pediatrics at \uc2 the University of Utah\uc2 , who has devoted his life to helping families dealing with trisomy 18. He supports pregnant women who chose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also helps couples who want to have babies with this rare condition, though most will be stillborn or die within a year.\par \par \uc2 Ashlee and Clint were undeterred. They could do it, they assured Dr. Carey. They would lovingly care for a baby with complex medical needs.\par \par \uc2 The consequences of trisomy 18 are dire. The babies have three copies of chromosome 18 instead of two and, as a result, have serious medical and developmental problems. Nearly all are unable to eat, walk or talk, and all have severe cognitive disabilities. They often need open-heart surgery and feeding and breathing tubes. Many women, after hearing what is in store, choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Texas last year, Kate Cox, whose fetus had trisomy 18, left the state to end her much-wanted pregnancy just hours before the Texas Supreme Court issued a decision forbidding the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier this month, she watched President Biden's State of the Union address in Jill Biden's viewing box.\par \par \uc2 But in a survey of parents who had children with trisomy 18, nearly all described their children as happy and said they had enriched their families' lives however long they lived.\par \par \uc2 This is the story of two families who chose to have babies with trisomy 18, but took very different paths in how they cared for them.\par \par \uc2 'She's my baby'\par \par \uc2 After talking to Dr. Carey on the phone, Ashlee and Clint knew what they had to do.\par \par \uc2 They sold everything they could, packed their belongings in a U-Haul, and drove off to start a new life near Salt Lake City, where Dr. Carey lives. Their baby, Lennie Cardwell-Risenmay, was born on Jan. 9, 2023, at the University of Utah Hospital, weighing 3 pounds 15 ounces. Ashley and Clint fell in love with her immediately.\par \par \uc2 Their lives have not been easy.\par \par \uc2 Ashlee got a job as a waitress at a Sizzler near their small suburban apartment. She worked through Lennie's three open-heart surgeries in the year after her daughter was born.\par \par \uc2 Lennie spent five months in the hospital and then alternated between the hospital and an extended care facility for seven months. Every night at 10, when Ashlee's shift ended, she took an hourlong train ride into Salt Lake City to see her baby, arriving at the hospital or extended care facility around 11 p.m., then rushing out 15 to 20 minutes later to catch the last train home at midnight.\par \par \uc2 ''I would kiss her and let her know she wasn't alone,'' Ashlee said.\par \par \uc2 Before she and Clint could take Lennie home, they had to demonstrate that they could feed her through the tube in her stomach and operate her two breathing machines -- one to open her lungs and the other to put medicine in her lungs. They had to show that they knew how to remove, clean and reinsert her breathing tube, give her all her medicines and change her dressings. Finally, she and Clint had to stay with Lennie for 24 hours in the extended care facility to prove they could care for her without the help of nurses.\par \par \uc2 Ashlee feels she has sometimes been judged to be the wrong kind of woman to care for a baby with so many medical needs. One nurse, she said, took a look at her piercings and tattoos and asked if she even had a home to take Lennie to or money to live on.\par \par \uc2 Such judgment has only pushed her to fight harder for Lennie.\par \par \uc2 ''These trisomy 18 children need to have a chance to live,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ashlee quit work when she and Cliff brought the baby home to the family's apartment in Midvale, Utah, a suburb of Salt Lake City. They also have a son, Xyeno, 4, who has autism.\par \par \uc2 Lennie loves Xyeno, Ashlee said: ''When he comes home from school, she kicks her legs and smiles.''\par \par \uc2 She and Clint have routines with Lennie that make them happy. They put a blanket on the floor and watch her smile and roll.\par \par \uc2 ''She started to talk over her trach,'' Clint said, referring to Lennie's breathing tube. ''It's like a little coo.'' The couple is enthralled by Lennie's baby voice. Clint said he had mourned the thought that he might never hear it.\par \par \uc2 Lennie loves bath time, splashing and smiling and kicking.\par \par \uc2 ''When I look at the definition of Lennie, there is one word: joy,'' Clint said.\par \par \uc2 She and Clint gently cradle Lennie, careful not to disturb the tubes and machines she is hooked up to. She has a pacemaker implanted in her chest, a feeding tube in her stomach and a tube in her throat connected to a ventilator that helps her breathe.\par \par \uc2 Caring for her is all-consuming.\par \par \uc2 In the bedroom that they share with Lennie, Ashlee and Clint periodically suction mucus that collects in the baby's mouth, nose and breathing tube. To keep track of Lennie's complicated medication schedule, Ashlee and Clint write it on a whiteboard. They give her 10 medications and supplements each day and eight additional medications as needed.\par \par \uc2 Alarms go off day and night when the monitors measuring Lennie's blood oxygen level, heart rate and the functioning of the ventilator pump detect a problem.\par \par \uc2 In order to leave the house with Lennie, Ashlee and Clint have to bring an oxygen tank -- there are four lined up in the living room -- to take the place of the home ventilator that pumps air into her lungs. They also have two large oxygen tanks in case of a power failure.\par \par \uc2 ''Yes, it's a lot, but she's worth every bit of it,'' Ashlee said.\par \par \uc2 Lennie is tiny for her age -- she weighs just 14.4 pounds -- but is smiley and attentive. She lies on her back in a large crib lined with stuffed toys.\par \par \uc2 ''We call her Lennie Bee,'' Ashlee said. The baby, she explained, is a little bundle who reminds her of a bumblebee.\par \par \uc2 Clint calls her his little princess.\par \par \uc2 Although Medicaid, which paid for Lennie's hospitalizations, surgeries and medications, would have covered a home nursing aide, Ashlee turned down every candidate. None were trained to care for a baby with a breathing or feeding tube, she said. And she worried about how gently they would handle Lennie, who has brittle bones. Ashlee and Clint scoop her up by cradling her buttocks and the back of her head.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carey said he is concerned about the toll the care is taking on Ashlee and Clint.\par \par \uc2 There are hard days, Ashlee said, when she goes into the bathroom and cries.\par \par \uc2 But, she said, ''I do this because she's my baby and I love her more than anything else in this life.''\par \par \uc2 She said she used to angrily condemn those who chose to terminate pregnancies. But caring for Lennie has changed her.\par \par \uc2 ''I can't judge,'' she said. Taking care of a baby with trisomy 18 ''is not for everyone.''\par \par \uc2 ''I know Lennie's time is limited,'' Ashlee said. ''But even two years or five years or six years is better than none at all.''\par \par \uc2 'I will help you'\par \par \uc2 Thousands of women each year become pregnant with fetuses that have trisomy 18. Many have miscarriages early on, before they're even aware of the diagnosis. Trisomy 18 can be detected by a blood test toward the end of the first trimester. But each year, about 1,300 women whose babies have trisomy 18 have a stillbirth, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or a live birth, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the number of babies born with trisomy 18 may rise because of the Supreme Court's decision in 2022 to overturn the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Laws in 14 states now prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost entirely, two ban it at about six weeks of pregnancy and two prohibit it after 12 weeks. Often the condition is not diagnosed until the second trimester of pregnancy, which starts around the 14th week.\par \par \uc2 What couples need most, Dr. Carey said, is accurate information about what the condition will mean for their child and for them without imposing judgments about the parents' choices.\par \par \uc2 His path as a trisomy 18 expert was unexpected. In the summer of 1979, when he started his academic career at \uc2 the University of Utah\uc2 , he found himself with a new patient named Kari Holladay. She was 2 years old and had trisomy 18.\par \par \uc2 He was surprised. He had thought all such babies died of central apnea, a condition in which the brain stops sending signals to the muscles that control breathing, or from a severe heart defect.\par \par \uc2 But here was this little girl who defied the odds. Her mother, Kris Holladay, asked Dr. Carey for help. She wanted to start a support group. Families, she told Dr. Carey, felt alone and isolated.\par \par \uc2 ''I will help you,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The group, Support Organization for Trisomy, or SOFT, gradually took over much of the lives of Dr. Carey and his wife, Leslie, a former newborn intensive care nurse.\par \par \uc2 ''I've probably seen more children over age of 1, over 10, over age of 20, over age of 30 than anyone else in the medical profession,'' Dr. Carey said, referring to his patients with trisomy 18. (Kari Holladay lived to age 10.)\par \par \uc2 Some hospitals refuse to operate on babies with trisomy 18, reasoning that surgery will cause pain and suffering for the babies, who will have a poor quality of life and whose care will consume their parents' lives.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carey said he used to share this view, but changed his mind when he got to know families who sought intensive treatment for their babies. Now, when they are refused such care, Dr. Carey helps them find hospitals willing to provide it.\par \par \uc2 ''There is something about seeing these families cope that inspired me,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''Before I had perceived this as the parents' and the child's misfortune, a kind of tragedy, a burden,'' he said. But then he saw that the children brought meaning to their parents' lives.\par \par \uc2 ''The connections and relationships are the most rewarding and authentic I have had as a physician,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 An agonizing choice\par \par \uc2 Shauna Demars, a nurse who lives in Francis, Utah, a mountain town near Park City, first found out that she was pregnant with a baby boy who had trisomy 18 when she had an ultrasound exam. She was 20 weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 She waited for what seemed like an interminable time until, finally, a doctor came in and told her that the pathway connecting the two hemispheres of her baby's brain seemed to be missing.\par \par \uc2 ''It looks like trisomy 18,'' the doctor said.\par \par \uc2 Reeling, Shauna excused herself to go to the bathroom, where she quickly searched the condition on the internet. Most babies die, she read.\par \par \uc2 In the midst of their shock and sorrow, Shauna and her husband, Chris Demars, an independent contractor who helps build custom houses, had to decide what to do when their baby was born. Should doctors treat his medical problems aggressively, or only enough to make him comfortable?\par \par \uc2 They chose comfort care.\par \par \uc2 ''I agonized almost every minute of every day about choices and if we were making the right ones,'' Shauna said.\par \par \uc2 Hank was born on Oct. 22, 2019, and whisked to intensive care. A nurse read a report aloud. ''It's trisomy 18, which is incompatible with life,'' Shauna recalled her saying.\par \par \uc2 ''What's your goal?'' a doctor asked Shauna.\par \par \uc2 ''I want him to come home,'' she said. ''I want to take him home.''\par \par \uc2 When the doctor told the couple that the baby needed a tube in his airway to keep it open so he could breath, they refused.\par \par \uc2 ''That was probably the most difficult choice I've ever made,'' Shauna said. But as a nurse, she had cared for patients tethered to a respirator and knew what it would mean for Hank.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carey met with Shauna and Chris in the hospital and introduced them to other families who had babies with trisomy 18.\par \par \uc2 Hank came home a week later to the couple's modest ranch house. He was in hospice care; had supplemental oxygen to keep his blood oxygen levels high enough; and had a heart monitor, a blood oxygen monitor and a feeding tube that was hooked up every few hours. He slept in bed with Shauna and Chris at night.\par \par \uc2 One day Hank stopped breathing. The hospice nurse asked Shauna if she wanted to call an ambulance.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't want him to die in the hospital,'' she replied. ''I don't want him on a ventilator.''\par \par \uc2 After what seemed like hours, he started breathing again.\par \par \uc2 ''I let myself have a little bit of hope,'' Shauna said. ''Maybe he will be that one person who makes it to age 1. I tried to be rational. But he's my baby. I wanted a miracle.''\par \par \uc2 The apnea episodes occurred more and more frequently until, starting at 3 p.m. on Nov. 19, 2019, they came every hour.\par \par \uc2 ''That night, I laid awake and didn't think he'd make it through the night,'' Shauna said.\par \par \uc2 At 3 p.m. the next day, he had another episode. Shauna was holding him. She held her stethoscope to his tiny chest. Silence.\par \par \uc2 What do you do when an infant dies? she asked herself. Would he be buried in a lonely little grave?\par \par \uc2 She learned that babies could be buried on top of an adult.\par \par \uc2 Hank's small funeral service was held on Nov. 24, conducted by their Mormon bishop. Shauna and Chris dressed him. Chris's father spoke. Shauna read a poem. Chris carried the casket. And Hank was buried on top of his paternal uncle, who died at age 26 in a car accident.\par \par \uc2 ''Even though Hank had only 29 days, he had a life,'' Shauna said.\par \par \uc2 She has been humbled by the experience. Although she would not terminate a pregnancy, ''I am not going to judge someone who has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''You don't know what it is like to be in that situation,'' Shauna said.\par \par \uc2 But she has no regrets.\par \par \uc2 ''Going through the last half of my pregnancy with him, knowing that odds were not in his favor, and then, of course, his death, were the hardest things I've ever done,'' she said. ''But I would do it all again in a heartbeat for the time that we got to spend with him.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. John Carey, a professor emeritus of pediatrics at the University of Utah, with Lennie, also above. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ASHLEE WISEMAN);Shauna and Chris Demars with their sons Jack, left, and Luke, in their home in Francis, Utah. Shauna held a photograph of Hank, who lived for 29 days. ''I tried to be rational,'' she said. ''But he's my baby. I wanted a miracle.''; A plaster cast of Hank's hand. The family chose not to treat his medical problems aggressively, opting instead to make him comfortable at home. (A14); After five months in the hospital, Lennie alternated between the hospital and an extended care facility. Before Ashlee Wiseman and Clint Risenmay could take Lennie home, they had to show they could feed her through the tube in her stomach and operate her breathing machines. They keep Lennie's medication schedule on a whiteboard in the bedroom.; Hank's small funeral service was conducted by the family's Mormon bishop. Chris's father spoke, and Shauna read a poem. Hank was buried at the grave of his paternal uncle, who died several years ago at age 26 in a car accident. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KIM RAFF FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240401ek4100035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
