\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Reading the Tea Leaves: Where Justices Focused Their Questions on Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Several justices questioned the remedy of applying nationwide restrictions to mifepristone because it would be the first time a court had second-guessed the F.D.A.'s regulatory authority.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the Supreme Court seemed inclined on Tuesday to reject a bid to sharply limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 During about 90 minutes of argument, most of the justices seemed doubtful that the plaintiffs, who do not prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or regularly treat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, even had standing to bring the challenge. The justices, including several in the conservative majority, questioned whether the plaintiffs could show that they faced the moral harm they claimed to suffer from the availability of the pill, mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The case centers on whether changes the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  made in 2016 and 2021, which broadened access to the drug, would have to be rolled back.\par \par \uc2 Those changes made it possible for patients to obtain prescriptions for mifepristone by telemedicine and receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the mail, which has greatly increased the availability of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several justices questioned the remedy the plaintiffs seek: to apply nationwide restrictions to the drug in a case that would have very broad implications because it would be the first time a court had second-guessed the F.D.A.'s regulatory authority.\par \par \uc2 ''This case seems like a prime example of turning what could be a small lawsuit into a nationwide legislative assembly on an F.D.A. rule or any other federal government action,'' said Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , an appointee of President Donald J. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Here are some takeaways:\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs' claims of being entitled to sue were met with great skepticism.\par \par \uc2 To have standing, plaintiffs must show they face concrete harm from the policy or action they are challenging in court. In this case, the plaintiffs, a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and organizations, say they face moral harm because patients who take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills might seek treatment afterward at emergency departments in hospitals where some of those doctors work.\par \par \uc2 Solicitor General Elizabeth B. Prelogar, arguing for the government, said the plaintiffs did not ''come within 100 miles of the kinds of circumstances this court has previously identified'' as grounds for standing. She cited the fact that the doctors do not prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and are not forced to treat women who take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. More crucially, she pointed to the fact that because serious complications from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are very rare, these doctors would not often encounter a woman who had experienced a serious complication requiring them to provide treatment.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs' lawyer, Erin Hawley, countered by saying the doctors had treated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill patients in emergency departments. She cited the written declarations in the case of Dr. Christina Francis and Dr. Ingrid Skop.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  questioned whether those doctors had provided examples of ''actually participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end the life of the embryo or fetus.'' She added, ''I don't read either Skop or Francis to say that they ever participated in that.''\par \par \uc2 The justices also questioned whether the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations in the case have standing. Those organizations contend that they experience harm because in order to challenge the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, they have had to divert resources from other advocacy efforts.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  seemed skeptical of that claim, saying that having to prioritize how an organization spends its time and money would apply to ''anyone who is aggressive or vigilant about bringing lawsuits. Just simply by using resources to advocate their position in court, you say now, causes an injury. That seems easy to manufacture.''\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of discussion about conscience protections.\par \par \uc2 Federal conscience protections allow doctors and other health care providers to opt out of providing care that they object to on moral or religious grounds. In many hospitals, doctors register their conscience objections in advance so they are never called upon to participate in care they object to.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the government and for a manufacturer of mifepristone, Danco Laboratories, said that if the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors did encounter an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient, they could easily invoke conscience protections and pass the case to another doctor who did not have moral objections. The plaintiffs are ''individuals who do not use this product, do not prescribe this product and have a conscience right not to treat anyone who has taken this product,'' said Jessica Ellsworth, a lawyer representing Danco.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawley said there were sometimes occasions in emergency departments where the plaintiffs would not have time to opt out, forcing them to ''choose between helping a woman with a life-threatening condition and violating their conscience.''\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  said there was ''a mismatch'' between what the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors are claiming they have experienced and the remedy they are seeking. ''The obvious common-sense remedy would be to provide them with an exemption, that they don't have to participate in this procedure,'' Justice Jackson said.\par \par \uc2 Noting that such a remedy already exists in the form of conscience protections, she said: ''I guess, then, what they're asking for in this lawsuit is more than that. They're saying, 'Because we object to having to be forced to participate in this procedure, we're seeking an order preventing anyone from having access to these drugs at all. ''\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett asked about the plaintiffs' claim that the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, or EMTALA, which requires emergency departments in hospitals to treat patients with urgent medical issues, would override doctors' conscience objections and force them to treat patients who have taken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills anyway. Ms. Prelogar said that would not happen because EMTALA applies to hospitals, not individual doctors, so doctors with moral objections could opt out.\par \par \uc2 The case could affect the government's role in regulating drugs -- and possibly in regulating anything.\par \par \uc2 Many experts on regulatory policy and leaders in the pharmaceutical industry have said that if the court decides to undermine the scientific expertise of the F.D.A., it would deter companies from developing new medications and would ultimately hurt patients who would not have those medications available. They say it could also shake the regulatory authority of other government agencies.\par \par \uc2 Several justices asked about this issue. ''Do you have concerns about judges parsing medical and scientific studies?'' Justice Jackson asked Ms. Ellsworth, the lawyer for the manufacturer. Ms. Ellsworth said that was a concern, noting that two studies the plaintiffs had cited to show mifepristone was unsafe had been recently retracted.\par \par \uc2 ''That is why \uc2 FDA\uc2  has many hundreds of pages of analysis in the record of what the scientific data showed,'' Ms. Ellsworth said. ''And courts are just not in a position to parse through and second-guess that.\par \par \uc2 A 19th-century anti-vice law made an appearance.\par \par \uc2 The Comstock Act, enacted in 1873, bars the mailing of drugs that can be used to terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Justices Alito and Thomas asked whether the act, which has not been used in decades and has been narrowed by the courts and Congress, applied, as the plaintiffs claim.\par \par \uc2 ''The Comstock provisions don't fall within F.D.A.'s lane,'' said Ms. Prelogar, who said that the F.D.A.'s responsibility was to determine the safety and effectiveness of drugs and to regulate them. She also pointed out that the Justice Department issued an opinion that the Comstock Act applied only if the sender intended for the recipient of the materials to ''use them unlawfully.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ellsworth warned of what might ensue should the court decide the act applied. ''I think this court should think hard about the mischief it would invite if it allowed agencies to start taking action based on statutory responsibilities that Congress has assigned to other agencies,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar, the Biden administration's solicitor general, spoke for the government. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ROD LAMKEY/SIPA) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240327ek3r0003u\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertal